window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "1.1.1 Inputs, promises, and desired outputs", "chapter": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#topic-1-1-1", "text": "1.1.1 Inputs, promises, and desired outputs § Deutsch’s problem · input family · promise · success criterion Imagine a sealed device with one input switch and one output lamp. Set the switch to \\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) and the lamp shows a bit \\(f(x)\\). The wiring inside is fixed but hidden. Your job is not to reconstruct the device. Your job is to report one property of it: do the two switch positions give the same lamp? Write the required answer as \\[d=f(0)\\oplus f(1),\\] where \\(\\oplus\\) is addition modulo two. The answer \\(d=0\\) means the outputs agree, and \\(d=1\\) means they differ. There are exactly four possible devices, and we can list them all. Look at what this table asks for. Two of the four functions share each answer. The algorithm must separate the rows into two groups, not identify the row. That is less than the full truth table, and the whole chapter turns on whether we can learn less more cheaply . A classical procedure that is allowed to look at the lamp can certainly compare both outputs. Can it do so with one look? Suppose it sets the switch to \\(0\\) and sees \\(f(0)=0\\). Rows one and two are both still possible, and they have different answers. The same happens whichever input is tried first and whichever value appears. One inspection cannot determine \\(d\\) with certainty, because the unseen value changes the answer. Three pieces of the problem statement are now explicit, and every later chapter will ask for the same three. The input family is the set of instances the algorithm must handle: here, all four functions. A promise is a restriction on that family which the algorithm may assume without checking. Deutsch’s problem needs none, since every one-bit function either agrees or disagrees with itself across inputs. For an \\(n\\)-bit function the analogous promise “constant or balanced” is a genuine restriction, and an algorithm that relies on it may give meaningless output on functions that break it. The success criterion says what counts as a correct run. Here we demand the right \\(d\\) with certainty on every row. Later problems allow error probability, say at most \\(1/3\\) per instance. That allowance must be granted to classical and quantum methods alike before their costs are compared. Problems Without looking back, list the four one-bit Boolean functions and the required output \\(d\\) for each. Which pairs of functions must the algorithm treat identically? Suppose the problem is changed to “report \\(f(0)\\) and \\(f(1)\\).” Show that any algorithm, classical or quantum, that reads one classical bit from each run needs at least two runs, whatever happens inside a run. For \\(n\\)-bit inputs, a student proposes the “promise” that \\(f\\) is either constant or has exactly one input with \\(f(x)=1\\). Give an algorithm that may rely on this promise and output an answer on any \\(f\\). What does it output on a function with two ones, and is that a bug?"}, {"title": "1.1.2 Decision, search, estimation, and sampling", "chapter": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#topic-1-1-2", "text": "1.1.2 Decision, search, estimation, and sampling § decision problem · search problem · estimation problem · sampling problem Deutsch’s task asks for one bit. Other tasks ask for very different things, and the kind of output determines how much information the algorithm must extract and how success is measured. Here are four kinds, all phrased for a Boolean function \\(f\\) on \\(N\\) inputs. A decision problem asks for yes or no: is there any \\(x\\) with \\(f(x)=1\\)? A search problem asks for a witness: output some \\(x\\) with \\(f(x)=1\\), or report that none exists. An estimation problem asks for a number within a tolerance: output \\(\\tilde p\\) with \\(|\\tilde p-p|\\le\\epsilon\\), where \\(p\\) is the fraction of inputs with \\(f(x)=1\\), with failure probability at most \\(\\delta\\). A sampling problem asks for a random output with a specified distribution: output \\(x\\) with probability close to some target \\(q(x)\\). These demands are not interchangeable. A search answer can usually be checked with one more evaluation of \\(f\\); an estimate cannot be checked that way. Estimating \\(p\\) to within \\(0.01\\) does not require producing any particular marked input, and producing one marked input says almost nothing about how many there are. A sampling algorithm is judged by the distribution of its outputs over many runs, not by any single output. Quantum algorithms add a fifth kind of output: a quantum state handed to a later computation. A procedure that prepares a state whose amplitudes are proportional to the entries of a vector \\(v\\) has not output \\(v\\). Reading even one entry to accuracy \\(\\epsilon\\) generally needs on the order of \\(1/\\epsilon^2\\) repetitions of the whole procedure, and listing all \\(N\\) entries is at least as costly as \\(N\\). Chapter 8 returns to this point, because linear-system algorithms produce exactly such states. So the second item of every problem statement in this course is the output type: a bit, a string, an estimate with tolerance and confidence, a sample with a distance to the target distribution, or a state with a fidelity requirement. Without it, “faster” has no meaning. Problems Classify each task: (a) find a prime factor of \\(L\\); (b) decide whether \\(L\\) is prime; (c) estimate the fraction of 20-bit strings satisfying a formula to within \\(0.05\\); (d) output a uniformly random satisfying assignment. A procedure outputs a marked item with probability \\(1/4\\) per run and otherwise outputs an unmarked one. Marked items can be recognized with one evaluation of \\(f\\). How many runs, with checking, make the probability of never seeing a marked item at most \\(10^{-3}\\)? A colleague claims: “My circuit prepares \\(\\sum_x v_x\\ket x\\) for a vector \\(v\\) of length \\(N=2^{30}\\) using only \\(30\\) qubits and \\(10^4\\) gates, so it computes \\(v\\) exponentially faster than any classical program.” What is wrong with the comparison?"}, {"title": "1.2.1 Classical data, quantum states, and oracles", "chapter": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#topic-1-2-1", "text": "1.2.1 Classical data, quantum states, and oracles § oracle · query · coherent access For Deutsch’s problem we assume access to a reversible version of the device, the oracle \\[U_f\\ket{x}\\ket{y}=\\ket{x}\\ket{y\\oplus f(x)},\\qquad x,y\\in\\{0,1\\}.\\] The first register selects the input and is left unchanged. The second, the target, is flipped exactly when \\(f(x)=1\\). On the four basis states, \\(U_f\\) is a permutation, so it is unitary; applying it twice restores everything, so \\(U_f^{-1}=U_f\\). One application of \\(U_f\\) is one query , whatever state it is applied to. That last clause is the whole difference from the lamp. Because \\(U_f\\) is linear, it can act on a superposition of inputs. Prepare the input in \\(\\ket+=(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and the target in \\(\\ket0\\). One query gives \\[U_f\\,\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\ket0=\\frac{\\ket0\\ket{f(0)}+\\ket1\\ket{f(1)}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Predict first. Both function values now appear in the state. Measure both registers. What is the probability of learning \\(d\\) from this one run? Access with this property, where a single use acts linearly on a superposition of questions, is called coherent access . It is an assumption about the problem, not a consequence of quantum mechanics. A physical lamp on a lab bench offers no such operation. When \\(f\\) is given as a classical program, we can build \\(U_f\\) by the reversible techniques of Chapter 2, and its cost is then a gate count, not an abstract query. Problems Write out the \\(4\\times4\\) matrix of \\(U_f\\) for \\(f(x)=x\\) in the basis order \\(00,01,10,11\\), and identify it as a familiar gate. Apply \\(U_f\\) for \\(f(x)=1\\oplus x\\) to \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0+\\ket1)\\ket0\\) and give the probabilities of the four measurement outcomes. Someone defines a “quantum oracle” as \\(V_f\\ket x=\\ket{f(x)}\\) on one qubit. For which \\(f\\) is \\(V_f\\) a valid quantum operation, and why does the two-register form avoid the problem?"}, {"title": "1.2.2 Available operations and hidden preparation costs", "chapter": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#topic-1-2-2", "text": "1.2.2 Available operations and hidden preparation costs § inverse access · controlled access · data loading The oracle contract above says what \\(U_f\\) does. It does not, by itself, give us related operations that later algorithms will want. It is worth listing them now, because each will be a separate assumption. Inverse access. For \\(U_f\\) the inverse is \\(U_f\\) itself. For a general unitary \\(U\\) handed to us as a sealed box, being able to apply \\(U\\) does not mean we can apply \\(U^\\dagger\\). If we know a gate sequence for \\(U\\), we can reverse its order and invert each gate. Controlled access. Controlled-\\(U\\) applies \\(U\\) only on the branch where a control qubit is \\(1\\). For a sealed box this is again an additional capability. From a gate sequence we can control each gate, but we must also fix the box’s global phase, since that phase becomes a relative phase between control branches. Copies of states. One copy of an unknown state \\(\\ket\\psi\\) is not a recipe for preparing more; no procedure can copy an unknown state. Algorithms that need \\(\\ket\\psi\\) many times must be given a preparation circuit or many copies, and the count of copies is a resource. Classical data. A classical table of \\(N\\) numbers is not a quantum state. Turning it into amplitudes, or into an oracle that can be queried in superposition, requires a data-loading circuit whose cost can be comparable to \\(N\\). Assuming that loading is free can erase an apparent speedup. None of these is pedantry. Amplitude amplification in Chapter 5 uses a preparation and its inverse; phase estimation in Chapter 4 uses controlled powers of a unitary; linear-system solvers in Chapter 8 assume block access to a matrix. In each case the speedup is stated relative to that access, and the access must be implemented and paid for in a full accounting. A box labelled \\(U_f\\) in a circuit diagram can contain thousands of elementary gates and its own workspace. Problems A gate sequence \\(U=G_3G_2G_1\\) is known, where \\(G_1\\) acts first. Write a sequence for \\(U^\\dagger\\). Two boxes implement \\(U\\) and \\(e^{i\\pi/2}U\\). Show that they are indistinguishable when used uncontrolled, but that their controlled versions produce different states on \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0+\\ket1)\\ket\\psi\\). An algorithm needs the amplitude-encoded state \\(\\sum_{x}v_x\\ket x/\\|v\\|\\) for a classical vector \\(v\\) with \\(N\\) entries, and then uses \\(O(\\log N)\\) further gates. Explain why its total cost cannot be stated as \\(O(\\log N)\\) without further assumptions."}, {"title": "1.3.1 Encoding the property that matters", "chapter": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#topic-1-3-1", "text": "1.3.1 Encoding the property that matters § phase oracle · phase kickback We want \\(f(0)\\) and \\(f(1)\\) to contribute to the same amplitude with signs that depend on the values. Signs combine the way we need: \\((-1)^{f(0)}+(-1)^{f(1)}\\) is \\(\\pm2\\) when the values agree and \\(0\\) when they differ. So aim to put the function values into signs. The target state \\(\\ket-=(\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) does this. Flipping it only changes its sign, \\(X\\ket-=-\\ket-\\). So when the oracle flips the target, the state picks up a factor \\(-1\\), and when it does not, nothing changes: \\[U_f\\ket{x}\\ket-=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket{x}\\ket-.\\] The target ends in the same state \\(\\ket-\\) for both inputs, and the function value has moved into a sign on the input. This is phase kickback ; the resulting map \\(\\ket x\\mapsto(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\) is called a phase oracle . Now query with the input in \\(\\ket+\\): \\[U_f\\ket+\\ket-=\\frac{(-1)^{f(0)}\\ket0+(-1)^{f(1)}\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket-.\\] The two signs are relative phases between the input branches, which a Hadamard can turn into probabilities. Using \\(H\\ket0=(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(H\\ket1=(\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), the input becomes \\[\\frac{(-1)^{f(0)}+(-1)^{f(1)}}{2}\\ket0+\\frac{(-1)^{f(0)}-(-1)^{f(1)}}{2}\\ket1.\\] If the values agree, the amplitude of \\(\\ket1\\) is zero and the amplitude of \\(\\ket0\\) has magnitude one. If they differ, the reverse. Measuring the input therefore returns \\(d\\) with certainty. Starting from \\(\\ket0\\ket1\\), two Hadamards prepare \\(\\ket+\\ket-\\). Register order is input first, then target. The target is never measured. Notice what the design required: we first wrote down the quantity we wanted, \\((-1)^{f(0)}\\pm(-1)^{f(1)}\\), and then chose the encoding and the final gate to produce it as an amplitude. That direction of reasoning, from desired amplitude back to circuit, is the habit this course tries to build. Problems Track the full two-qubit state through the circuit for \\(f(x)=x\\), at the three marked slices. Repeat for \\(f\\equiv1\\). What is the final state, and why does its overall sign not matter? Replace the final Hadamard on the input by the gate \\(S=\\mathrm{diag}(1,i)\\) followed by a Hadamard. Compute the probability of outcome \\(1\\) for a balanced and a constant function. What design principle does the failure illustrate?"}, {"title": "1.3.2 Removing records that prevent cancellation", "chapter": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#topic-1-3-2", "text": "1.3.2 Removing records that prevent cancellation § which-path record Why not keep the simpler target \\(\\ket0\\)? Let us see exactly what goes wrong, because the same failure will reappear inside every larger algorithm. Predict first. Use target \\(\\ket0\\) instead of \\(\\ket-\\), keep the input preparation and final Hadamard, and take \\(f(x)=x\\). What is the probability of measuring \\(d=1\\) on the input? The query gives \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). Applying \\(H\\) to the first qubit, \\[\\frac{1}{2}\\big(\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{01}-\\ket{11}\\big).\\] There is a minus sign, as in the successful circuit, but it sits on \\(\\ket{11}\\), while the plus sign it would need to cancel sits on \\(\\ket{10}\\). Those are different basis states of the pair. Amplitudes add only when they belong to the same complete configuration, so nothing cancels. Each input outcome has probability \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\). The target has become a which-path record : its value tells which input branch produced each term. Whenever two branches carry orthogonal records, they cannot interfere, and it makes no difference whether anyone looks at the record. Ignoring it does not erase it. The \\(\\ket-\\) target avoided creating a distinguishing record in the first place: it ends in the same state on both branches, so the branches differ only by a sign on the input, and the sign can be made to cancel. In larger algorithms some temporary record is unavoidable. Computing \\(f\\) from a formula usually leaves intermediate values in scratch qubits, and those values depend on \\(x\\). Chapter 2 shows how to remove them by running the computation backwards after its answer has been used. For now, the design rule is simple to state: everything that distinguishes branches meant to interfere must be gone before they meet. Problems In the failed circuit with target \\(\\ket0\\), compute the outcome probabilities on the input for the constant function \\(f\\equiv0\\). After the query with target \\(\\ket0\\), apply a second CNOT from the input to the target (a copy of the oracle for \\(f(x)=x\\)) before the final Hadamard. What does the input measurement give now for \\(f(x)=x\\)? A student argues: “The target is never measured in the failing circuit, so it cannot affect the input’s statistics.” Give a one-line counterexample from this subsection and state the correct principle."}, {"title": "1.4.1 Distinguishable answers and measurement", "chapter": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#topic-1-4-1", "text": "1.4.1 Distinguishable answers and measurement § orthogonal states · measurement basis Look at the input register just before the final Hadamard. Constant functions leave it in \\(\\pm\\ket+\\) and balanced functions in \\(\\pm\\ket-\\). Measured directly in the computational basis, both give a fair coin. The answer is fully present, but this measurement is blind to it. What makes the answer readable at all is that \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) are orthogonal : \\(\\braket{+}{-}=\\frac12(1\\cdot1+1\\cdot(-1))=0\\). Orthogonal states can be distinguished with certainty by measuring in a basis that contains them. The Hadamard is the change of basis that does this, since \\(H\\ket+=\\ket0\\) and \\(H\\ket-=\\ket1\\). Applying \\(H\\) and then measuring in the computational basis is the same as using \\(\\{\\ket+,\\ket-\\}\\) as the measurement basis. The complete output state is \\[(-1)^{f(0)}\\ket{d}\\ket-,\\qquad d=f(0)\\oplus f(1).\\] The factor \\((-1)^{f(0)}\\) is a global phase and changes no probability. So the circuit separates the two classes of function but cannot tell the two members of each class apart. That loss is not a defect: we asked only for \\(d\\). It is also a warning. If the states encoding different answers had a nonzero overlap \\(|\\braket{a}{b}|\\), no measurement could separate them perfectly; the best achievable error probability for two equally likely states is \\(\\frac12\\big(1-\\sqrt{1-|\\braket ab|^2}\\big)\\). Designing an algorithm includes making the answer states orthogonal, or nearly so. Problems Verify that \\(H\\) maps \\(\\ket+\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) to \\(\\ket1\\) by multiplying amplitude lists. Suppose a faulty preparation makes the two answer states \\(\\ket{a}=\\cos(\\pi/8)\\ket0+\\sin(\\pi/8)\\ket1\\) and \\(\\ket b=\\cos(\\pi/8)\\ket0-\\sin(\\pi/8)\\ket1\\), equally likely. Compute their overlap and the best possible error probability. Is it possible to modify the Deutsch circuit so that one run reports \\(d\\) and \\(f(0)\\)? Use the output state to argue."}, {"title": "1.4.2 Success probability, precision, and repetition", "chapter": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#topic-1-4-2", "text": "1.4.2 Success probability, precision, and repetition § majority vote · repetition · Chebyshev’s inequality Deutsch’s circuit is exact. Most algorithms are not: they output a correct answer with some probability less than one, or an estimate with some error. The readout procedure must then say how runs are combined and what the combination guarantees. Boosting a decision. Suppose a decision algorithm is correct with probability at least \\(3/4\\) on every input. Run it three times independently and take a majority vote . The majority is right if at least two runs are right: \\[\\Pr[\\text{majority correct}]\\ \\ge\\ \\left(\\tfrac34\\right)^3+3\\left(\\tfrac34\\right)^2\\tfrac14=\\tfrac{27}{64}+\\tfrac{27}{64}=\\tfrac{27}{32}\\approx0.84.\\] With more runs the failure probability falls exponentially in the number of repetitions, as long as the single-run success is bounded above one half. The figure shows the effect. Searching with a checker. If a run finds a valid answer with probability \\(p\\) and validity can be checked, then \\(R\\) independent runs all fail with probability \\((1-p)^R\\le e^{-pR}\\). About \\(\\ln(1/\\delta)/p\\) runs bring the failure below \\(\\delta\\). The checker is essential; without it we would not know which run to believe. Estimating a probability. Suppose a measured bit is \\(1\\) with unknown probability \\(p\\), and we average \\(R\\) independent results. The average has mean \\(p\\) and variance \\(p(1-p)/R\\le1/(4R)\\); variance is the expected squared deviation from the mean. Whenever the average misses \\(p\\) by more than \\(\\epsilon\\), its squared deviation exceeds \\(\\epsilon^2\\). Since the mean squared deviation is at most \\(1/(4R)\\), the probability of such a miss is at most \\[\\Pr\\big[|\\bar p-p|>\\epsilon\\big]\\le\\frac{1}{4R\\epsilon^2}.\\] This is Chebyshev’s inequality . It shows that precision \\(\\epsilon\\) costs on the order of \\(1/\\epsilon^2\\) repetitions. Halving the error quadruples the work. Chapter 5 will show how coherent methods can reduce this to order \\(1/\\epsilon\\), at the price of longer circuits and stronger access assumptions. Repetition counts belong in every resource statement. One more requirement hides in the word “independent.” Repetitions must use fresh preparations. Re-measuring the same post-measurement state returns the same outcome and adds no information. Problems A decision algorithm is correct with probability \\(2/3\\). What is the success probability of a majority vote over three runs? Using the Chebyshev bound, how many runs guarantee \\(|\\bar p-p|\\le0.01\\) with failure probability at most \\(0.05\\)? Compare with \\(\\epsilon=0.001\\). A decision algorithm is correct with probability exactly \\(1/2\\) on some inputs. Explain why majority voting cannot help on those inputs, and why “correct with probability at least \\(1/2+c\\)” for a fixed \\(c>0\\) is the standard requirement."}, {"title": "1.5.1 Correctness for a family of inputs", "chapter": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#topic-1-5-1", "text": "1.5.1 Correctness for a family of inputs § balanced function · Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm Substituting the four rows of the table into the output formula gives the final states \\(\\ket0\\ket-\\), \\(\\ket1\\ket-\\), \\(-\\ket1\\ket-\\), and \\(-\\ket0\\ket-\\). That checks every allowed input. It is also more than a check: the formula \\(\\frac12\\big((-1)^{f(0)}\\pm(-1)^{f(1)}\\big)\\) explains why the answer comes out, which is what lets us generalize. And there is nothing to scale yet. Deutsch’s problem has one size. A query count of one versus two is a constant-factor statement about a fixed problem. It cannot be extrapolated to say anything about how costs grow. To claim that one method scales better than another we need a family of problems indexed by a size \\(n\\), a proof of correctness for every allowed input of every size, and expressions for how each resource grows with \\(n\\) and with the requested accuracy. Here is the first such family, which Chapter 3 develops fully. Let \\(f:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\) be promised to be either constant or balanced (a balanced function takes the value \\(1\\) on exactly half its inputs). Run the same design on \\(n\\) input qubits: Hadamards on all of them, one phase query, Hadamards again. The amplitude of the all-zero outcome is \\[\\frac{1}{2^n}\\sum_{x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n}(-1)^{f(x)},\\] which is \\(\\pm1\\) for a constant function and \\(0\\) for a balanced one. One query decides the promise problem exactly for every \\(n\\). That is a statement about a family, and its proof is a single line valid for all \\(n\\). It is the Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm . Generalizing correctly also means keeping the assumptions in view. For \\(n=1\\) the promise was automatic; for \\(n\\ge2\\) it is not. A function with three ones among eight inputs gives zero-outcome amplitude \\((5-3)/8=1/4\\), so the circuit outputs “constant” with probability \\(1/16\\) and something else otherwise. On such inputs the output means nothing, and the problem statement must say so. Problems For \\(n=2\\) and the balanced function \\(f(x_1x_2)=x_1\\), compute the zero-outcome amplitude directly from the sum. For a constant function on \\(n\\) bits, show that the final state is \\(\\pm\\ket{0^n}\\ket-\\), using \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\ket{0^n}=2^{-n/2}\\sum_x\\ket x\\) and the fact that \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\) is its own inverse. A paper reports that its quantum circuit solved a 3-qubit instance of a problem using 5 gates where a classical program used 12 operations, and concludes that the method is “exponentially faster.” List what is missing from this argument."}, {"title": "1.5.2 Classical baselines and complete resource costs", "chapter": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#topic-1-5-2", "text": "1.5.2 Classical baselines and complete resource costs § query complexity · classical baseline · resource accounting A speedup is a comparison against a classical baseline : the best known classical method for the same task, under the same access and success criterion . Both sides must play the same game. For Deutsch’s problem the game is query complexity : count only uses of the function, under exact success. Classically two queries are necessary; quantumly one suffices. Could a randomized classical algorithm do better with one query if it is allowed to err? No better than guessing. Pick the four functions uniformly at random and fix the algorithm’s internal coin flips. Whichever input it queries, the other value is a fresh fair bit, independent of what it saw. So \\(d\\) is a fair bit given everything the algorithm knows, and any output rule is right with probability exactly \\(1/2\\) on average. Averaging over the algorithm’s coins preserves this, so on at least one of the four functions the success probability is at most \\(1/2\\). Random guessing already achieves \\(1/2\\) on every input. The quantum circuit gives certainty. That is the query comparison. Complete resource accounting lists more. For the circuit as drawn, with ideal oracle access: The table separates resources that are often blurred. A query count is not a runtime. A runtime comparison would include building the oracle on both sides, the gates, the measurement, and any classical processing, all under the same allowed error. For Deutsch’s problem in particular, if \\(f\\) is given as a small classical circuit, evaluating it twice classically is cheap and the quantum version saves essentially nothing. The value of the example lies elsewhere: it isolates the mechanism of phase kickback plus interference, which becomes powerful when the same mechanism acts on exponentially many inputs at once. Problems In the Deutsch–Jozsa setting with \\(n\\)-bit inputs and exact success, show that a deterministic classical algorithm may need \\(2^{n-1}+1\\) queries. Now allow error. A randomized classical algorithm queries \\(k\\) uniformly random inputs and reports “constant” if all answers agree. Bound its error probability on a balanced function and compare its cost to the exact case. A colleague implements the Deutsch–Jozsa oracle for a specific \\(f\\) given as a classical circuit with \\(g\\) gates, and reports “one query versus \\(2^{n-1}+1\\).” What should the end-to-end gate count include, and does the exponential gap survive against a bounded-error classical method?"}, {"title": "1.6.1 Deutsch’s algorithm as a design case study", "chapter": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#topic-1-6-1", "text": "1.6.1 Deutsch’s algorithm as a design case study § design process Here is the design process, decision by decision. Each row is a question you can ask of any proposed quantum algorithm. The decisive step was the encoding. We needed the parity of two values, so we encoded those values as signs and made the signed amplitudes meet in one output amplitude. The \\(\\ket-\\) target made the oracle change a relative phase without leaving a record, and the final Hadamard converted that phase relation into a classical bit. Everything else was bookkeeping, but bookkeeping that would have hidden a mistake if skipped. The one-query form of the algorithm presented here, with the target in \\(\\ket-\\), is due to Cleve, Ekert, Macchiavello, and Mosca, Quantum Algorithms Revisited (1998) , refining Deutsch’s original 1985 procedure, which succeeded only half the time. The family generalization is Deutsch and Jozsa, Rapid solution of problems by quantum computation (1992) . Problems Replace the oracle target \\(\\ket-\\) by \\(\\ket+\\), keeping everything else. Predict the output for every function. Apply the checklist to this task: given coherent access \\(U_f\\) to \\(f:\\{0,1\\}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\), output \\(f(0)\\). What does the checklist suggest, and how many queries are needed? The checklist says to “prove correctness for the family.” For Deutsch’s problem we checked four rows. Why is the formula-based argument still preferable, even though exhaustive checking is complete here?"}, {"title": "1.6.2 From reusable constructions to new algorithms", "chapter": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#topic-1-6-2", "text": "1.6.2 From reusable constructions to new algorithms § technique · composition For a new problem, the same questions apply in the same order. What must be learned, and what does the input representation allow? Which encoding makes amplitudes or phases depend on that property? Which branches must interfere, and what records would stop them? What measurement would reveal the answer, and how often must it be repeated? How do all costs grow with size and accuracy, and what is the best classical method for the same task? The rest of the course supplies tools for answering these questions, organized by technique rather than by famous algorithm. Named algorithms appear as applications or combinations. The techniques overlap and build on one another. Phase estimation is controlled powers plus phase kickback plus an inverse Fourier transform. Amplitude estimation is amplification plus phase estimation. Qubitization is a walk whose reflections come from a block encoding . When a chapter uses an earlier technique, it says so and reuses the earlier analysis instead of repeating it. Our next obstacle is already visible. Realistic function evaluation needs scratch space, and scratch space that remembers the input is exactly the kind of which-path record that destroyed interference in Section 1.3.2. Chapter 2 builds function evaluations that leave precisely the intended output and restore their temporary storage. Problems From the table, which techniques does standard phase estimation combine? Which does amplitude estimation add? Sketch, at the level of the checklist, a design for this task: given coherent access to \\(f:\\{0,1\\}^2\\to\\{0,1\\}\\), decide the parity \\(f(00)\\oplus f(01)\\oplus f(10)\\oplus f(11)\\). (You need not find the optimal query count.) Explain in two sentences why “quantum parallelism” alone—evaluating \\(f\\) on a superposition of all inputs—is not a technique in the sense of this course."}, {"title": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#exercise-1", "text": "A one-bit oracle is used with target |0⟩ and input |+⟩, followed by a Hadamard on the input. For which of the four functions does the input measurement give the correct d with certainty? For the two constant functions the target is the same on both branches, the input returns to |0⟩, and the output 0 is correct with certainty. For the two balanced functions the target records the input, and the outcome is a fair coin. So only the constant functions are handled correctly."}, {"title": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#exercise-2", "text": "Run the Deutsch circuit (input |0⟩, target |1⟩, Hadamards on both, one query, Hadamard on the input) for f(x)=1⊕ x. Write the two-qubit state after the query and after the final Hadamard. Which outcome is measured, and what does the overall sign mean? After the Hadamards the state is |+⟩|−⟩. The query multiplies the |0⟩ branch by (−1)¹=−1 and leaves the |1⟩ branch alone, giving (−|0⟩+|1⟩)/√2|−⟩=−|−⟩|−⟩. The final Hadamard maps |−⟩ to |1⟩, so the state is −|1⟩|−⟩ and the input reads 1=d with certainty, as it should for a balanced function. The factor −1=(−1)^(f(0)) multiplies the whole state: it is a global phase and changes no probability, which is why the circuit cannot tell f(x)=1⊕ x from f(x)=x."}, {"title": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#exercise-3", "text": "You are handed a sealed box that applies U_f|x⟩|y⟩=|x⟩|y⊕ f(x)⟩ and nothing else. For each operation, say whether it comes free with the box, can be built from it, or is a separate assumption: (a) applying U_f to a superposition of inputs; (b) U_f⁻¹; (c) controlled-U_f; (d) a second copy of the state U_f|+⟩|0⟩ produced by an earlier query. (a) Free: coherent access is exactly the assumption that one use acts linearly on superpositions. (b) Free for this oracle, because U_f²=I, so U_f⁻¹=U_f; for a general sealed unitary the inverse would be a separate assumption. (c) Buildable at a price: compute f(x) into a fresh qubit |0⟩ with one query, apply a Toffoli gate controlled on the control qubit and that fresh qubit with the real target as its target, then query again to return the fresh qubit to |0⟩. The cost is two queries and one extra qubit; it works because this oracle has no troublesome global phase. (d) Not available: no procedure copies an unknown state, so a second copy needs a second preparation and a second query."}, {"title": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#exercise-4", "text": "For the Deutsch–Jozsa circuit with n = 3, a function has f(x) = 1 on exactly two of the eight inputs. Compute the probability that the circuit outputs the all-zero string. What should an algorithm relying on the promise do with this input? The amplitude is (6 − 2)/8 = 1/2, so the all-zero outcome has probability 1/4. The function is neither constant nor balanced, so it lies outside the promise; the specification requires nothing on it, and the circuit's output carries no guaranteed meaning."}, {"title": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#exercise-5", "text": "For the two-bit Deutsch–Jozsa circuit, take the balanced function f(x₁x₂)=x₁⊕ x₂. Compute the amplitude of every outcome z=z₁z₂, using the fact that the final amplitude is 1/4Σ_x(−1)^(f(x)+x· z), where x· z=x₁z₁⊕ x₂z₂. Repeat for f(x)=1⊕ x₁. What pattern do you see? For f(x)=x₁⊕ x₂=x· 11, the exponent is x·(11⊕ z). When z=11 every term is +1 and the amplitude is 1; for any other z the string 11⊕ z is nonzero, exactly half the four inputs give −1, and the amplitude is 0. The circuit outputs 11 with certainty. For f(x)=1⊕ x₁, the exponent is 1+x·(10⊕ z), so the outcome 10 has amplitude −1 and all others 0. Both functions are balanced and both give a nonzero string, as the promise problem requires. But more is true: whenever f(x)=a· x (possibly complemented), the circuit returns a itself. That observation is the Bernstein–Vazirani algorithm developed in Chapter 3."}, {"title": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#exercise-6", "text": "A decision algorithm is correct with probability 3/4 per run. Compute the exact success probability of a majority vote over five independent runs and compare it with the three-run value 27/32. Then a search procedure finds a checkable witness with probability 0.1 per run: how many runs bring the failure probability below 10⁻⁶? With q=3/4, Pr[≥3 correct]=binom53q³(1−q)²+binom54q⁴(1−q)+q⁵=(270+405+243)/1024=918/1024=459/512≈0.896. Three runs gave 27/32≈0.844; two more runs cut the failure probability from about 0.156 to about 0.104, and further runs keep shrinking it exponentially. For the search, e^(−0.1R)≤10⁻⁶ requires R≥10ln 10⁶≈138.2, so 139 runs suffice (the exact bound (0.9)^R≤10⁻⁶ gives 132). Each run is checked with one extra evaluation of f, which belongs in the total cost."}, {"title": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#exercise-7", "text": "Each run of an estimation circuit uses one query and 40 other gates and outputs a bit that is 1 with unknown probability p. Using the Chebyshev bound, how many runs guarantee |bar p−p|≤0.02 with failure probability at most 0.1? Give the total query and gate counts, and say what happens to both if the tolerance is halved. We need R≥1/(4deltaε²)=1/(4×0.1×0.0004)=6250 runs. Each run is a fresh preparation, so the cost is 6250 queries and 6250×40=250 000 further gates, plus classical averaging. Halving the tolerance to 0.01 multiplies R by four: 25 000 queries and one million gates. The resource statement must therefore say which tolerance and confidence were assumed; quoting the cost of one circuit hides a factor that grows as 1/ε². (Chebyshev is loose: sharper bounds lower the constant, but not the 1/ε² dependence.)"}, {"title": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#exercise-8", "text": "After one query with target |−⟩, a student discards the target qubit, reasoning that it may still hold information about f. Another student wants to reuse it for a second query on a fresh input register. Who is right? Compare with the target |0⟩. The second student. Since U_f(Σ_x a_x|x⟩)|−⟩=(Σ_x(−1)^(f(x))a_x|x⟩)⊗|−⟩, the target leaves the query in exactly the state it entered, unentangled with the input. It holds no record of f or of the input branch, so it can be reused for any number of later queries; throwing it away is harmless but wasteful. With target |0⟩ the state after the query is Σ_xa_x|x⟩|f(x)⟩, which is generally entangled: the target is a which-path record. Reusing it would carry that record into the next computation, and discarding it does not undo the damage, because the branches it distinguishes can no longer interfere."}, {"title": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#exercise-9", "text": "A bounded-error quantum algorithm for a decision problem succeeds with probability 0.6 per run. How many runs make the majority vote fail with probability at most 0.01, using the bound that the majority of R runs fails with probability at most e^(−2Rc²), where c = 0.1 is the advantage over 1/2? What does this imply about the definition of bounded error? We need 0.02R ≥ ln 100 ≈ 4.61, so R = 231 runs suffice by this bound. The count depends only on the advantage and the target confidence, not on the input size. That is why any fixed advantage above 1/2 (often stated as success 2/3) defines the same class of efficiently solvable problems: amplifying to any constant confidence costs only a constant factor."}, {"title": "Quantum Algorithmic Thinking — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-quantum-algorithmic-thinking.html#exercise-10", "text": "Deutsch’s original approach used the target |0⟩. After the query on |+⟩|0⟩, apply a Hadamard to both qubits and measure both. Compute the probability of each outcome pair (a,b). Show that when b=1 the input outcome a equals d with certainty, while b=0 carries no information. What is the overall success probability, and why is this weaker than the one-query circuit with target |−⟩? Starting from 1/√2Σ_x|x⟩|f(x)⟩, the Hadamards give amplitude 1/2√2[(−1)^(f(0)b)+(−1)^(a+f(1)b)] for (a,b). If b=0 this is 1/2√2(1+(−1)^a): outcome a=0 with probability 1/2 whatever f is, so nothing is learned. If b=1 it is nonzero only when a=f(0)⊕ f(1)=d, with probability 1/2. So half the time the procedure reports d with certainty and flags that it has done so; the other half it must report “don’t know” and query again. The expected number of queries is 2, no better than classical. The |−⟩ target removes the useless b=0 branch by preparing the target in the state that the b=1 measurement selected, which is why it succeeds every time."}, {"title": "2.1.1 Input and output contracts", "chapter": "Coherent Computation", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#topic-2-1-1", "text": "2.1.1 Input and output contracts § clean oracle · ancilla · workspace Suppose a search procedure must recognize three-bit strings satisfying \\(f(a,b,c)=(a\\land b)\\oplus c\\). In an ordinary program we would compute \\(g=a\\land b\\), combine it with \\(c\\), and throw \\(g\\) away. In a quantum algorithm, throwing away is not an operation we have. Worse, Section 1.3.2 showed that a leftover bit which depends on the input can silently destroy interference. So before building anything, we state precisely what the finished subroutine is allowed to leave behind. For an \\(n\\)-bit input \\(x\\) and an \\(m\\)-bit value \\(f(x)\\), the clean oracle contract is \\[\\ket{x}\\ket{y}\\ket{0^w}\\longmapsto\\ket{x}\\ket{y\\oplus f(x)}\\ket{0^w}\\quad\\text{for every }x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n,\\ y\\in\\{0,1\\}^m.\\] The three registers have distinct roles. The input \\(x\\) is preserved. The output target \\(y\\) may start in any state, and \\(f(x)\\) is added into it bit by bit with XOR. The workspace , \\(w\\) ancilla qubits used as scratch, must start in \\(\\ket{0^w}\\) and must be returned to \\(\\ket{0^w}\\). The equation must hold exactly, including phase, on every basis state. Once it does, linearity fixes the action on every superposition: \\[\\sum_{x,y}\\alpha_{x,y}\\ket{x}\\ket{y}\\ket{0^w}\\longmapsto\\sum_{x,y}\\alpha_{x,y}\\ket{x}\\ket{y\\oplus f(x)}\\ket{0^w}.\\] Why keep \\(x\\)? Because the naive map \\(\\ket{x}\\mapsto\\ket{f(x)}\\) is not reversible whenever two inputs share a value: it would send two orthogonal states to the same state. Preserving \\(x\\) and adding into a separate target makes the map a permutation of basis states for every \\(f\\). Applying it twice restores \\(y\\), since \\(y\\oplus f(x)\\oplus f(x)=y\\). Why insist on returning the workspace? Because the workspace is outside the contract’s promise to the caller. A caller who uses the oracle inside an interference experiment, as Deutsch’s algorithm did, needs the branches for different \\(x\\) to differ only in what the contract says. Any extra input-dependent record in the workspace would be a which-path record . Problems Write the clean contract for \\(f(a,b)=a\\land b\\) with one output qubit and no workspace, and check that applying the map twice is the identity. An implementation of a predicate maps \\(\\ket{x}\\ket{0}\\ket{0}\\mapsto\\ket{x}\\ket{f(x)}\\ket{g(x)}\\), where \\(g\\) is some intermediate value. For which functions \\(g\\) does it satisfy the clean contract with one ancilla? The contract demands that the map hold on all basis states with the stated phase. Give a map that sends every basis state \\(\\ket{x}\\ket{y}\\) to \\(\\ket{x}\\ket{y\\oplus f(x)}\\) up to a phase \\(e^{i\\theta(x)}\\) depending on \\(x\\), and explain why it does not satisfy the contract."}, {"title": "2.1.2 From Boolean circuits to reversible circuits", "chapter": "Coherent Computation", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#topic-2-1-2", "text": "2.1.2 From Boolean circuits to reversible circuits § Toffoli gate · garbage How do we build a clean oracle from an ordinary description of \\(f\\)? Any Boolean function has a circuit of AND, OR, and NOT gates. NOT is already reversible. AND is not: three of its four inputs give output \\(0\\). The Toffoli gate fixes this by keeping both inputs and adding the AND into a target, \\[\\mathrm{TOF}\\ket{a}\\ket{b}\\ket{t}=\\ket{a}\\ket{b}\\ket{t\\oplus ab}.\\] With \\(t=0\\), the target becomes \\(ab\\). OR follows from De Morgan’s law, \\(a\\lor b=\\lnot(\\lnot a\\land\\lnot b)\\), so it costs a Toffoli and some \\(X\\) gates. XOR into an existing bit is a CNOT. So translate the circuit gate by gate: every AND or OR gets a fresh ancilla initialized to \\(0\\) as its target. The translation works, but it leaves a trail. Every intermediate gate value now sits in its own ancilla. For our predicate \\(f=(a\\land b)\\oplus c\\), a direct translation computes \\(g=ab\\) into one ancilla, then \\(t=g\\oplus c\\) into another: The final values \\((a,b,c,g,t)=(a,b,c,ab,f(a,b,c))\\) contain the answer, but also the intermediate value \\(ab\\). That extra, input-dependent data is called garbage . In a classical computer garbage is merely wasteful; it can be overwritten later. In a quantum computer garbage is a which-path record that prevents the branches it distinguishes from interfering. The next section removes it. A word on size. If the original circuit has \\(G\\) AND/OR gates, the direct translation uses \\(G\\) Toffolis and up to \\(G\\) ancillas, plus \\(X\\) and CNOT gates. The number of qubits can therefore grow with the running time of the classical computation, not just with its memory. Section 2.3 returns to this. Problems Construct \\(a\\lor b\\) into a fresh ancilla using one Toffoli and \\(X\\) gates, restoring \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) afterwards. Translate \\(f(a,b,c)=(a\\land b)\\lor c\\) directly into reversible gates. How many ancillas and Toffolis does the direct translation use, and what garbage remains? Why can we not simply apply a “reset to \\(0\\)” operation to the garbage ancilla at the end of the circuit?"}, {"title": "2.1.3 Arithmetic on superpositions", "chapter": "Coherent Computation", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#topic-2-1-3", "text": "2.1.3 Arithmetic on superpositions § reversible arithmetic · modular addition A reversible circuit applies the same gate sequence to every basis component, so a classical reversible computation becomes a quantum one by linearity. Reversible arithmetic is the most important case: phase estimation , period finding, and amplitude estimation all need it. Start with incrementing a two-bit register \\(\\ket{b_1b_0}\\), where \\(b_1\\) is the high bit. Apply a CNOT from \\(b_0\\) to \\(b_1\\), then an \\(X\\) on \\(b_0\\). The CNOT flips the high bit exactly when the low bit is \\(1\\), which is when adding one produces a carry; the \\(X\\) then flips the low bit. Check all four inputs: \\[00\\mapsto01,\\qquad01\\mapsto10,\\qquad10\\mapsto11,\\qquad11\\mapsto00.\\] This is modular addition : adding one modulo four. The word modulo specifies what happens on overflow: \\(3+1\\) wraps to \\(0\\) rather than needing a third bit. That wrap-around is what makes the map a permutation, and hence reversible. On a superposition, nothing new is needed: \\((\\ket{00}+i\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\mapsto(\\ket{01}+i\\ket{00})/\\sqrt2\\). No measurement, no classical branching. Reversing the gate order gives the inverse, subtraction of one. The \\(n\\)-bit incrementer follows the same idea. Process target bits from highest to lowest: flip bit \\(j\\) if all lower bits are \\(1\\), then finally flip bit \\(0\\). Descending order matters, because each controlled flip reads the lower bits before they change. Bit \\(j\\) needs a \\(j\\)-controlled \\(X\\), which is not an elementary gate; with one or more ancillas it decomposes into \\(O(j)\\) Toffolis, so the whole incrementer costs \\(O(n^2)\\) Toffolis this way, and more careful constructions reach \\(O(n)\\). More generally, reversible adders computing \\(\\ket{a}\\ket{b}\\mapsto\\ket{a}\\ket{a+b\\bmod2^n}\\) are known with \\(O(n)\\) Toffoli and CNOT gates and one ancilla, for example the ripple-carry adder of Cuccaro, Draper, Kutin, and Moulton (2004) . Modular multiplication and exponentiation, built from repeated addition, are the dominant cost of Shor’s algorithm in Chapter 4. Problems Apply the two-bit incrementer to \\(\\frac12(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{11})\\). What changes? Reverse the two gates of the incrementer (first \\(X\\) on \\(b_0\\), then CNOT from \\(b_0\\) to \\(b_1\\)). What map results? Write a gate sequence for a three-bit incrementer using Toffoli, CNOT, and \\(X\\), and verify it on input \\(011\\)."}, {"title": "2.2.1 Compute, copy, uncompute", "chapter": "Coherent Computation", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#topic-2-2-1", "text": "2.2.1 Compute, copy, uncompute § uncomputation · compute–copy–uncompute Let \\(C\\) be the garbage-producing circuit of Section 2.1.2: Toffoli from \\(a,b\\) into \\(g\\), CNOT from \\(g\\) into \\(t\\), CNOT from \\(c\\) into \\(t\\). Afterwards \\(g=ab\\) and \\(t=f(a,b,c)\\). Now do three things. Compute: apply \\(C\\). Copy: CNOT from \\(t\\) into the external target \\(y\\). Uncompute: apply \\(C^{-1}\\), the gates of \\(C\\) in reverse order (each is its own inverse). For a basis input \\(x=(a,b,c)\\), with register order \\((x,g,t,y)\\), \\[\\ket{x,0,0,y}\\xrightarrow{C}\\ket{x,ab,f(x),y}\\xrightarrow{\\text{copy}}\\ket{x,ab,f(x),y\\oplus f(x)}\\xrightarrow{C^{-1}}\\ket{x,0,0,y\\oplus f(x)}.\\] Why does the last step clear the workspace without disturbing \\(y\\)? Because \\(C^{-1}\\) does not touch \\(y\\), and the registers it does touch, \\((x,g,t)\\), are exactly as \\(C\\) left them: the copy only read \\(t\\). Undoing \\(C\\) on its own output returns its own input, \\((x,0,0)\\). The input \\(x\\) is what makes this possible, since it still contains everything needed to recompute and hence un-recompute the intermediate values. Predict first. Run the circuit on \\((\\ket{000}+\\ket{110})/\\sqrt2\\) with \\(g=t=y=0\\). What is the state after the copy, and after uncomputation? This compute–copy–uncompute pattern is due to Bennett, Logical Reversibility of Computation (1973) , who used it to show that any classical computation can be made reversible without accumulating garbage. Nothing in the argument depends on the input being a basis state, so by linearity it gives a clean quantum oracle. Notice what is not cleared: the output target still records \\(f(x)\\). Uncomputation removes everything the contract did not ask for and nothing it did. Problems Trace the six registers through the circuit for input \\(x=111\\), \\(y=0\\). Count the gates in compute–copy–uncompute for this predicate and compare with the direct construction (Toffoli into \\(y\\), then CNOT from \\(c\\) into \\(y\\)). Does the copying CNOT violate the no-cloning theorem? Consider its action on \\(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) in the \\(t\\) register."}, {"title": "2.2.2 Phase oracles from clean evaluation", "chapter": "Coherent Computation", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#topic-2-2-2", "text": "2.2.2 Phase oracles from clean evaluation § Chapter 1 used the phase oracle \\(\\ket{x}\\mapsto(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket{x}\\). With a clean evaluation it comes for free: initialize the external target in \\(\\ket-\\). The copy step is then a CNOT into \\(\\ket-\\), which multiplies by \\((-1)^{t}\\) and leaves the target in \\(\\ket-\\). After uncomputation, \\[\\sum_x\\alpha_x\\ket{x}\\ket{0^w}\\ket-\\longmapsto\\sum_x\\alpha_x(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket{x}\\ket{0^w}\\ket-.\\] Both the workspace and the target factor out, unchanged. On the two-input example, \\((\\ket{000}+\\ket{110})/\\sqrt2\\) becomes \\((\\ket{000}-\\ket{110})/\\sqrt2\\). The sign is now a relative phase between input branches, available to later interference, with nothing else distinguishing them. There is a slightly cheaper variant. Instead of copying into a \\(\\ket-\\) target, apply a \\(Z\\) gate to \\(t\\) directly: \\(Z\\ket{t}=(-1)^t\\ket t\\). Then uncompute. This saves the target qubit. For multi-bit outputs, a phase \\(e^{i\\phi(v)}\\) depending on a computed value \\(v\\) can be applied by phase gates on the bits of \\(v\\), as Chapter 3 develops. Either way, the phase oracle costs about twice the gates of computing \\(f\\), since the computation runs forwards and backwards. Counting a phase query as “one query” in an algorithm’s analysis is legitimate in the oracle model; in a gate count, each such query costs a compute and an uncompute. Problems Verify the \\(Z\\)-variant on input \\(110\\): compute, apply \\(Z\\) to \\(t\\), uncompute. What phase appears? For a two-bit computed value \\(v=2v_1+v_0\\), give phase gates that apply \\(e^{2\\pi iv/4}\\) before uncomputation. A student applies \\(Z\\) to the garbage bit \\(g=ab\\) instead of \\(t\\), then uncomputes. What oracle is implemented, and could it ever be useful?"}, {"title": "2.2.3 What garbage does to interference", "chapter": "Coherent Computation", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#topic-2-2-3", "text": "2.2.3 What garbage does to interference § dirty oracle It is worth seeing the damage concretely, with an experiment that distinguishes clean from dirty oracles. Prepare the input \\((\\ket{000}+\\ket{110})/\\sqrt2\\) and run the phase-oracle construction twice: once with uncomputation, once without. Clean. The input becomes \\((\\ket{000}-\\ket{110})/\\sqrt2\\), with workspace \\(\\ket{00}\\). Now apply a CNOT from the first input bit to the second, giving \\((\\ket{000}-\\ket{100})/\\sqrt2=\\ket-\\ket{00}\\), and a Hadamard on the first bit. The first bit becomes \\(\\ket1\\) with certainty. Dirty. Without uncomputation the state is \\((\\ket{000}\\ket{00}-\\ket{110}\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). The same CNOT gives \\((\\ket{000}\\ket{00}-\\ket{100}\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). The two branches now differ in the workspace, \\(\\ket{00}\\) versus \\(\\ket{11}\\). The Hadamard on the first bit produces amplitudes \\(\\pm\\frac12\\) on four distinct complete configurations, and nothing cancels: the first bit is a fair coin. From the input register’s point of view, the dirty state is indistinguishable from a random mixture: half the time \\(\\ket{000}\\), half the time \\(-\\ket{110}\\). The relative sign that the algorithm worked to create is no longer accessible to any operation on the input alone. Garbage behaves exactly like an environment that has measured the branch. This is why every oracle in the rest of the course is assumed clean, and why constructing one costs uncomputation. Problems In the dirty case, compute the probability that the first input bit reads \\(0\\) after the CNOT and Hadamard, by listing the final amplitudes. Suppose the garbage happened to be the same for both inputs, for instance \\(g(x)=0\\) on both. Would the dirty oracle still fail the test? Could a later step “fix” the dirty state by applying \\(C^{-1}\\) after the Hadamard instead of before it?"}, {"title": "2.3.1 Counting gates, qubits, and Toffolis", "chapter": "Coherent Computation", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#topic-2-3-1", "text": "2.3.1 Counting gates, qubits, and Toffolis § gate count · Toffoli count · T gate Suppose a reversible circuit \\(C\\) uses \\(T\\) gates, preserves its input, and computes an \\(m\\)-bit answer together with garbage. The gate count of compute–copy–uncompute is \\[T\\ (\\text{compute})+m\\ (\\text{copy CNOTs})+T\\ (\\text{uncompute})=2T+m\\ \\text{gates},\\] using the same workspace as \\(C\\) plus an \\(m\\)-qubit target. This is a construction bound, not a lower bound; clever circuits often do better. But it is a uniform bound: whatever classical algorithm computes \\(f\\) in \\(T\\) reversible gates, a clean quantum oracle costs about \\(2T\\). What counts as one gate? In fault-tolerant quantum computers, the expensive operation is usually the \\(T\\) gate, \\(T=\\mathrm{diag}(1,e^{i\\pi/4})\\), not the CNOT. A Toffoli is not elementary there. A standard decomposition uses two Hadamards, six CNOTs, and seven \\(T\\) or \\(T^\\dagger\\) gates. So a resource estimate might report Toffoli count or \\(T\\) count separately from CNOT count and depth. Multi-controlled gates must also be decomposed: a \\(k\\)-controlled \\(X\\) needs on the order of \\(k\\) Toffolis with \\(k-2\\) clean ancillas. The lesson for reading algorithm papers is to check the unit. “\\(O(n^2)\\) gates” might count Toffolis, two-qubit gates, or \\(T\\) gates, and the conversions between them are constant factors that can be large in practice. A box in a circuit diagram labelled “\\(U_f\\)” or “ modular exponentiation ” might contain millions of elementary gates. Problems A classical reversible circuit for \\(f\\) uses 1000 Toffolis and 3000 CNOTs and outputs 16 bits. Estimate the Toffoli and CNOT counts of a clean oracle built by compute–copy–uncompute. Using the decomposition above, convert 2000 Toffolis into \\(T\\)-gate and CNOT counts. Two papers report “\\(10^6\\) gates” and “\\(10^5\\) gates” for the same oracle. What questions must you ask before concluding the second is ten times cheaper?"}, {"title": "2.3.2 Time–space tradeoffs", "chapter": "Coherent Computation", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#topic-2-3-2", "text": "2.3.2 Time–space tradeoffs § pebbling · time–space tradeoff The direct translation keeps every intermediate value until the end, so its workspace grows with the number of gates. For a long computation that is prohibitive. Can we free intermediate values earlier? Consider a computation in stages: \\(u_1=F_1(x)\\), \\(u_2=F_2(u_1)\\), \\(u_3=F_3(u_2)\\), and so on up to \\(u_k\\). To compute \\(u_3\\) we need \\(u_2\\), but once \\(u_3\\) exists, \\(u_2\\) could be uncomputed, provided \\(u_1\\) is still present, because uncomputing \\(u_2=F_2(u_1)\\) requires \\(u_1\\). Then, to clear \\(u_1\\) later, we need \\(x\\). Clearing a value early saves space, but if a later uncomputation needs it, we must recompute it, which costs time. This is a time–space tradeoff . This bookkeeping is a game called pebbling . Place a pebble on stage \\(i\\) (compute \\(u_i\\)) only if stage \\(i-1\\) has a pebble; remove it (uncompute) under the same condition. The goal is to end with pebbles on \\(x\\) and \\(u_k\\) only. Keeping everything uses \\(k\\) pebbles and \\(2k\\) moves. A recursive strategy does better on space: to reach stage \\(k\\), pebble stage \\(k/2\\), use it to pebble \\(k\\), then unpebble \\(k/2\\) by running the first half backwards. Each level of recursion triples the work and adds one pebble. Bennett showed in 1989 that a computation using time \\(T\\) and space \\(S\\) can be made reversible in time \\(O(T^{1+\\epsilon})\\) and space \\(O(S\\log T)\\) for any fixed \\(\\epsilon>0\\) ( Bennett, Time/Space Trade-Offs for Reversible Computation ). Practical quantum compilers use similar schedules, choosing when to uncompute to fit within a qubit budget. The central constraint is always the same: a value can be uncomputed only while the values it was computed from are still present, and the operations between compute and uncompute must leave those values unchanged. Problems For a chain of \\(k=4\\) stages, give a pebbling that ends with pebbles only on \\(u_4\\) (besides \\(x\\)) and never uses more than three pebbles at once. Compare with keeping everything. In the incorrect sequence “CNOT \\(x\\to g\\); \\(X\\) on \\(x\\); CNOT \\(x\\to g\\)”, intended to compute and then uncompute \\(g=x\\), what is left in \\(g\\)? Why is the recursive pebbling strategy more important for quantum computers than for classical reversible hardware?"}, {"title": "2.4.1 Preparation and inverse access", "chapter": "Coherent Computation", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#topic-2-4-1", "text": "2.4.1 Preparation and inverse access § preparation circuit · reflection A preparation circuit \\(A\\) is a unitary with \\(A\\ket{0^n}=\\ket\\psi\\). If its gates are known, reversing their order and inverting each gives \\(A^\\dagger\\), which maps \\(\\ket\\psi\\) back to \\(\\ket{0^n}\\). A supplied copy of \\(\\ket\\psi\\) does not give us this; neither does a box that applies \\(A\\) but cannot be run backwards. Why would we want \\(A^\\dagger\\)? To reflect about \\(\\ket\\psi\\). Let \\(S_0=2\\ket{0^n}\\bra{0^n}-I\\), which leaves \\(\\ket{0^n}\\) unchanged and negates every orthogonal basis state. Then \\[R_\\psi=A\\,S_0\\,A^\\dagger=2\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi-I.\\] Check on two kinds of input. On \\(\\ket\\psi\\): \\(A^\\dagger\\) sends it to \\(\\ket{0^n}\\), \\(S_0\\) leaves it, \\(A\\) restores \\(\\ket\\psi\\). On a state \\(\\ket\\phi\\) orthogonal to \\(\\ket\\psi\\): \\(A^\\dagger\\ket\\phi\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\ket{0^n}\\), because \\(A^\\dagger\\) preserves inner products, so \\(S_0\\) negates it and \\(A\\) returns \\(-\\ket\\phi\\). A reflection about \\(\\ket\\psi\\) is exactly the map that fixes \\(\\ket\\psi\\) and negates everything orthogonal to it. The zero-state reflection \\(S_0\\) itself is a clean phase oracle for the predicate “\\(x\\neq0^n\\)”: it is \\(-\\)(phase oracle of \\(x=0^n\\)), which differs by a global sign. With \\(X\\) gates on every qubit and an \\(n\\)-controlled \\(Z\\), it costs \\(O(n)\\) Toffolis. Chapter 5 uses \\(R_\\psi\\) to amplify success probabilities, at the cost of one \\(A\\) and one \\(A^\\dagger\\) per reflection. Problems For one qubit with \\(A=H\\), compute \\(R_\\psi=HS_0H\\) as a matrix and identify it. Show that \\(R_\\psi^2=I\\) and that \\(R_\\psi\\) is Hermitian. Someone has a box \\(B\\) that outputs fresh copies of \\(\\ket\\psi\\) on demand, but no circuit for it. Can they implement \\(R_\\psi\\) exactly?"}, {"title": "2.4.2 Controlled versions and phase conventions", "chapter": "Coherent Computation", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#topic-2-4-2", "text": "2.4.2 Controlled versions and phase conventions § controlled version · global phase Phase estimation in Chapter 4 needs controlled-\\(U\\): apply \\(U\\) to a target register only on the branch where a control qubit is \\(1\\), \\[\\mathrm{C}U=\\ket0\\bra0\\otimes I+\\ket1\\bra1\\otimes U.\\] Given a gate sequence \\(U=G_k\\cdots G_1\\), we can build \\(\\mathrm{C}U\\) by controlling each gate: \\(\\mathrm{C}U=\\mathrm{C}G_k\\cdots\\mathrm{C}G_1\\). This works because on the control-\\(0\\) branch every controlled gate does nothing, and on the control-\\(1\\) branch every one acts. A controlled single-qubit gate costs a few CNOTs and rotations; a controlled Toffoli is a three-controlled \\(X\\), and so on. So controlling a circuit multiplies its cost by a constant. There is one subtlety. Uncontrolled, \\(U\\) and \\(e^{i\\theta}U\\) are the same physical operation. Controlled, they differ: \\(\\mathrm{C}(e^{i\\theta}U)=\\mathrm{C}U\\cdot(P(\\theta)\\otimes I)\\), where \\(P(\\theta)=\\mathrm{diag}(1,e^{i\\theta})\\) on the control. The global phase of \\(U\\) becomes a relative phase on the control. In phase estimation, that phase is exactly what is measured. So a controlled version is defined only once the global phase of \\(U\\) is fixed. A gate library that implements rotations “up to global phase” must be checked before its gates are controlled. For a sealed box \\(U\\), no general procedure produces \\(\\mathrm{C}U\\). This is a theorem, not a gap in our cleverness: given only black-box access to an unknown unitary, no circuit implements its controlled version, essentially because the box cannot reveal its own global phase ( Araújo, Feix, Costa, and Brukner, 2014 ). Algorithms that need \\(\\mathrm{C}U\\) must assume it as access, or construct \\(U\\) from known gates. Problems Show that \\(\\mathrm{C}(-I)=Z\\otimes I\\) on a control and one target qubit. The rotation \\(R_z(\\theta)=\\mathrm{diag}(e^{-i\\theta/2},e^{i\\theta/2})\\) and the phase gate \\(P(\\theta)=\\mathrm{diag}(1,e^{i\\theta})\\) differ by a global phase. What is the relative phase on the control between their controlled versions? In phase estimation, \\(U\\) has eigenvalue \\(e^{2\\pi i\\phi}\\) on \\(\\ket u\\). If a compiler silently replaces \\(U\\) by \\(e^{i\\alpha}U\\) before controlling it, what does phase estimation report?"}, {"title": "2.4.3 Output requirements", "chapter": "Coherent Computation", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#topic-2-4-3", "text": "2.4.3 Output requirements § A clean oracle applied to a superposition produces \\(\\sum_x\\alpha_x\\ket{x}\\ket{f(x)}\\). It is tempting to say this “computes all values of \\(f\\).” It does not, in any sense a user can collect. Measuring yields one pair \\((x,f(x))\\) with probability \\(|\\alpha_x|^2\\). Collecting all \\(2^n\\) values by repetition would take at least \\(2^n\\) runs, more than a classical program that simply evaluates each. A state of this kind is useful only if the next coherent operation extracts a property from it: a phase pattern resolved by Hadamards (Chapter 3), a period resolved by a Fourier transform (Chapter 4), a marked component amplified by reflections (Chapter 5). The subroutine’s contract should therefore say what form its output takes and what the caller is expected to do with it. We now have a concrete method for evaluating functions coherently, the costs it incurs, and the access a subroutine can offer: forward, inverse, controlled, and the reflection built from them. When the retained effect is a sign, the computation becomes a phase encoding. Chapter 3 uses phase encoding to recover a hidden string of any length and makes precise how a small interference example extends to a family. Problems For \\(n=10\\) and the uniform superposition, how many runs of “evaluate and measure” are needed on average to see every input at least once? A subroutine’s output is “the state \\(\\sum_x\\alpha_x\\ket x\\ket{f(x)}\\) with a clean workspace.” Name two different follow-up operations from later chapters and the classical output each produces. Why must a subroutine whose output is a quantum state specify its accuracy as a distance between states, rather than as an error in each amplitude?"}, {"title": "Coherent Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#exercise-1", "text": "A circuit maps |x⟩|0⟩|0⟩ to |x⟩|f(x)⟩|x₁⟩, where x₁ is the first bit of x. Does it satisfy the clean contract? What would it do to the Deutsch–Jozsa experiment for inputs that differ in x₁? No: the workspace holds x₁, which depends on the input. In the Deutsch–Jozsa experiment, branches with different x₁ carry orthogonal workspace states and cannot interfere, so the final Hadamards no longer concentrate amplitude on the answer. Uncomputing x₁ (a CNOT from x₁ into the workspace) repairs it."}, {"title": "Coherent Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#exercise-2", "text": "A circuit maps |x⟩|0⟩|0⟩↦|x⟩|f(x)⟩|f(x)⟩: it leaves a second copy of the answer in its one workspace qubit. Since the garbage is “only the answer,” is the oracle clean? Use it with the output target in |−⟩ inside Deutsch’s algorithm and find the probability of the correct answer for a balanced f. No. The clean contract requires the workspace to return to |0⟩; here it holds f(x), which depends on the input. With the target in |−⟩, the query gives 1/√2((−1)^(f(0))|0⟩|f(0)⟩+(−1)^(f(1))|1⟩|f(1)⟩)|−⟩. For a balanced function the workspace values differ, so the workspace is a which-path record. After the final Hadamard the input outcomes 0 and 1 each have probability 1/2, so the answer is correct only half the time. For a constant function the workspace is the same on both branches and the circuit still works, which is why a test on constant inputs alone would not reveal the bug."}, {"title": "Coherent Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#exercise-3", "text": "A clean oracle for a predicate with exactly one marked input among N=2¹⁰ is applied to the uniform superposition with target |0⟩, and the target is measured. What is the probability of seeing 1? If it is seen, what is the state of the input register, and why is this not yet a fast search algorithm? The marked branch has amplitude 2⁻⁵, so the target reads 1 with probability 1/1024. When it does, the input collapses to the marked string |x*⟩, which can be read out. But a success probability of 1/N means about N repetitions on average, the same as testing inputs one by one. The clean oracle has written the answer into the state without making it likely to be observed. Increasing the marked amplitude before measuring is the job of amplitude amplification in Chapter 5, which needs the reflections built from clean preparation and inverse access."}, {"title": "Coherent Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#exercise-4", "text": "Build a clean phase oracle for f(a,b,c) = a AND b AND c using one ancilla, and count Toffolis. Toffoli from a,b into g; controlled-Z between g and c (phase −1 exactly when g = c = 1, i.e. a = b = c = 1); Toffoli from a,b into g again to uncompute. Two Toffolis and one CZ, with the ancilla returned to |0⟩. Equivalently, a doubly controlled Z on a,b,c decomposed with one ancilla."}, {"title": "Coherent Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#exercise-5", "text": "The majority function on three bits is MAJ(a,b,c)=ab⊕ ac⊕ bc. (a) Check this identity on all inputs with at least two ones. (b) Build a clean oracle |abc⟩|y⟩↦|abc⟩|y⊕MAJ⟩ with no workspace and count Toffolis. (c) Build the phase oracle (−1)^(MAJ) with no Toffolis at all. (a) If exactly two bits are 1, exactly one product is 1, so the XOR is 1. If all three are 1, all three products are 1 and 1⊕1⊕1=1. With fewer than two ones every product is 0. (b) Apply three Toffolis into y, controlled on (a,b), (a,c), and (b,c). Each adds one term by XOR, so y receives MAJ. No intermediate value is stored, so there is nothing to uncompute: three Toffolis and no ancilla, compared with three Toffolis, three ancillas, and an uncompute for a naive translation. (c) The phase factorizes as (−1)^(ab)(−1)^(ac)(−1)^(bc), so three controlled-Z gates on the three pairs implement the phase oracle exactly. The lesson: when a function is an XOR of terms, computing each term straight into the target, or straight into a phase, avoids garbage altogether."}, {"title": "Coherent Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#exercise-6", "text": "Let INC be the two-bit incrementer, |j⟩↦|j+1 mod 4⟩, with j=2b₁+b₀. Apply it to |chi⟩=1/2(|0⟩−|1⟩+|2⟩−|3⟩) and to |eta⟩=1/2(|0⟩+i|1⟩−|2⟩−i|3⟩). What do you find? Generalize to amplitudes cⱼ=iʲᵏ/2. Since INCΣⱼcⱼ|j⟩=Σⱼcⱼ₋₁|j⟩ (indices mod 4), each output amplitude is the input amplitude of the previous label. For |chi⟩, cⱼ=(−1)ʲ/2 and cⱼ₋₁=−cⱼ, so INC|chi⟩=−|chi⟩. For |eta⟩, cⱼ=iʲ/2 and cⱼ₋₁=i⁻¹cⱼ, so INC|eta⟩=−i|eta⟩. In general cⱼ=iʲᵏ/2 gives cⱼ₋₁=i⁻ᵏcⱼ: the state is unchanged except for the factor e^(−2π ik/4). These four states, one for each k, are the eigenstates of modular addition; the arithmetic shows up only as a phase. Chapter 4 exploits exactly this: controlled powers of modular arithmetic, applied to such states, write k/4 into a phase that the Fourier transform can read."}, {"title": "Coherent Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#exercise-7", "text": "A computation has k=8 stages uᵢ=Fᵢ(uᵢ₋₁), u₀=x. Compare two reversible schedules that end holding only x and u₈. (a) Keep everything: compute all stages, copy u₈, uncompute the rest. (b) Recursive halving: to reach stage k, pebble stage k/2, use it to pebble stage k, then unpebble stage k/2. Count stage evaluations and the maximum number of stage values held at once in each. (a) Computing u₁,dots,u₈ takes 8 evaluations and uncomputing u₇,dots,u₁ takes 7, so 15 evaluations with up to 8 stage values held. (b) Reaching stage k costs three half-size problems, so P(8)=3P(4)=9P(2)=27P(1)=27 evaluations. While the second half runs, u_(k/2) must be kept in addition to that half’s own peak, so S(8)=S(4)+1=S(2)+2=S(1)+3=4. Recursion halves the memory at the cost of almost doubling the work. In general it uses about k^(log₂3)≈ k^(1.58) evaluations and log₂k+1 stored values. On a quantum computer, where each stored value occupies qubits that must stay coherent, trading evaluations for qubits is often worthwhile."}, {"title": "Coherent Computation — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#exercise-8", "text": "Circuit C computes g=ab into a workspace qubit (Toffoli), then t=g⊕ c. A programmer uncomputes with a different circuit C' for the same f=ab⊕ c: C' copies a into the workspace (CNOT), applies a Toffoli from the workspace and b into t, then a CNOT from c into t. They apply C, copy t into y, then C'⁻¹. “Both circuits compute f, so either can uncompute.” Trace a basis input and find what remains in the workspace. After C and the copy, the registers hold (a,b,c, w=ab, t=ab⊕ c, y⊕ f). Now C'⁻¹: the CNOT from c gives t=ab; the Toffoli from w=ab and b adds ab· b=ab, so t=0; the CNOT from a gives w=ab⊕ a=a∧lnot b. The target is cleared by coincidence, but the workspace holds a∧lnot b, which is 1 on inputs with a=1,b=0. The oracle is dirty and will destroy interference between those inputs and the rest. The principle: an uncomputation must undo the particular intermediate values the computation produced. Using the exact reverse of C guarantees this; another circuit for f generally assumes different garbage."}, {"title": "Coherent Computation — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#exercise-9", "text": "A preparation circuit A uses 500 gates. Each amplification iteration in Chapter 5 uses one reflection about the prepared state and one phase oracle costing 300 gates. Estimate the gate cost of 20 iterations, including an initial preparation, and identify which access assumptions the count relies on. Each iteration costs 300 (oracle) + 500 (A†) + z (zero reflection) + 500 (A) = 1300 + z gates. Twenty iterations plus the initial A cost 500 + 20(1300 + z) = 26,500 + 20z gates. The count assumes a known gate sequence for A, so that A† is available at equal cost, and a clean oracle; with only a sealed box for A, the reflection could not be built this way."}, {"title": "Coherent Computation — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-coherent-computation.html#exercise-10", "text": "Suppose uncomputation is imperfect, so the two input branches of the test in Section 2.2.3 carry workspace states |g₀⟩ and |g₁⟩ instead of identical ones. After the CNOT the state is 1/√2(|000⟩|g₀⟩−|100⟩|g₁⟩). Show that the Hadamard on the first bit gives outcome 1 with probability 1/2(1+Re⟨g₀|g₁⟩). Evaluate it for |g₀⟩=|0⟩,|g₁⟩=|+⟩, and interpret the clean and fully dirty cases. After the Hadamard the state is 1/2[|0⟩|00⟩(|g₀⟩−|g₁⟩)+|1⟩|00⟩(|g₀⟩+|g₁⟩)]. The probability of first bit 1 is 1/4||g₀⟩+|g₁⟩|²=1/4(2+2 Re⟨g₀|g₁⟩)=1/2(1+Re⟨g₀|g₁⟩). Clean (|g₀⟩=|g₁⟩): probability 1. Fully dirty, orthogonal garbage (as with |00⟩ versus |11⟩): probability 1/2, a fair coin. For |0⟩ and |+⟩, the overlap is 1/√2 and the probability is 1/2(1+1/√2)≈0.854. Interference visibility is set by the overlap of the leftover records: partial garbage gives partial damage, and the error compounds when the oracle is called many times."}, {"title": "3.1.1 Eigenstate targets", "chapter": "Phase Encoding", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#topic-3-1-1", "text": "3.1.1 Eigenstate targets § eigenstate · eigenvalue The \\(\\ket-\\) target worked because flipping it only multiplied it by \\(-1\\). Look for the same property in any unitary \\(V\\): a state \\(\\ket u\\) that \\(V\\) changes only by a phase, \\[V\\ket u=e^{i\\phi}\\ket u.\\] Such a state is an eigenstate of \\(V\\), and \\(e^{i\\phi}\\) is its eigenvalue . Because \\(V\\) is unitary, it preserves lengths, so the eigenvalue has magnitude one: it is a pure phase. For \\(V=X\\), the eigenstates are \\(\\ket+\\) with eigenvalue \\(1\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) with eigenvalue \\(-1\\). Now control \\(V\\) with a qubit in \\(\\ket+\\): \\[\\mathrm{C}V\\,\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\ket u=\\frac{\\ket0\\ket u+\\ket1\\,e^{i\\phi}\\ket u}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket u.\\] The target is untouched and factors out. The phase \\(e^{i\\phi}\\), which was a global phase of \\(V\\ket u\\) and therefore unobservable, is now a relative phase between the control branches. This is phase kickback in its general form. It needs three things: a target prepared in an eigenstate, controlled access to \\(V\\), and the eigenvalue being the information we want. All three are real requirements. If \\(\\ket u\\) is not an eigenstate, the target becomes entangled with the control and the clean factorization fails. If only an uncontrolled \\(V\\) is available, the phase stays global. And if many eigenstates are present at once, each contributes its own phase on its own branch; Chapter 4 shows that this produces a random sample of the eigenvalues rather than all of them. Problems Find the eigenstates and eigenvalues of \\(Z\\) and of \\(S=\\mathrm{diag}(1,i)\\). What does controlled-\\(S\\) do to \\(\\ket+\\ket1\\)? Apply controlled-\\(X\\) to \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\). Is \\(\\ket0\\) an eigenstate of \\(X\\)? What goes wrong? Show that if \\(V\\ket u=e^{i\\phi}\\ket u\\), then controlled-\\(V^k\\) kicks back \\(e^{ik\\phi}\\). Why does this make powers of \\(V\\) useful for learning \\(\\phi\\) precisely?"}, {"title": "3.1.2 The Hadamard test", "chapter": "Phase Encoding", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#topic-3-1-2", "text": "3.1.2 The Hadamard test § Hadamard test Once a phase is on the control, a Hadamard turns it into a probability. Apply \\(H\\) to \\((\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\): \\[\\frac{1+e^{i\\phi}}{2}\\ket0+\\frac{1-e^{i\\phi}}{2}\\ket1,\\qquad \\Pr[0]=\\left|\\frac{1+e^{i\\phi}}{2}\\right|^2=\\frac{1+\\cos\\phi}{2}.\\] So measuring the control is a coin whose bias reveals \\(\\cos\\phi\\). This circuit, \\(H\\), controlled-\\(U\\), \\(H\\), measure, is the Hadamard test . It works even when the target is not an eigenstate. For a general state \\(\\ket\\psi\\), the same calculation gives \\[\\Pr[0]=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{Re}\\bra\\psi U\\ket\\psi}{2}.\\] To see this, write the state before the final Hadamard as \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0\\ket\\psi+\\ket1U\\ket\\psi)\\). The Hadamard puts amplitude \\(\\frac12(\\ket\\psi+U\\ket\\psi)\\) on control outcome \\(0\\), whose squared length is \\(\\frac14(2+\\bra\\psi U\\ket\\psi+\\bra\\psi U^\\dagger\\ket\\psi)=\\frac12(1+\\operatorname{Re}\\bra\\psi U\\ket\\psi)\\). Inserting \\(S^\\dagger\\) on the control before the final Hadamard gives \\(\\frac12(1+\\operatorname{Im}\\bra\\psi U\\ket\\psi)\\) instead. The Hadamard test is the simplest phase-reading circuit, and its limitation is instructive. One run gives one bit. To estimate \\(\\cos\\phi\\) to precision \\(\\epsilon\\) we need on the order of \\(1/\\epsilon^2\\) runs, by the Chebyshev argument of Section 1.4.2. And \\(\\cos\\phi\\) does not distinguish \\(\\phi\\) from \\(-\\phi\\). Phase estimation (Chapter 4) and iterative estimation (Chapter 11) do better by combining tests with controlled powers . Problems Apply the Hadamard test with \\(U=Z\\) and \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket+\\). Compute \\(\\Pr[0]\\) both from the formula and directly. For an eigenstate with \\(\\phi=\\pi/3\\), what is \\(\\Pr[0]\\)? How many runs are needed so that, by Chebyshev’s bound, the estimate of \\(\\Pr[0]\\) is within \\(0.01\\) with probability at least \\(0.9\\)? The Hadamard test estimates \\(\\cos\\phi\\). Explain why the outcomes for \\(\\phi\\) and \\(2\\pi-\\phi\\) are identical, and how the \\(S^\\dagger\\) variant resolves the ambiguity."}, {"title": "3.1.3 Bit oracles and phase oracles", "chapter": "Phase Encoding", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#topic-3-1-3", "text": "3.1.3 Bit oracles and phase oracles § bit oracle Two forms of Boolean oracle appear in the literature: the bit oracle \\(U_f\\ket x\\ket y=\\ket x\\ket{y\\oplus f(x)}\\) and the phase oracle \\(O_f\\ket x=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\). How do they relate? From a bit oracle to a phase oracle is one query: put the target in \\(\\ket-\\), as in Chapter 1. Or, if \\(U_f\\) is built by compute–copy–uncompute, replace the copy by a \\(Z\\) gate and save the target entirely. The other direction is subtler. A phase oracle \\(O_f\\) cannot distinguish \\(f\\) from \\(1\\oplus f\\): their phase oracles differ by the global factor \\(-1\\). A bit oracle can, since \\(U_f\\ket x\\ket0\\) and \\(U_{1\\oplus f}\\ket x\\ket0\\) give different target values. So an uncontrolled phase oracle alone cannot implement a bit oracle. With a controlled phase oracle it can. Apply \\(H\\) to the target \\(\\ket y\\), use the target as the control of \\(O_f\\), and apply \\(H\\) again: \\[\\ket x\\ket y\\xrightarrow{H}\\ket x\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\textstyle\\sum_b(-1)^{yb}\\ket b\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{C}O_f}\\ket x\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\sum_b(-1)^{b(y\\oplus f(x))}\\ket b\\xrightarrow{H}\\ket x\\ket{y\\oplus f(x)}.\\] The controlled version fixes the global phase: the control-\\(0\\) branch gets no sign at all, so \\(f\\) and \\(1\\oplus f\\) become distinguishable. This is the controlled-access lesson of Section 2.4.2 in a specific case. When a paper says “given a phase oracle”, check whether controlled access is assumed. Problems Verify the bit-from-phase construction on \\(\\ket x\\ket0\\) when \\(f(x)=1\\). Show that one query to the bit oracle \\(U_f\\) implements the controlled phase oracle \\(\\ket c\\ket x\\mapsto(-1)^{c f(x)}\\ket c\\ket x\\). A phase oracle \\(O_f\\) is used in Deutsch–Jozsa. Does it matter that it cannot distinguish \\(f\\) from \\(1\\oplus f\\)?"}, {"title": "3.2.1 The Hadamard transform on many qubits", "chapter": "Phase Encoding", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#topic-3-2-1", "text": "3.2.1 The Hadamard transform on many qubits § Hadamard transform · bitwise inner product On one qubit, \\[H\\ket x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\big(\\ket0+(-1)^x\\ket1\\big)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\sum_{z\\in\\{0,1\\}}(-1)^{xz}\\ket z\\]. On \\(n\\) qubits, apply \\(H\\) to each and multiply out: \\[H^{\\otimes n}\\ket x=\\bigotimes_{j=1}^n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\sum_{z_j}(-1)^{x_jz_j}\\ket{z_j}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2^n}}\\sum_{z\\in\\{0,1\\}^n}(-1)^{x\\cdot z}\\ket z,\\] where \\(x\\cdot z=x_1z_1\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus x_nz_n\\) is the bitwise inner product modulo two. The sign of each term is the parity of the positions where both strings have a \\(1\\). This is the Hadamard transform . Two consequences carry the whole section. First, \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\ket{0^n}\\) is the uniform superposition, since \\(0\\cdot z=0\\) for every \\(z\\). Second, \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\) is its own inverse, because each \\(H\\) is. So if a state has the form \\(2^{-n/2}\\sum_x(-1)^{s\\cdot x}\\ket x\\) for some string \\(s\\), then \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\) maps it exactly to \\(\\ket s\\). Sign patterns of this special “linear” form are read perfectly by one layer of Hadamards. The rows of the table are orthogonal: for \\(s\\neq s'\\), \\[\\sum_x(-1)^{s\\cdot x}(-1)^{s'\\cdot x}=\\sum_x(-1)^{(s\\oplus s')\\cdot x}=0\\], since a nonzero string \\(t=s\\oplus s'\\) has \\(t\\cdot x=1\\) for exactly half the \\(x\\). This cancellation identity is the binary analogue of the roots-of-unity cancellation that drives Fourier methods in Chapter 4. Problems Write \\(H^{\\otimes2}\\ket{11}\\) explicitly. Show that for a nonzero string \\(t\\), exactly half of all \\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) have \\(t\\cdot x=1\\). Express the zero-outcome amplitude after \\(H^{\\otimes n}O_fH^{\\otimes n}\\ket{0^n}\\) for an arbitrary Boolean \\(f\\), and interpret it."}, {"title": "3.2.2 Bernstein–Vazirani", "chapter": "Phase Encoding", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#topic-3-2-2", "text": "3.2.2 Bernstein–Vazirani § Bernstein–Vazirani algorithm · hidden string Suppose \\(f_s(x)=s\\cdot x\\) for a hidden string \\(s\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\), and we have a clean oracle for \\(f_s\\). The task is to find \\(s\\). A classical query returns one bit, \\(s\\cdot x\\), which is one linear equation for the unknown \\(s\\). Querying \\(x=e_j\\) (a single \\(1\\) in position \\(j\\)) returns \\(s_j\\), so \\(n\\) queries suffice. They are also necessary for any classical algorithm: there are \\(2^n\\) possible strings, and \\(k\\) queries return \\(k\\) bits, which can distinguish at most \\(2^k\\) cases. The quantum algorithm uses one query. Prepare \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\ket{0^n}\\) and the target \\(\\ket-\\), query, and apply \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\): \\[\\ket{0^n}\\xrightarrow{H^{\\otimes n}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2^n}}\\sum_x\\ket x\\xrightarrow{O_{f_s}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2^n}}\\sum_x(-1)^{s\\cdot x}\\ket x\\xrightarrow{H^{\\otimes n}}\\ket s.\\] The last step is the observation of Section 3.2.1: the phase pattern is a row of the Hadamard table, so the Hadamard layer maps it to the corresponding basis state. Measuring returns \\(s\\) with certainty. Here is an \\(n=3\\) example with \\(s=101\\). The signs \\((-1)^{s\\cdot x}\\) on \\(x=000,001,\\ldots,111\\) are \\(+,-,+,-,-,+,-,+\\). The final amplitude of \\(z\\) is \\(\\frac18\\sum_x(-1)^{(s\\oplus z)\\cdot x}\\), which is \\(1\\) for \\(z=101\\) and \\(0\\) otherwise. This is the Bernstein–Vazirani algorithm (1993/1997) . Its query separation, one versus \\(n\\), holds for any success criterion , since the classical bound is an information count. Its cost is otherwise ordinary: \\(2n+1\\) Hadamards and \\(n\\) measurements around the oracle. And if \\(f_s\\) is given as a circuit we can read, \\(s\\) could often be found by inspecting the circuit; the speedup is relative to black-box access. Problems For \\(n=2\\) and \\(s=11\\), list the four signs after the query and verify that the Hadamards produce \\(\\ket{11}\\). Suppose the oracle is promised to compute \\(s\\cdot x\\oplus b\\) for unknown \\(s\\) and an unknown bit \\(b\\). What does the circuit return, and can it learn \\(b\\)? An implementation of the oracle leaves the garbage bit \\(x_1\\) in its workspace. What does the circuit return for \\(s=000\\)?"}, {"title": "3.2.3 Deutsch–Jozsa and promise assumptions", "chapter": "Phase Encoding", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#topic-3-2-3", "text": "3.2.3 Deutsch–Jozsa and promise assumptions § promise problem The Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm is the same circuit with a different promise. For any Boolean \\(f\\), the final amplitude of \\(\\ket{0^n}\\) is the average of \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\), as Problem 3 of Section 3.2.1 showed. Under the promise that \\(f\\) is constant or balanced, that average is \\(\\pm1\\) or \\(0\\), so the circuit outputs \\(0^n\\) with certainty for constant \\(f\\) and never for balanced \\(f\\). One query decides this promise problem exactly. What about functions outside the promise? If \\(f\\) takes the value \\(1\\) on \\(k\\) of the \\(2^n\\) inputs, the zero outcome has probability \\(\\big(1-2k/2^n\\big)^2\\). For \\(n=3\\): Only \\(k=0\\), \\(k=8\\) (constant) and \\(k=4\\) (balanced) give certain answers. A function with \\(k=3\\) gives zero with probability \\(1/16\\). An algorithm that announced “balanced” whenever the outcome is nonzero would be making claims about inputs the problem never covered. The classical comparison depends heavily on the success criterion, as Section 1.5.2 showed. Exact deterministic classical algorithms need \\(2^{n-1}+1\\) queries in the worst case. Randomized algorithms with error \\(\\delta\\) need only \\(O(\\log(1/\\delta))\\). So Deutsch–Jozsa separates exact quantum and exact classical query complexity exponentially, but bounded-error classical algorithms close the gap. Bernstein–Vazirani’s separation is smaller (one versus \\(n\\)) but survives randomization. Simon’s problem , next, gives an exponential separation that survives randomization. Problems For \\(n=4\\), compute the zero-outcome probability when \\(f\\) is \\(1\\) on 6 inputs. Show that for a balanced \\(f\\), every nonzero outcome \\(z\\) is possible in principle but the probability distribution depends on \\(f\\). Give an example for \\(n=2\\). A test suite checks a Deutsch–Jozsa implementation on the two constant functions and on \\(f(x)=x_1\\) only. Name an implementation bug that would pass this suite."}, {"title": "3.3.1 Simon’s problem", "chapter": "Phase Encoding", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#topic-3-3-1", "text": "3.3.1 Simon’s problem § Simon’s problem Let \\(f:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}^n\\) satisfy, for some hidden nonzero \\(s\\), \\[f(x)=f(y)\\iff y=x\\ \\text{or}\\ y=x\\oplus s.\\] So \\(f\\) is two-to-one, and its collisions are exactly the pairs \\(\\{x,x\\oplus s\\}\\). The task of Simon’s problem is to find \\(s\\). Classically, the only way to learn about \\(s\\) is to find a collision \\(f(x)=f(y)\\), which reveals \\(s=x\\oplus y\\). With \\(k\\) queries there are about \\(k^2/2\\) pairs, each colliding with probability about \\(2^{-n}\\), so on the order of \\(2^{n/2}\\) queries are needed. Simon proved this is a lower bound for any bounded-error classical algorithm. The quantum circuit uses a clean bit oracle with an \\(n\\)-qubit output register. Prepare the uniform superposition, query, and measure the output register: \\[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2^n}}\\sum_x\\ket x\\ket{f(x)}\\ \\xrightarrow{\\text{measure output, see }f(x_0)}\\ \\frac{\\ket{x_0}+\\ket{x_0\\oplus s}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] The measurement leaves a superposition of the two inputs consistent with the observed value. We do not learn \\(x_0\\) usefully: it is random. But the difference between the two branches is \\(s\\), wherever they sit. Now apply \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\). The amplitude of \\(z\\) is \\[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2^{n+1}}}\\Big((-1)^{x_0\\cdot z}+(-1)^{(x_0\\oplus s)\\cdot z}\\Big)=\\frac{(-1)^{x_0\\cdot z}}{\\sqrt{2^{n+1}}}\\big(1+(-1)^{s\\cdot z}\\big).\\] It is zero whenever \\(s\\cdot z=1\\), and has magnitude \\(2/\\sqrt{2^{n+1}}\\) whenever \\(s\\cdot z=0\\). The random \\(x_0\\) contributes only a phase. So each run returns a uniformly random \\(z\\) satisfying \\(s\\cdot z=0\\). Measuring the output register is not essential; leaving it unmeasured gives the same statistics for \\(z\\), since the output is never used again. It is a convenient way to see the state. The algorithm is due to Simon, On the Power of Quantum Computation (1994/1997) . Problems For \\(n=3\\) and \\(s=110\\), list the four collision pairs and the strings \\(z\\) the circuit can return. Why does the random \\(x_0\\) not spoil the result, even though different runs collapse onto different pairs? What does the circuit return if \\(f\\) is one-to-one (equivalently, \\(s=0^n\\))? How could an algorithm detect this case?"}, {"title": "3.3.2 Recovering the string", "chapter": "Phase Encoding", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#topic-3-3-2", "text": "3.3.2 Recovering the string § linear algebra over GF(2) · classical post-processing Each run gives one equation \\(s\\cdot z=0\\) over bits. These are linear equations modulo two, and solving them is linear algebra over GF(2) , arithmetic in which \\(1+1=0\\). Collect \\(n-1\\) linearly independent equations; then the solution space is \\(\\{0^n,s\\}\\), and the nonzero solution is \\(s\\). Gaussian elimination finds it in \\(O(n^3)\\) classical bit operations. Continue the example \\(s=110\\). Suppose the runs return \\(z=001\\) and then \\(z=111\\). The equations are \\(s_3=0\\) and \\(s_1\\oplus s_2\\oplus s_3=0\\), so \\(s_1=s_2\\). The nonzero solution is \\(s=110\\). If the second run had returned \\(000\\) or \\(001\\) again, it would add no information, and we would run again. How many runs are needed? The samples are uniform over the \\((n-1)\\)-dimensional space of strings orthogonal to \\(s\\). If we already have \\(k\\) independent samples, a new one is dependent with probability \\(2^{k}/2^{n-1}\\). Summing the expected waiting times, the expected number of runs to collect \\(n-1\\) independent samples is less than \\(n+1\\). With \\(n+O(\\log(1/\\delta))\\) runs, failure probability is below \\(\\delta\\). Simon’s algorithm uses \\(O(n)\\) queries against the classical \\(\\Omega(2^{n/2})\\), an exponential separation for bounded-error algorithms in the black-box model. Its structure is the template for period finding: a coherent query, a measurement that collapses onto a coset of the hidden structure, a Fourier-type transform that turns the unknown shift into a harmless phase, and classical post-processing of several samples. In Chapter 4 the group \\(\\{0,1\\}^n\\) with XOR becomes the integers modulo \\(N\\) with addition, the Hadamard transform becomes the quantum Fourier transform , and Gaussian elimination becomes continued fractions. Problems For \\(n=3\\), suppose the samples are \\(110\\) and \\(111\\). Find \\(s\\). For \\(n=4\\), what is the probability that three samples are linearly independent? Is the speedup of Simon’s algorithm a speedup for any practical problem?"}, {"title": "3.4.1 Phases from computed values", "chapter": "Phase Encoding", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#topic-3-4-1", "text": "3.4.1 Phases from computed values § phase gate · binary fraction Suppose a clean computation writes an \\(m\\)-bit integer \\(v=\\sum_jv_j2^j\\) into a register, and we want the phase \\(e^{2\\pi iv/2^m}\\). The exponent splits into a sum over bits, so the phase splits into a product: \\[e^{2\\pi iv/2^m}=\\prod_{j=0}^{m-1}e^{2\\pi iv_j2^j/2^m}=\\prod_{j=0}^{m-1}P\\!\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{2^{m-j}}\\right)^{v_j},\\] where \\(P(\\theta)=\\mathrm{diag}(1,e^{i\\theta})\\) is the phase gate . So apply \\(P(2\\pi/2^{m-j})\\) to bit \\(j\\), one gate per bit, then uncompute \\(v\\). The highest bit gets \\(P(\\pi)=Z\\), the next \\(P(\\pi/2)=S\\), then \\(P(\\pi/4)=T\\), and so on. The quantity \\(v/2^m\\) is the binary fraction \\(0.v_{m-1}\\cdots v_0\\), and each bit contributes its own share of a full turn. For \\(m=3\\) and \\(v=5=101_2\\): \\(Z\\) on \\(v_2=1\\) gives \\(-1\\), \\(S\\) on \\(v_1=0\\) gives \\(1\\), \\(T\\) on \\(v_0=1\\) gives \\(e^{i\\pi/4}\\). The product is \\(-e^{i\\pi/4}=e^{i5\\pi/4}=e^{2\\pi i\\cdot5/8}\\). Correct. The same idea applies with a real angle: if \\(\\theta(x)\\) is computed to \\(m\\) bits as an approximation, applying the phase gates gives \\(e^{i\\tilde\\theta(x)}\\) with \\(\\tilde\\theta\\) the rounded angle. The cost is \\(m\\) controlled rotations plus computing and uncomputing \\(\\tilde\\theta\\). Small-angle rotations are not elementary in fault-tolerant gate sets; each must itself be synthesized to some accuracy, which Section 3.4.3 accounts for. Problems Which phase gates produce \\(e^{2\\pi iv/16}\\) for a four-bit \\(v\\), and what is the phase for \\(v=6\\)? Does the bitwise product formula still produce \\(e^{2\\pi iv/3}\\) for an \\(m\\)-bit \\(v\\)? What changes in practice? A student applies the phase gates to the bits of \\(v\\) but forgets to uncompute \\(v\\). Describe the resulting state for an input superposition \\(\\sum_x\\alpha_x\\ket x\\)."}, {"title": "3.4.2 Phase kickback from modular addition", "chapter": "Phase Encoding", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#topic-3-4-2", "text": "3.4.2 Phase kickback from modular addition § Fourier state · phase-gradient trick There is a second way, which reuses arithmetic instead of rotation gates. On an \\(m\\)-qubit register with \\(M=2^m\\), prepare the state \\[\\ket\\chi=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt M}\\sum_{y=0}^{M-1}e^{-2\\pi iy/M}\\ket y.\\] Adding an integer \\(v\\) modulo \\(M\\) to this register multiplies it by a phase. Indeed, substituting \\(z=y+v\\bmod M\\), \\[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt M}\\sum_ye^{-2\\pi iy/M}\\ket{y+v}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt M}\\sum_ze^{-2\\pi i(z-v)/M}\\ket z=e^{2\\pi iv/M}\\ket\\chi.\\] The phase is consistent modulo \\(M\\) because \\(e^{-2\\pi iM/M}=1\\). So \\(\\ket\\chi\\) is an eigenstate of “add \\(v\\)”, with eigenvalue \\(e^{2\\pi iv/M}\\). Section 3.1.1 then says what to do: control the addition by the input. If a clean computation produces \\(v(x)\\) and we add it into \\(\\ket\\chi\\), the phase \\(e^{2\\pi iv(x)/M}\\) is kicked back onto \\(\\ket x\\), and \\(\\ket\\chi\\) is untouched. This construction, sometimes called the phase-gradient trick , turns an adder into a bank of phase rotations. The state \\(\\ket\\chi\\) is prepared once and reused for every query, since it is never changed. It is a Fourier state : Chapter 4 shows it is the quantum Fourier transform of a basis state. The cost is a reversible adder per phase operation plus a one-time preparation. Problems For \\(m=1\\) (\\(M=2\\)), what is \\(\\ket\\chi\\), and what does “add \\(v\\) mod 2” do to it? For \\(M=4\\), verify by direct calculation that adding \\(1\\) maps \\(\\ket\\chi\\) to \\(i\\ket\\chi\\). Why must the adder be exactly modulo \\(M\\) for the trick to work? What happens with an adder into an \\((m+1)\\)-bit register that does not wrap?"}, {"title": "3.4.3 Precision and workspace restoration", "chapter": "Phase Encoding", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#topic-3-4-3", "text": "3.4.3 Precision and workspace restoration § phase error · operator norm Phases computed to finitely many bits are approximate. How much does that phase error matter? Suppose the intended phase oracle multiplies \\(\\ket x\\) by \\(e^{i\\phi(x)}\\), but the implementation uses \\(\\tilde\\phi(x)\\) with \\(|\\phi(x)-\\tilde\\phi(x)|\\le\\delta\\) for every \\(x\\). On a basis state the error is \\[|e^{i\\phi}-e^{i\\tilde\\phi}|=2\\left|\\sin\\frac{\\phi-\\tilde\\phi}{2}\\right|\\le|\\phi-\\tilde\\phi|\\le\\delta.\\] On a normalized superposition \\(\\sum_x\\alpha_x\\ket x\\), the error vector has squared length \\(\\sum_x|\\alpha_x|^2|e^{i\\phi(x)}-e^{i\\tilde\\phi(x)}|^2\\le\\delta^2\\). So the state error is at most \\(\\delta\\), for every input state. The largest such error over all normalized inputs is the operator norm of the difference of the two operations, here at most \\(\\delta\\). Rounding \\(\\phi\\) to the nearest multiple of \\(2\\pi/M\\) gives \\(\\delta\\le\\pi/M\\), so \\(m=\\log_2M\\) bits give error \\(O(2^{-m})\\). Errors add when operations are composed. If two operations have errors \\(\\delta_1\\) and \\(\\delta_2\\) in operator norm , their product has error at most \\(\\delta_1+\\delta_2\\): unitary operations preserve lengths, so an error introduced early is not amplified later. An algorithm using \\(q\\) approximate phase oracles therefore has total error at most \\(q\\delta\\). To keep the total below \\(\\epsilon\\), each oracle needs \\(\\delta\\le\\epsilon/q\\), which costs only \\(\\log_2(q/\\epsilon)\\) bits of phase precision. Precision is not the only failure mode. Every construction in this section computes an intermediate value and must uncompute it; a forgotten uncomputation leaves a record, and the phases become useless no matter how precise they are (Problem 3 of Section 3.4.1). With clean phase oracles of known precision in hand, we can ask what structured phase patterns reveal. Chapter 4 shows that patterns \\(e^{2\\pi ixy/N}\\) are read by the quantum Fourier transform, which turns periodicity into a measurable frequency. Problems An algorithm makes \\(1000\\) phase-oracle calls and needs total error at most \\(10^{-3}\\). How many bits of phase precision suffice per call? Show that the product of two operations with errors \\(\\delta_1\\), \\(\\delta_2\\) has error at most \\(\\delta_1+\\delta_2\\). Write the approximations as \\(\\tilde A=A+E\\), \\(\\tilde B=B+F\\) with \\(A,B,\\tilde A\\) unitary. If phase errors are random and independent across calls rather than worst-case, the total error often grows like \\(\\sqrt q\\delta\\) instead of \\(q\\delta\\). Why should a resource estimate still usually state the worst-case bound?"}, {"title": "Phase Encoding — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#exercise-1", "text": "For n = 4 and s = 0110, give the sign of the |x⟩ term after the Bernstein–Vazirani query for x = 0101 and x = 0011. For x = 0101, s and x share a 1 only in the second position, so s·x = 1 and the sign is −. For x = 0011, they share a 1 only in the third position, so the sign is also −."}, {"title": "Phase Encoding — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#exercise-2", "text": "The target of a Hadamard test is |1⟩, an eigenstate of T=diag(1,e^(iπ/4)). Compute Pr[0] when the controlled operation is controlled-T, and again when it is controlled-T⁴. Which of the two runs tells you more about the phase, and why? Controlled-T kicks back φ=π/4, so Pr[0]=(1+cosπ/4)/2≈0.854. Controlled-T⁴=controlled-Z kicks back 4φ=π, so Pr[0]=0: the outcome is 1 with certainty. The second run is deterministic, so a single shot settles a question about the phase (that 4φ is an odd multiple of π), while the first run is a biased coin that needs many repetitions to estimate. Powers of the unitary multiply the phase, and choosing powers so that the outcome becomes nearly certain is the idea behind phase estimation."}, {"title": "Phase Encoding — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#exercise-3", "text": "After a phase query on the uniform superposition of three qubits, the signs on x=000,001,dots,111 are +,+,−,−,+,+,−,−. What does H^(⊗3) produce, and why is the outcome certain? Writing x=x₁x₂x₃, the sign is − exactly when the middle bit x₂=1, so the pattern is (−1)^(s· x) with s=010. That is a row of the Hadamard table, and since H^(⊗3) is its own inverse it maps the state exactly to |010⟩. The outcome is certain because the sign pattern is linear; this is a Bernstein–Vazirani oracle with hidden string 010."}, {"title": "Phase Encoding — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#exercise-4", "text": "In Simon’s algorithm with n = 3, the samples are 011 and 101. Find s, and say whether a third sample is needed. The equations give s₂ = s₃ and s₁ = s₃, so s ∈ {000, 111}; the nonzero solution is s = 111. The two samples are independent and n − 1 = 2, so no third sample is needed. Check: 111·011 = 0 and 111·101 = 0."}, {"title": "Phase Encoding — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#exercise-5", "text": "Run the Bernstein–Vazirani circuit on a function that is not linear: f(x₁x₂)=x₁∧ x₂. Compute the amplitude of each outcome z using 1/4Σ_x(−1)^(f(x)+x· z). What can the measurement tell you about f? Since (−1)^(f(x))=1−2[x=11], the amplitude is 1/4(Σ_x(−1)^(x· z)−2(−1)^(z₁⊕ z₂))=[z=00]−1/2(−1)^(z₁⊕ z₂). This gives 1/2,1/2,1/2,−1/2 for z=00,01,10,11. Every outcome has probability 1/4: the measurement is a uniformly random string. The sign pattern +,+,+,− is equally far from all four linear patterns, overlapping each with magnitude 1/2, so no outcome is favored. The Hadamard readout is perfect only for linear phase patterns; for other functions it samples the overlaps with linear patterns, and here they are all equal."}, {"title": "Phase Encoding — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#exercise-6", "text": "In Simon’s algorithm with n=3, each run returns a uniformly random string from the four strings orthogonal to s, including 000. Compute the expected number of runs needed to collect two linearly independent samples. Compare with the bound n+1 from the text. The first stage waits for a nonzero sample, which appears with probability 3/4, so it takes 4/3 runs on average. Once a nonzero z₁ is held, a new sample adds information unless it lies in {000,z₁}, which has probability 2/4; the expected wait is 2 runs. The total expectation is 4/3+2=10/3≈3.3 runs, below n+1=4. Each run uses one query, so about 3.3 queries on average, plus Gaussian elimination on two equations. For larger n the same staging gives Σ_(k=0)ⁿ⁻²1/(1−2ᵏ⁻ⁿ⁺¹), which stays below n+1."}, {"title": "Phase Encoding — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#exercise-7", "text": "For M=8, write the phase-gradient state |chi⟩=1/√8Σ_ye^(−2π iy/8)|y⟩ and verify that adding v=3 modulo 8 multiplies it by e^(3π i/4). Then suppose a library prepares |chi'⟩=1/√8Σ_ye^(+2π iy/8)|y⟩ instead. What phase does adding v produce, and how can you obtain the intended phase using the same state? After adding 3, the amplitude on |z⟩ is the old amplitude on |z−3⟩, namely e^(−2π i(z−3)/8)/√8=e^(6π i/8)e^(−2π iz/8)/√8. So the state is e^(3π i/4)|chi⟩, as claimed; wrap-around is harmless because the phases repeat with period 8. With |chi'⟩ the same substitution gives the factor e^(−2π iv/8): the phase has the opposite sign. Running the adder backwards (subtracting v) then gives e^(+2π iv/8). Sign conventions of Fourier states must be checked, because a reversed phase passes every test that only looks at probabilities of a single query."}, {"title": "Phase Encoding — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student writes: “Phase kickback never changes the target, so any target state will do.” Test the claim with controlled-Z, control in |+⟩, and target |+⟩. Compute the joint state and the Hadamard-test probability Pr[0], and check it against 1/2(1+Re⟨ψ| U|ψ⟩). The controlled-Z gives 1/√2(|0⟩|+⟩+|1⟩|−⟩). The target is now entangled with the control and holds a perfect record of the control branch, because |+⟩ and |−⟩ are orthogonal. After the Hadamard on the control, both outcomes have probability 1/2. The formula agrees: ⟨+|Z|+⟩=0, so Pr[0]=1/2. The claim fails because kickback leaves the target unchanged only when the target is an eigenstate. Here |+⟩ is a superposition of the eigenstates |0⟩ and |1⟩, with eigenvalues +1 and −1, and the two phases land on different target components."}, {"title": "Phase Encoding — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#exercise-9", "text": "The Hadamard test is run on an eigenstate with unknown phase φ, estimating cos φ to within ε. Show that the resulting uncertainty in φ is largest near φ = 0 and φ = π, and explain why using controlled-U² does not remove the ambiguity between φ and −φ. Since d(cos φ)/dφ = −sin φ, an error ε in cos φ gives an error of about ε/|sin φ| in φ, which blows up near φ = 0 and π, where the cosine is flat. Using U² kicks back 2φ and measures cos 2φ, which is still an even function of φ, so φ and −φ remain indistinguishable; only the sin φ test (with S†) resolves the sign. Phase estimation in Chapter 4 avoids both problems by reading phases with a Fourier transform."}, {"title": "Phase Encoding — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-phase-encoding.html#exercise-10", "text": "In Simon’s algorithm the samples are uniform over the (n−1)-dimensional space s^(perp) of strings orthogonal to s. Show that n−1+t samples fail to span s^(perp) with probability less than 2^(−t). Use the fact that samples fail to span exactly when they all lie in some subspace of s^(perp) of one lower dimension, and that s^(perp) has 2ⁿ⁻¹−1 such subspaces. Fix a subspace W of s^(perp) of dimension n−2. It contains half of s^(perp), so a uniform sample lies in W with probability 1/2, and n−1+t independent samples all lie in W with probability 2^(−(n−1+t)). If the samples fail to span, they lie in at least one such W. There are 2ⁿ⁻¹−1 choices, so by the union bound Pr[fail]≤(2ⁿ⁻¹−1)2^(−(n−1+t))<2^(−t). With t=log₂(1/delta) extra runs, failure is below delta, giving the n+O(log(1/delta)) query bound in the text. The bound is independent of which s is hidden."}, {"title": "4.1.1 Roots of unity and cancellation", "chapter": "Fourier Methods", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#topic-4-1-1", "text": "4.1.1 Roots of unity and cancellation § root of unity · quantum Fourier transform Let \\(N\\) be a positive integer and \\(\\omega=e^{2\\pi i/N}\\). Its powers \\(1,\\omega,\\omega^2,\\ldots,\\omega^{N-1}\\) are \\(N\\) equally spaced points on the unit circle, the \\(N\\)th roots of unity ; \\(\\omega^N=1\\). The fact that makes them useful is that they cancel. For an integer \\(k\\) not divisible by \\(N\\), \\[\\sum_{x=0}^{N-1}\\omega^{kx}=0.\\] Here is a proof that needs no geometry. Call the sum \\(T\\). Multiplying by \\(\\omega^k\\) shifts every term to the next one, and the last term \\(\\omega^{k(N-1)}\\) becomes \\(\\omega^{kN}=1\\), the first. So \\(\\omega^kT=T\\). Since \\(\\omega^k\\neq1\\), \\(T=0\\). Geometrically: \\(N\\) arrows equally spaced around a circle (or wound around it several times) sum to nothing. When \\(N\\) divides \\(k\\), every term is \\(1\\) and the sum is \\(N\\). Define the quantum Fourier transform on an \\(N\\)-dimensional register by \\[F_N\\ket x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt N}\\sum_{y=0}^{N-1}\\omega^{xy}\\ket y.\\] Is it unitary? The inner product of the images of \\(\\ket x\\) and \\(\\ket{x'}\\) is \\[\\frac1N\\sum_y\\omega^{-xy}\\omega^{x'y}=\\frac1N\\sum_y\\omega^{(x'-x)y}\\], which is \\(1\\) if \\(x=x'\\) and \\(0\\) otherwise by the cancellation identity. So \\(F_N\\) maps an orthonormal basis to an orthonormal basis: it is unitary. Its inverse is \\(F_N^\\dagger\\ket y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt N}\\sum_x\\omega^{-xy}\\ket x\\). For \\(N=2\\), \\(\\omega=-1\\) and \\(F_2=H\\). For \\(N=2^n\\), \\(F_N\\) is not \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\): the Hadamard transform uses signs \\((-1)^{x\\cdot z}\\) with the bitwise product, while \\(F_N\\) uses \\(\\omega^{xy}\\) with ordinary multiplication of integers. The two are Fourier transforms over different groups, bit strings under XOR and integers under addition modulo \\(N\\). Problems Write \\(F_4\\) as a matrix with rows indexed by \\(y\\) and columns by \\(x\\). Check directly that \\(1+\\omega^2+\\omega^4+\\omega^6=0\\) for \\(N=8\\), and that \\(1+\\omega^4+\\omega^8+\\omega^{12}=0\\). Show that \\(F_N^2\\ket x=\\ket{-x\\bmod N}\\). What is \\(F_N^4\\)?"}, {"title": "4.1.2 Periodic states and frequency peaks", "chapter": "Fourier Methods", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#topic-4-1-2", "text": "4.1.2 Periodic states and frequency peaks § period · frequency What does \\(F_N\\) do to a state whose amplitudes repeat? Take \\(N=8\\) and the state supported on every second position, \\[\\ket{\\psi_0}=\\tfrac12(\\ket0+\\ket2+\\ket4+\\ket6).\\] Predict first. Before calculating: how many outcomes of nonzero probability do you expect after \\(F_8\\), and where? The amplitude of \\(\\ket y\\) is \\(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt8}\\sum_{k=0}^{3}\\omega^{2ky}\\). With \\(\\omega^2=e^{2\\pi i/4}\\), this is a sum of fourth roots of unity raised to the power \\(y\\): it is \\(4\\) when \\(4\\mid y\\) and \\(0\\) otherwise. So \\[F_8\\ket{\\psi_0}=\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0+\\ket4).\\] Now shift the pattern by one: \\(\\ket{\\psi_1}=\\tfrac12(\\ket1+\\ket3+\\ket5+\\ket7)\\). Every term gains an extra factor \\(\\omega^{y}\\), so \\(F_8\\ket{\\psi_1}=\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0-\\ket4)\\). The probabilities are unchanged. The Fourier transform converts a shift into a phase, exactly as the Hadamard transform did in Simon’s algorithm, and a phase is invisible to measurement. In general, if \\(r\\) divides \\(N\\) and a state is uniform on \\(x_0,x_0+r,x_0+2r,\\ldots\\), its Fourier transform is uniform over the \\(r\\) multiples of \\(N/r\\), the frequencies \\(0,N/r,2N/r,\\ldots\\) (each frequency counts how many times the pattern repeats across the register, in units of \\(1/r\\)), with phases depending on \\(x_0\\). Measuring gives a random multiple \\(kN/r\\), from which \\(N/r\\), and hence \\(r\\), can be inferred once a few samples are combined. When \\(r\\) does not divide \\(N\\), the peaks are no longer exact: they spread over nearby integers, and Section 4.3 handles that case. Problems For \\(N=8\\), compute \\(F_8\\) of \\(\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0+\\ket4)\\). For \\(N=12\\) and a state uniform on \\(\\{1,4,7,10\\}\\) (period 3), which outcomes occur after \\(F_{12}\\)? A student measures one outcome, \\(4\\), in the \\(N=12\\) example. Can they conclude \\(r=3\\)? What about the outcome \\(0\\)?"}, {"title": "4.1.3 Circuit construction", "chapter": "Fourier Methods", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#topic-4-1-3", "text": "4.1.3 Circuit construction § controlled phase gate · product representation A unitary on \\(n=\\log_2N\\) qubits is a \\(2^n\\times2^n\\) matrix; a general one needs exponentially many gates. The Fourier transform is special. Write \\(x=\\sum_jx_j2^j\\) and \\(y=\\sum_ky_k2^k\\). The phase \\(\\omega^{xy}=e^{2\\pi ixy/2^n}\\) depends on \\(xy/2^n\\) only modulo one, and \\[\\frac{xy}{2^n}=\\sum_k y_k\\,\\frac{x\\,2^k}{2^n}\\equiv\\sum_ky_k\\,(0.x_{n-k-1}\\cdots x_1x_0)\\pmod 1,\\] because multiplying \\(x\\) by \\(2^k\\) shifts its binary point, and the integer part contributes nothing. Each output bit \\(y_k\\) receives a phase determined by a binary fraction of the input bits. The sum over \\(y\\) therefore factors into a product over output bits: \\[F_N\\ket x=\\bigotimes_{k=n-1}^{0}\\frac{\\ket0+e^{2\\pi i\\,(0.x_{n-k-1}\\cdots x_0)}\\ket1}{\\sqrt2},\\] with the highest output bit written first. This product representation says the output is unentangled for every basis input, and each factor can be built separately. The highest output bit gets \\(e^{2\\pi i\\,0.x_0}=(-1)^{x_0}\\), a Hadamard on \\(x_0\\). The lowest gets the full fraction \\(0.x_{n-1}\\cdots x_0\\). The circuit builds the factors in place. Apply \\(H\\) to the top input bit \\(x_{n-1}\\), giving phase \\(e^{2\\pi i\\,0.x_{n-1}}\\); then controlled phase gates \\(P(\\pi/2)\\) from \\(x_{n-2}\\), \\(P(\\pi/4)\\) from \\(x_{n-3}\\), and so on, add \\(0.0x_{n-2}\\), \\(0.00x_{n-3}\\), …, completing the factor for \\(y_0\\). Repeat on the next wire with one fewer rotation. The factors end in reversed order, so swaps restore the bit order. The circuit uses \\(n\\) Hadamards, \\(n(n-1)/2\\) controlled phase gates, and \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) swaps: \\(O(n^2)\\) gates for a transform on a space of dimension \\(2^n\\). The inverse \\(F_N^\\dagger\\) is the same circuit run backwards with negated angles. The construction, and its approximate version below, are due to Coppersmith (1994) . Problems Use the product representation to write \\(F_4\\ket{2}\\) and check it against the matrix of Section 4.1.1. How many controlled phase gates does the \\(n=10\\) QFT use, and what is the smallest angle? The QFT acts on \\(2^n\\) amplitudes with \\(O(n^2)\\) gates, while the classical fast Fourier transform of a list of \\(2^n\\) numbers takes \\(O(n2^n)\\) operations. Why is this not an exponential speedup for computing Fourier transforms of data?"}, {"title": "4.1.4 Approximation and cost", "chapter": "Fourier Methods", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#topic-4-1-4", "text": "4.1.4 Approximation and cost § approximate QFT The smallest rotations in the QFT have angles \\(2\\pi/2^n\\). For large \\(n\\) they are physically tiny and, in fault-tolerant gate sets, expensive to synthesize. Do we need them? A controlled phase \\(\\mathrm{C}P(\\theta)\\) differs from the identity only on \\(\\ket{11}\\), by \\(e^{i\\theta}-1\\), whose magnitude is at most \\(|\\theta|\\). So omitting it changes the operation by at most \\(|\\theta|\\) in operator norm . By the composition bound of Section 3.4.3, omitting several gates changes the whole circuit by at most the sum of their angles. If we drop every rotation with angle below \\(2\\pi/2^b\\), each wire loses rotations \\(2\\pi/2^{b+1},2\\pi/2^{b+2},\\ldots\\), totalling less than \\(2\\pi/2^b\\). Over \\(n\\) wires, the approximate QFT has error less than \\(2\\pi n/2^b\\). To reach error \\(\\epsilon\\), choose \\(b\\approx\\log_2(2\\pi n/\\epsilon)\\). Each wire then keeps at most \\(b\\) rotations, so the gate count falls from \\(O(n^2)\\) to \\(O(n\\log(n/\\epsilon))\\). In phase estimation a modest constant error in the QFT only slightly lowers the success probability , so \\(b\\) of order \\(\\log n\\) suffices. Each remaining rotation must still be synthesized, and its synthesis error is added to the total in the same way. When a resource estimate quotes the QFT as “\\(O(n^2)\\) gates”, ask whether it is exact or approximate, and in which gate set. Problems For \\(n=1000\\) and target error \\(\\epsilon=10^{-3}\\), estimate \\(b\\) and the number of controlled rotations in the approximate QFT. Prove \\(|e^{i\\theta}-1|\\le|\\theta|\\). The approximate QFT bound adds errors linearly. Why is that safe even though the omitted gates are not the last ones in the circuit?"}, {"title": "4.2.1 Controlled powers and kickback", "chapter": "Fourier Methods", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#topic-4-2-1", "text": "4.2.1 Controlled powers and kickback § phase estimation · controlled powers Let \\(U\\) be a unitary with eigenstate \\(\\ket u\\) and eigenvalue \\(e^{2\\pi i\\phi}\\), where \\(0\\le\\phi<1\\). Phase estimation is the task of finding \\(\\phi\\). Assume we can prepare \\(\\ket u\\) and apply controlled-\\(U^{2^j}\\) for \\(j=0,\\ldots,m-1\\). Take \\(m\\) control qubits, each in \\(\\ket+\\). Control qubit \\(j\\) (counting the lowest as \\(j=0\\)) controls \\(U^{2^j}\\), which kicks back \\(e^{2\\pi i\\,2^j\\phi}\\) onto its \\(\\ket1\\) branch, by Problem 3 of Section 3.1.1. The target stays \\(\\ket u\\). The control register becomes \\[\\bigotimes_{j=m-1}^{0}\\frac{\\ket0+e^{2\\pi i\\,2^j\\phi}\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2^m}}\\sum_{k=0}^{2^m-1}e^{2\\pi ik\\phi}\\ket k,\\] where the equality expands the product: a basis state \\(\\ket k\\) with bits \\(k_j\\) collects the phase \\(\\prod_je^{2\\pi i\\,k_j2^j\\phi}=e^{2\\pi ik\\phi}\\). This state is the steady phase ramp the Fourier transform was built to read. The controlled powers are the expensive part. Controlled-\\(U^{2^j}\\) built by repeating controlled-\\(U\\) costs \\(2^j\\) uses, so all powers together cost \\(1+2+\\cdots+2^{m-1}=2^m-1\\) uses of controlled-\\(U\\). Each extra bit of precision doubles the cost. For some unitaries, \\(U^{2^j}\\) has a shortcut. Modular multiplication by \\(a\\), raised to the power \\(2^j\\), is multiplication by \\(a^{2^j}\\bmod L\\), which costs the same as multiplication by \\(a\\) once \\(a^{2^j}\\) is precomputed classically. That shortcut is what makes Shor’s algorithm efficient. Hamiltonian evolution \\(e^{-iHt}\\) generally has no such shortcut, which is a central fact of Chapter 9. Problems For \\(m=2\\) and \\(\\phi=1/4\\), write the control register after the controlled powers. How many uses of controlled-\\(U\\) does phase estimation with \\(m=20\\) need if powers are made by repetition? Why must the eigenstate \\(\\ket u\\) be the same for every controlled power, and what happens if each power is applied to a fresh copy of \\(\\ket u\\) instead?"}, {"title": "4.2.2 Exact phases", "chapter": "Fourier Methods", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#topic-4-2-2", "text": "4.2.2 Exact phases § inverse quantum Fourier transform Compare the control register with the Fourier transform of a basis state. With \\(M=2^m\\), \\[F_M\\ket j=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt M}\\sum_ke^{2\\pi ijk/M}\\ket k.\\] If \\(\\phi=j/M\\) exactly, for an integer \\(j\\), the control register is \\(F_M\\ket j\\). Applying the inverse quantum Fourier transform \\(F_M^\\dagger\\) returns \\(\\ket j\\), and measuring gives \\(j\\) with certainty. In binary, \\(\\phi=0.j_{m-1}\\cdots j_0\\): phase estimation reads the first \\(m\\) binary digits of the phase. Here is an \\(m=3\\) example. Let \\(\\phi=3/8=0.011_2\\). The controls receive phases \\(e^{2\\pi i\\cdot3/8}\\) (lowest, from \\(U\\)), \\(e^{2\\pi i\\cdot6/8}\\) (from \\(U^2\\)), and \\(e^{2\\pi i\\cdot12/8}=e^{2\\pi i\\cdot4/8}=-1\\) (highest, from \\(U^4\\)). The register is \\(F_8\\ket3\\), and \\(F_8^\\dagger\\) maps it to \\(\\ket{011}\\). The product form explains why the digits come out separately. Control qubit \\(j\\) carries phase \\(e^{2\\pi i\\,2^j\\phi}\\), and multiplying \\(\\phi\\) by \\(2^j\\) shifts its binary point, so modulo one this phase depends only on the digits of \\(\\phi\\) from position \\(j+1\\) onward. The highest control, with \\(U^{2^{m-1}}\\), carries \\(e^{2\\pi i\\,0.j_0}=\\pm1\\): it holds the last digit as a sign, readable by a single Hadamard. The others need the controlled phase corrections inside \\(F_M^\\dagger\\), which subtract the contributions of digits already known. Chapter 11 rebuilds phase estimation with one control qubit by applying those corrections classically, one digit at a time. Problems For \\(m=2\\) and \\(\\phi=1/4\\), apply \\(F_4^\\dagger\\) to the register from Problem 1 of Section 4.2.1. For \\(m=3\\), which outcomes are possible when \\(\\phi=1/2\\)? When \\(\\phi=0\\)? A student applies \\(F_M\\) instead of \\(F_M^\\dagger\\) at the end. What is measured for \\(\\phi=3/8\\), \\(m=3\\)?"}, {"title": "4.2.3 Resolution and success probability", "chapter": "Fourier Methods", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#topic-4-2-3", "text": "4.2.3 Resolution and success probability § phase resolution Most phases are not exact multiples of \\(1/M\\). Then the inverse transform cannot concentrate all amplitude on one outcome. How much lands near the right answer? The amplitude of outcome \\(j\\) is the overlap of the register with \\(F_M\\ket j\\): \\[a_j=\\frac1M\\sum_{k=0}^{M-1}e^{2\\pi ik(\\phi-j/M)}.\\] Write \\(\\Delta=\\phi-j/M\\). This is a geometric series with ratio \\(e^{2\\pi i\\Delta}\\), so \\[|a_j|^2=\\frac{1}{M^2}\\left|\\frac{1-e^{2\\pi iM\\Delta}}{1-e^{2\\pi i\\Delta}}\\right|^2=\\frac{\\sin^2(\\pi M\\Delta)}{M^2\\sin^2(\\pi\\Delta)}.\\] For the nearest grid point, \\(|\\Delta|\\le\\frac{1}{2M}\\). Using \\(|\\sin(\\pi M\\Delta)|\\ge2M|\\Delta|\\) (since \\(\\sin u\\ge2u/\\pi\\) on \\([0,\\pi/2]\\)) and \\(|\\sin\\pi\\Delta|\\le\\pi|\\Delta|\\), we get \\(|a_j|^2\\ge4/\\pi^2\\approx0.405\\). The distribution is peaked around \\(M\\phi\\), and falls off roughly as \\(1/(M\\Delta)^2\\). For \\(M=8\\) and \\(\\phi=0.3\\), so that \\(M\\phi=2.4\\), the outcomes \\(2\\) and \\(3\\) have probabilities about \\(0.58\\) and \\(0.26\\); together \\(0.84\\). The phase resolution is \\(1/M\\): outcomes within one grid spacing are likely, and outcomes \\(e\\) spacings away have probability falling like \\(1/e^2\\). A standard bound says that to obtain \\(\\phi\\) to within \\(2^{-t}\\) with probability at least \\(1-\\delta\\), it suffices to use \\(m=t+\\lceil\\log_2(2+1/(2\\delta))\\rceil\\) control qubits. Extra control qubits buy confidence; repetition with a median also works. In terms of cost: precision \\(\\epsilon\\) needs \\(M\\approx1/\\epsilon\\), hence about \\(1/\\epsilon\\) uses of controlled-\\(U\\). Compare the Hadamard test , whose \\(1/\\epsilon^2\\) runs each used \\(U\\) once. This quadratic improvement, \\(1/\\epsilon\\) instead of \\(1/\\epsilon^2\\), comes from coherence across the controlled powers, and it is the same improvement amplitude estimation achieves in Chapter 5. Problems For \\(M=4\\) and \\(\\phi=1/8\\), compute the probabilities of outcomes \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). How many control qubits give \\(\\phi\\) to within \\(2^{-10}\\) with probability at least \\(0.99\\), by the bound above? Why does the nearest-grid-point bound \\(4/\\pi^2\\) not tell us the probability of an answer within \\(1/M\\) of \\(\\phi\\)? Give a better lower bound for that event."}, {"title": "4.2.4 Superpositions of eigenstates", "chapter": "Fourier Methods", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#topic-4-2-4", "text": "4.2.4 Superpositions of eigenstates § eigenbasis expansion What if the target is not an eigenstate? Any state can be written in terms of the eigenstates of \\(U\\): \\(\\ket\\psi=\\sum_sc_s\\ket{u_s}\\), with \\(U\\ket{u_s}=e^{2\\pi i\\phi_s}\\ket{u_s}\\) and \\(\\sum_s|c_s|^2=1\\). (For a unitary, eigenstates with distinct eigenvalues are orthogonal and can be chosen to form a basis.) This eigenbasis expansion is the key: phase estimation is linear, so it acts on each term separately: \\[\\ket{0^m}\\ket\\psi\\longmapsto\\sum_sc_s\\,\\ket{\\tilde\\phi_s}\\ket{u_s},\\] where \\(\\ket{\\tilde\\phi_s}\\) is the control register that phase \\(\\phi_s\\) would produce. Measuring the controls returns an estimate of one \\(\\phi_s\\), chosen with probability \\(|c_s|^2\\), and leaves the target close to \\(\\ket{u_s}\\) (exactly, if the phases are distinct grid points). Two consequences shape later chapters. First, phase estimation samples eigenvalues; it does not list them. To find the smallest eigenvalue of a Hamiltonian (Chapter 9), we need an initial state with substantial overlap \\(|c_0|^2\\) with the ground state , and the expected number of repetitions is about \\(1/|c_0|^2\\). Preparing such a state can be the hard part. Second, phase estimation followed by measurement prepares an eigenstate. This turns it into a filter, which Chapter 8 refines into polynomial transformations that act on all eigenvalues coherently without measuring. Order finding , next, exploits the first consequence in an unusual way: there, the easy-to-prepare state \\(\\ket1\\) is an equal superposition of eigenstates, and the random eigenphase it yields is exactly what we need. Problems \\(U=Z\\) and \\(\\ket\\psi=\\cos\\theta\\ket0+\\sin\\theta\\ket1\\). With one control qubit, what outcomes occur and with what probabilities? If the ground state has overlap \\(|c_0|^2=0.01\\) with the initial state, how many runs of phase estimation are expected before the ground energy appears? Is the post-measurement target always an exact eigenstate? Consider two eigenphases that round to the same outcome."}, {"title": "4.3.1 Modular multiplication as a unitary", "chapter": "Fourier Methods", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#topic-4-3-1", "text": "4.3.1 Modular multiplication as a unitary § order · modular exponentiation Let \\(L\\) have \\(n\\) bits and \\(\\gcd(a,L)=1\\). On an \\(n\\)-qubit register define \\[U_a\\ket y=\\ket{ay\\bmod L}\\quad(0\\le y<L),\\qquad U_a\\ket y=\\ket y\\quad(L\\le y<2^n).\\] Because \\(a\\) has an inverse modulo \\(L\\), multiplication by \\(a\\) permutes the residues \\(0,\\ldots,L-1\\), so \\(U_a\\) is a permutation and hence unitary. It can be built reversibly from modular addition (Section 2.1.3), using \\(O(n^2)\\) gates with schoolbook arithmetic. The order \\(r\\) of \\(a\\) is visible in the orbit of \\(1\\): \\(1\\to a\\to a^2\\to\\cdots\\to a^{r-1}\\to1\\). Take \\(L=15\\), \\(a=7\\): \\[1\\to7\\to49\\bmod15=4\\to28\\bmod15=13\\to91\\bmod15=1,\\] so \\(r=4\\). Classically, finding \\(r\\) by stepping along the orbit takes up to \\(r\\) steps, and \\(r\\) can be nearly as large as \\(L\\), exponential in \\(n\\). For phase estimation we need controlled-\\(U_a^{2^j}\\). Since \\(U_a^{2^j}=U_{a^{2^j}\\bmod L}\\), compute \\(a^{2^j}\\bmod L\\) classically by repeated squaring, \\(m\\) squarings in all, and build a controlled multiplier by that constant. All the controlled powers together form modular exponentiation , \\(\\ket k\\ket y\\mapsto\\ket k\\ket{a^ky\\bmod L}\\), which costs \\(m\\) controlled multiplications, \\(O(mn^2)\\) gates. This avoids the exponential cost of repetition noted in Section 4.2.1. Problems Find the order of \\(2\\) modulo \\(21\\) by listing powers. Why must \\(\\gcd(a,L)=1\\) for \\(U_a\\) to be unitary? Give a counterexample with \\(L=15\\). Compute \\(7^{2^j}\\bmod15\\) for \\(j=0,1,2\\) by repeated squaring, and say which multipliers the phase estimation circuit with \\(m=3\\) uses."}, {"title": "4.3.2 Eigenstates and the sample s/r", "chapter": "Fourier Methods", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#topic-4-3-2", "text": "4.3.2 Eigenstates and the sample s/r § order finding What are the eigenstates of \\(U_a\\)? Since \\(U_a\\) cycles \\(1\\to a\\to\\cdots\\to a^{r-1}\\to1\\), it acts on the orbit like a shift on \\(r\\) positions, and Section 4.1.2 says shifts are diagonalized by Fourier states. For \\(s=0,1,\\ldots,r-1\\), define \\[\\ket{u_s}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt r}\\sum_{k=0}^{r-1}e^{-2\\pi isk/r}\\ket{a^k\\bmod L}.\\] Applying \\(U_a\\) shifts \\(k\\) to \\(k+1\\), and the substitution used in Section 3.4.2 gives \\(U_a\\ket{u_s}=e^{2\\pi is/r}\\ket{u_s}\\). The eigenphases are \\(s/r\\): the order is in the denominator. We cannot prepare \\(\\ket{u_s}\\) without knowing \\(r\\). But we do not need to. Sum over \\(s\\): \\[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt r}\\sum_{s=0}^{r-1}\\ket{u_s}=\\frac1r\\sum_k\\Big(\\sum_se^{-2\\pi isk/r}\\Big)\\ket{a^k}=\\ket1,\\] because the inner sum is \\(r\\) for \\(k=0\\) and \\(0\\) otherwise. The state \\(\\ket1\\), trivial to prepare, is an equal superposition of all \\(r\\) eigenstates. This is the heart of order finding: by Section 4.2.4, phase estimation on \\(\\ket1\\) returns an estimate of \\(s/r\\) for a uniformly random \\(s\\in\\{0,\\ldots,r-1\\}\\). For \\(L=15\\), \\(a=7\\), \\(r=4\\), and \\(m=3\\) control qubits, the phases \\(s/4\\in\\{0,\\frac14,\\frac12,\\frac34\\}\\) are exact grid points \\(j/8\\) with \\(j\\in\\{0,2,4,6\\}\\). Each outcome occurs with probability \\(\\frac14\\). The outcome \\(6\\) gives \\(6/8=3/4\\), so \\(r=4\\). The outcome \\(4\\) gives \\(1/2\\), which suggests \\(r=2\\); checking \\(7^2\\bmod15=4\\neq1\\) rejects it, and another run is needed. The outcome \\(0\\) gives nothing. Problems Verify \\(U_a\\ket{u_1}=e^{2\\pi i/4}\\ket{u_1}\\) for \\(L=15\\), \\(a=7\\). With \\(r=6\\) and \\(s\\) uniform, what is the probability that \\(\\gcd(s,6)=1\\), so that the reduced fraction \\(s/r\\) has denominator exactly \\(6\\)? Why does it not matter that phase estimation is run on \\(\\ket1\\) rather than on a single eigenstate, given that the answer we want is \\(r\\), not a particular \\(s\\)?"}, {"title": "4.3.3 Continued fractions", "chapter": "Fourier Methods", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#topic-4-3-3", "text": "4.3.3 Continued fractions § continued fraction · convergent When \\(r\\) does not divide \\(2^m\\), phase estimation returns an integer \\(j\\) with \\(j/2^m\\approx s/r\\). How do we recover \\(r\\) from an approximation? The key is that \\(s/r\\) is a fraction with a small denominator, \\(r<L\\), and the approximation is very good. If \\(|j/2^m-s/r|<\\frac{1}{2r^2}\\), then \\(s/r\\) is one of the convergents of the continued fraction of \\(j/2^m\\), and the convergents can be computed by Euclid’s algorithm. Choosing \\(2^m\\ge L^2\\), about \\(m=2n\\), guarantees this precision whenever phase estimation succeeds. A continued fraction writes a number as \\(a_0+1/(a_1+1/(a_2+\\cdots))\\), and the convergents are the fractions obtained by stopping early. Here is a worked case. Take \\(L=21\\), \\(a=2\\), so \\(r=6\\) (Problem 1 of Section 4.3.1), and \\(m=9\\), \\(2^m=512\\ge21^2\\). Suppose \\(s=1\\); then \\(512/6\\approx85.3\\) and the most likely outcome is \\(j=85\\). Run Euclid on \\(85/512\\): \\[512=6\\cdot85+2,\\qquad85=42\\cdot2+1,\\qquad2=2\\cdot1.\\] So \\(85/512=[0;6,42,2]\\), with convergents \\(0\\), \\(\\tfrac16\\), \\(\\tfrac{42}{253}\\), \\(\\tfrac{85}{512}\\). The last convergent with denominator below \\(L=21\\) is \\(1/6\\). Candidate \\(r=6\\); check \\(2^6=64\\equiv1\\pmod{21}\\). Confirmed. The overall order-finding procedure is then: run phase estimation on \\(\\ket1\\) with \\(m\\approx2n+1\\); compute the continued-fraction convergents of \\(j/2^m\\); for each denominator \\(q<L\\), test whether \\(a^q\\equiv1\\); combine denominators from a few runs by least common multiples if needed. A single run succeeds with probability at least of order \\(1/\\log\\log L\\) in the worst case (the chance that \\(s\\) and \\(r\\) share no factor), and usually far more often; a constant number of repetitions, plus classical checks, gives high confidence. The quantum part does one thing: it produces a sample of \\(s/r\\) to high precision. Everything else is classical number theory. Problems Phase estimation with \\(m=4\\) returns \\(j=13\\), and the order is known to satisfy \\(r\\le5\\). Use continued fractions to find \\(s/r\\). In the \\(L=21\\) example, suppose the run returns \\(j=171\\) (from \\(s=2\\)). What does the continued fraction give, and what should be done? Why is \\(m\\approx2n\\) needed rather than \\(m\\approx n\\)?"}, {"title": "4.4.1 From order to factors", "chapter": "Fourier Methods", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#topic-4-4-1", "text": "4.4.1 From order to factors § Shor’s algorithm · factoring reduction Suppose \\(L\\) is odd, not a prime power, and we want a nontrivial factor. Pick \\(a\\) at random in \\(\\{2,\\ldots,L-1\\}\\). If \\(\\gcd(a,L)>1\\), we have a factor already. Otherwise find the order \\(r\\) of \\(a\\). If \\(r\\) is even, then \\[a^r-1=(a^{r/2}-1)(a^{r/2}+1)\\equiv0\\pmod L.\\] So \\(L\\) divides the product. It does not divide \\(a^{r/2}-1\\), since \\(r\\) is the smallest exponent with \\(a^r\\equiv1\\). If also \\(a^{r/2}\\not\\equiv-1\\pmod L\\), then \\(L\\) does not divide \\(a^{r/2}+1\\) either, and the prime factors of \\(L\\) must be split between the two factors. Then \\(\\gcd(a^{r/2}-1,L)\\) is a nontrivial factor, computed by Euclid’s algorithm. For \\(L=15\\), \\(a=7\\), \\(r=4\\): \\(7^2=49\\equiv4\\), and \\(\\gcd(3,15)=3\\), \\(\\gcd(5,15)=5\\). For \\(L=21\\), \\(a=2\\), \\(r=6\\): \\(2^3=8\\), \\(\\gcd(7,21)=7\\), \\(\\gcd(9,21)=3\\). This factoring reduction can fail: \\(r\\) may be odd, or \\(a^{r/2}\\equiv-1\\). For \\(L=15\\), \\(a=14\\) has \\(r=2\\) and \\(14\\equiv-1\\), so the gcds are trivial. Number theory shows that for odd \\(L\\) with at least two distinct prime factors, a random coprime \\(a\\) succeeds with probability at least \\(1/2\\). So a few random choices suffice. This is Shor’s algorithm (1994/1997) . Prime powers \\(L=p^k\\) are detected and factored classically. Problems Factor \\(L=35\\) with \\(a=2\\), given that the order is \\(r=12\\). For \\(L=15\\), which \\(a\\in\\{2,4,7,8,11,13,14\\}\\) lead to failure of the reduction? Why can the reduction not be used for prime \\(L\\), and how is that case handled?"}, {"title": "4.4.2 Resources", "chapter": "Fourier Methods", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#topic-4-4-2", "text": "4.4.2 Resources § resource estimate Where does the cost of Shor’s algorithm go? For an \\(n\\)-bit \\(L\\): Modular exponentiation dominates. With schoolbook arithmetic it is \\(2n\\) controlled modular multiplications, each \\(O(n^2)\\) gates, so \\(O(n^3)\\) gates in total. Faster multiplication algorithms lower the exponent asymptotically. The inverse QFT on \\(2n\\) qubits costs \\(O(n^2)\\) exactly, or \\(O(n\\log n)\\) approximately. Applied one qubit at a time with measurement and classical feedback (Chapter 11), it needs only one control qubit. Qubits: about \\(2n\\) control qubits (or one, recycled) plus \\(n\\) for the target and \\(O(n)\\) workspace for arithmetic. Classical processing: continued fractions, gcds, and modular checks, all polynomial in \\(n\\). Asymptotically this is polynomial, \\(O(n^3)\\), against the best known classical algorithm, the number field sieve, which runs in time roughly \\(\\exp(c\\,n^{1/3}(\\log n)^{2/3})\\). The speedup is superpolynomial, but it is a comparison with the best known classical method: no proof rules out a fast classical factoring algorithm. Concrete resource estimates are sobering. Factoring a 2048-bit RSA modulus requires billions of logical operations, each protected by error correction. A careful 2019 estimate found about 20 million noisy physical qubits running for about eight hours ( Gidney and Ekerå ); a 2025 estimate by Gidney reduced this to under a million noisy qubits running for under a week, using improved arithmetic and error-correction techniques ( Gidney 2025 ). Such estimates depend on assumed physical error rates and cycle times, and they change as methods improve. Problems Roughly how much does the \\(O(n^3)\\) gate count grow from \\(n=1024\\) to \\(n=2048\\)? Which part of Shor’s algorithm would you optimize first to reduce a resource estimate, and why? “Shor’s algorithm proves that quantum computers are exponentially faster than classical ones.” Correct this statement."}, {"title": "4.4.3 Hidden subgroup problems", "chapter": "Fourier Methods", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#topic-4-4-3", "text": "4.4.3 Hidden subgroup problems § hidden subgroup problem Simon’s problem and order finding share a structure. In each, a function \\(f\\) on a group is constant on the cosets of a hidden subgroup and distinct across cosets. In Simon’s problem the group is \\(\\{0,1\\}^n\\) under XOR and the subgroup is \\(\\{0,s\\}\\). In order finding the group is the integers under addition and the subgroup is the multiples of \\(r\\), since \\(a^x\\) is constant on \\(x_0+r\\mathbb Z\\). The task in the hidden subgroup problem is to find the subgroup. The algorithm is the same in both: evaluate \\(f\\) on a uniform superposition, measure the output to collapse onto a random coset, apply the Fourier transform of the group, and measure. The Fourier transform turns the random coset shift into a phase and leaves a sample that constrains the subgroup. Classical processing of a few samples recovers it. For all finite abelian groups (those where the order of the group operation does not matter) this works efficiently. Discrete logarithms, the basis of elliptic-curve cryptography, are an abelian hidden subgroup problem over \\(\\mathbb Z_N\\times\\mathbb Z_N\\), solved by Shor’s same paper. For nonabelian groups the situation is different. The symmetric group would give graph isomorphism, and the dihedral group is related to certain lattice problems; for these no efficient quantum algorithm is known. Measuring a random coset of a nonabelian subgroup and Fourier sampling yields too little information per sample, even though a polynomial number of queries is known to suffice information-theoretically. The best known dihedral algorithm ( Kuperberg ) is subexponential, not polynomial. Fourier methods resolve periodicity, but they return useful outcomes only with some probability, and estimating a quantity to precision \\(\\epsilon\\) still costs on the order of \\(1/\\epsilon\\) controlled operations. Chapter 5 asks a complementary question: when a useful outcome has small probability, can we make it large? Its answer, reflection, combines with phase estimation into amplitude estimation. Problems Identify the group, the hidden subgroup, and the cosets for order finding with \\(a=7\\), \\(L=15\\), restricted to \\(x\\in\\{0,\\ldots,15\\}\\). In Simon’s problem, what plays the role of the Fourier transform, and what plays the role of continued fractions? Why does an efficient algorithm for the hidden subgroup problem on the symmetric group not follow from the abelian case, even though the same circuit can be written down?"}, {"title": "Fourier Methods — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#exercise-1", "text": "Compute F₈ applied to (|1⟩ + |5⟩)/√2. The amplitude of y is ω^y(1 + (−1)^y)/4, which is ω^y/2 for even y and 0 for odd y. So the output is ½(|0⟩ + ω²|2⟩ + ω⁴|4⟩ + ω⁶|6⟩) = ½(|0⟩ + i|2⟩ − |4⟩ − i|6⟩): period 4 gives frequencies at multiples of 2, with phases set by the offset 1."}, {"title": "Fourier Methods — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#exercise-2", "text": "Use the product representation to write F₈|5⟩ as a product of three one-qubit states, highest output bit first. Check one amplitude against the definition F₈|x⟩=1/√8Σ_yomega^(xy)|y⟩. The three phases are e^(2π i 0.1₂)=−1, e^(2π i 0.01₂)=i, and e^(2π i 0.101₂)=e^(2π i·5/8). So F₈|5⟩=(|0⟩−|1⟩)/√2⊗(|0⟩+i|1⟩)/√2⊗(|0⟩+e^(5π i/4)|1⟩)/√2. Check y=7=111₂: the product gives (−1)(i)e^(5π i/4)/√8=e^(iπ(1+1/2+5/4))/√8=e^(11π i/4)/√8=e^(3π i/4)/√8. The definition gives omega³⁵/√8=e^(2π i·35/8)/√8=e^(2π i·3/8)/√8=e^(3π i/4)/√8. They agree."}, {"title": "Fourier Methods — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#exercise-3", "text": "Take L=15 and a=4. Find the order r, list the phase-estimation outcomes with m=3 control qubits and their probabilities, and say whether the factoring reduction succeeds. The order is r=2. Starting from |1⟩, phase estimation returns s/2 for s∈{0,1}, which are the exact grid points 0/8 and 4/8: outcomes 0 and 4, each with probability 1/2. Outcome 4 gives 1/2, so r=2, confirmed by 4²≡1. Outcome 0 gives nothing and the run is repeated. For the reduction, r is even and 4¹=4≢−1 (mod 15), so gcd(4−1,15)=3 and gcd(4+1,15)=5 are the factors."}, {"title": "Fourier Methods — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#exercise-4", "text": "Phase estimation with m = 4 is applied to an eigenstate with φ = 0.2. Which two outcomes are most likely, and approximately what are their probabilities? For j = 3, Δ = 0.0125: sin²(0.2π)/(256 sin²(0.0125π)) ≈ 0.3455/(256 × 0.001541) ≈ 0.876. For j = 4, Δ = −0.05: sin²(0.8π)/(256 sin²(0.05π)) ≈ 0.3455/(256 × 0.02447) ≈ 0.055. So outcome 3 (estimate 0.1875) has probability about 0.88 and outcome 4 about 0.06."}, {"title": "Fourier Methods — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#exercise-5", "text": "Phase estimation with m=3 is applied to an eigenstate with φ=5/16, exactly halfway between the grid points 2/8 and 3/8. Compute the probabilities of outcomes 2 and 3 from |aⱼ|²=sin²(π MDelta)/(M²sin²πDelta). Compare their sum with the nearest-grid-point bound 4/π², and say what this worst case means for choosing m. For j=2 and j=3, |Delta|=1/16, so sin²(π MDelta)=sin²(π/2)=1 and |aⱼ|²=1/(64sin²(π/16))≈1/(64×0.03806)≈0.411. Each nearest neighbor has probability about 0.41, just above 4/π²≈0.405, and together they have about 0.82. The remaining 0.18 spreads over outcomes farther away (0.051 each for 1 and 4, less beyond). A phase halfway between grid points is the worst case: no single outcome is likely. So the guarantees are stated as “within one grid spacing”, and extra control qubits or repetitions with a median are added to raise the confidence."}, {"title": "Fourier Methods — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#exercise-6", "text": "In order finding with L=21, a=2 (r=6), and m=9, the run with s=5 most likely returns j=427. Expand 427/512 as a continued fraction, list its convergents, and recover r. Euclid’s algorithm gives 427=0·512+427, 512=1·427+85, 427=5·85+2, 85=42·2+1, 2=2·1. So 427/512=[0;1,5,42,2]. The convergents are 0, 1, 5/6, 211/253, 427/512. The last one with denominator below 21 is 5/6, giving candidate r=6; the check 2⁶=64≡1 (mod 21) confirms it. The approximation is close enough: |427/512−5/6|=1/1536<1/(2·36), as the continued-fraction theorem requires."}, {"title": "Fourier Methods — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#exercise-7", "text": "For L=21, apply the factoring reduction with a=4, a=5, and a=8. Find each order classically and say which choices give a factor. For a=4: 4,16,64≡1, so r=3, which is odd. The reduction fails. For a=5: 5,4,20,16,17,1, so r=6, but 5³≡20≡−1, so gcd(5³+1,21)=21 and gcd(5³−1,21)=gcd(124,21)=1, both trivial. It fails. For a=8: 8²=64≡1, so r=2, and 8≢−1. Then gcd(7,21)=7 and gcd(9,21)=3, so it succeeds. Two of three choices fail here, though the theorem only promises success with probability at least 1/2 over a random coprime a. Each failure costs another run of order finding, and the classical checks reveal it."}, {"title": "Fourier Methods — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student runs phase estimation with U=Z on the target |+⟩ and expects to obtain the “average phase” φ=1/4, halfway between the eigenphases 0 and 1/2. Using two control qubits, compute what is actually observed and what the target is left in. Write |+⟩=1/√2(|0⟩+|1⟩), eigenstates of Z with phases 0 and 1/2. Both are exact two-bit grid points, so phase estimation produces 1/√2(|00⟩|0⟩+|10⟩|1⟩). Measuring the controls gives 00 (phase 0) or 10 (phase 1/2), each with probability 1/2, and the target collapses to the matching eigenstate. The outcome 01, phase 1/4, never occurs. Phase estimation samples one eigenphase with probability |c_s|². An average over runs estimates Σ_s|c_s|²φ_s, but no single run reports it, and the average is generally not an eigenphase at all."}, {"title": "Fourier Methods — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#exercise-9", "text": "Factor L = 33 using a = 5, by finding the order of 5 modulo 33 classically and applying the reduction. Then explain how many control qubits phase estimation would use. Powers of 5 mod 33: 5, 25, 26, 31, 23, 16, 14, 4, 20, 1, so r = 10. Then 5⁵ ≡ 23 (not −1 ≡ 32), and gcd(22, 33) = 11, gcd(24, 33) = 3, so 33 = 3 × 11. With n = 6 bits, m ≈ 2n = 12 (or 13) control qubits ensure 2^m ≥ L², so that continued fractions recover s/10 from a successful run."}, {"title": "Fourier Methods — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-fourier-methods.html#exercise-10", "text": "Suppose the order is r=12 and each order-finding run returns the reduced fraction s/r for a uniformly random s∈{0,dots,11}. (a) What is the probability that a single run reveals r directly, as the reduced denominator? (b) Two runs return 3/12=1/4 and 10/12=5/6. Show that the least common multiple of their denominators gives r. (c) What is the probability that two independent runs determine r in this way? (a) The denominator equals 12 exactly when gcd(s,12)=1, that is s∈{1,5,7,11}: probability 1/3. (b) The denominators are 4 and 6, and lcm(4,6)=12=r. Check a¹²≡1 to confirm. (c) The lcm of the two denominators is 12/gcd(s₁,s₂,12), so it equals 12 unless both s are even or both are multiples of 3. Since s is uniform modulo 12, these events are independent: Pr=(1−1/4)(1−1/9)=3/4·8/9=2/3. Combining samples doubles the success chance relative to a single run, which is why the procedure collects denominators from a few runs before testing."}, {"title": "5.1.1 The good–bad plane", "chapter": "Amplitude Manipulation", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#topic-5-1-1", "text": "5.1.1 The good–bad plane § good subspace · success probability Let \\(A\\) be a preparation circuit on \\(n\\) qubits, and let a predicate \\(\\chi(x)\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) mark the good basis states. The predicate defines the good subspace , spanned by basis states with \\(\\chi(x)=1\\), and the bad subspace, its orthogonal complement. Split the prepared state into its components in each: \\[A\\ket{0^n}=\\ket\\psi=\\sin\\theta\\,\\ket G+\\cos\\theta\\,\\ket B,\\qquad0\\le\\theta\\le\\tfrac\\pi2,\\] where \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\) are the normalized good and bad components. Measuring \\(\\ket\\psi\\) gives a good outcome with success probability \\(p=\\sin^2\\theta\\). The decomposition is the key simplification. The space has dimension \\(2^n\\), but every operation we are about to use maps the plane spanned by \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\) to itself. So we can draw the whole algorithm in two dimensions, with \\(\\ket B\\) along the horizontal axis and \\(\\ket G\\) along the vertical. The state starts at angle \\(\\theta\\) above the horizontal. Success means getting close to the vertical axis. Nothing requires \\(A\\) to produce a uniform superposition or the good states to be equally weighted. For Grover search, \\(A=H^{\\otimes n}\\), there are \\(t\\) marked items among \\(N\\), and \\(\\sin^2\\theta=t/N\\). For a randomized heuristic made reversible, \\(A\\) might put large amplitude on promising candidates, and \\(p\\) is whatever it is. The plane picture covers both. Problems For \\(A=H^{\\otimes 3}\\) and the predicate \\(\\chi(x)=1\\) exactly for \\(x\\in\\{011,101\\}\\), find \\(\\ket G\\), \\(\\ket B\\), and \\(\\theta\\). A preparation gives amplitudes \\(\\frac12,\\frac12,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2},0\\) on \\(\\ket{00},\\ket{01},\\ket{10},\\ket{11}\\), and \\(\\chi\\) marks \\(01\\) and \\(11\\). Find \\(p\\) and \\(\\ket G\\). Why is the plane spanned by \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\) different for different preparations \\(A\\), even with the same predicate?"}, {"title": "5.1.2 Two reflections make a rotation", "chapter": "Amplitude Manipulation", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#topic-5-1-2", "text": "5.1.2 Two reflections make a rotation § amplitude amplification · Grover iterate We have two reflections available. The predicate gives a phase oracle , built cleanly as in Chapter 2: \\[S_G=I-2\\Pi_G,\\] which negates good states and leaves bad ones alone; \\(\\Pi_G\\) is the projection onto the good subspace . In the plane, \\(S_G\\) reflects across the horizontal axis. The preparation gives the reflection about \\(\\ket\\psi\\) from Section 2.4.1, \\[R_\\psi=A(2\\ket{0^n}\\bra{0^n}-I)A^\\dagger=2\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi-I,\\] which needs both \\(A\\) and \\(A^\\dagger\\). Composing two reflections across lines at angle \\(\\theta\\) gives a rotation by \\(2\\theta\\). Let us check this by multiplying matrices in the basis \\((\\ket G,\\ket B)\\): \\[S_G=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad R_\\psi=\\begin{pmatrix}-\\cos2\\theta&\\sin2\\theta\\\\\\sin2\\theta&\\cos2\\theta\\end{pmatrix},\\quad Q=R_\\psi S_G=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos2\\theta&\\sin2\\theta\\\\-\\sin2\\theta&\\cos2\\theta\\end{pmatrix}.\\] \\(Q\\) is the Grover iterate , a rotation by \\(2\\theta\\) toward \\(\\ket G\\). Applying it \\(k\\) times to \\(\\ket\\psi\\), which sits at angle \\(\\theta\\), \\[Q^k\\ket\\psi=\\sin\\big((2k+1)\\theta\\big)\\ket G+\\cos\\big((2k+1)\\theta\\big)\\ket B.\\] This is amplitude amplification , due to Brassard, Høyer, Mosca, and Tapp (2002) , generalizing Grover (1996) . When \\(p\\) is small, \\(\\theta\\approx\\sqrt p\\), and reaching angle near \\(\\pi/2\\) takes about \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4\\sqrt p}\\) iterations. Classical repetition of \\(A\\) with checking needs about \\(1/p\\) tries. The speedup is quadratic, in units of calls to \\(A\\) and the predicate. Each iteration costs one oracle call for \\(S_G\\), one \\(A^\\dagger\\), one \\(A\\), and a zero-state reflection. If \\(A\\) is expensive, as it often is in applications, the count of \\(A\\)-calls dominates the cost, not the count of oracle queries. Problems For \\(p=1/4\\), compute the success probability after one iteration. Verify the product \\(R_\\psi S_G\\) by applying it to \\(\\ket G\\). A programmer implements \\(S_GR_\\psi\\) instead of \\(R_\\psi S_G\\). Does amplification still work?"}, {"title": "5.1.3 Choosing the number of iterations", "chapter": "Amplitude Manipulation", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#topic-5-1-3", "text": "5.1.3 Choosing the number of iterations § overshoot · exponential search The rotation does not stop at the good axis. Iterate too many times and the state swings past it, and the success probability falls again. With \\(p\\) known, choose \\[k=\\operatorname{round}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4\\theta}-\\frac12\\right),\\] which puts \\((2k+1)\\theta\\) within \\(\\theta\\) of \\(\\pi/2\\). The success probability is then at least \\(\\cos^2\\theta=1-p\\). This overshoot is a real hazard. For \\(p=1/4\\) one iteration gives certainty and a second gives \\(\\sin^2(5\\pi/6)=1/4\\), back where we started. Unlike classical repetition, where more tries never hurt, more iterations can undo the work. What if \\(p\\) is unknown? Guessing a single \\(k\\) can land anywhere on the oscillation. The remedy, exponential search ( Boyer, Brassard, Høyer, Tapp, 1996 ), picks \\(k\\) uniformly at random from \\(\\{0,\\ldots,K-1\\}\\), runs, checks the result, and if it fails multiplies \\(K\\) by a factor such as \\(6/5\\). Averaged over a random \\(k\\), the success probability is close to \\(1/2\\) once \\(K\\) exceeds about \\(1/\\sin2\\theta\\). The expected total number of iterations is \\(O(1/\\sqrt p)\\), the same order as if \\(p\\) were known. If no good item exists, the procedure never succeeds; it must stop after a budget of order \\(\\sqrt{N}\\) and report “none found” with a stated error probability. Problems For \\(p=0.01\\), give the optimal \\(k\\) and the success probability. How many classical tries reach the same success probability? With \\(p=0.01\\) and \\(k=14\\) by mistake, what is the success probability? Why does the average of \\(\\sin^2((2k+1)\\theta)\\) over \\(k\\in\\{0,\\ldots,K-1\\}\\) approach \\(1/2\\) for large \\(K\\)?"}, {"title": "5.2.1 Exact amplification", "chapter": "Amplitude Manipulation", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#topic-5-2-1", "text": "5.2.1 Exact amplification § exact amplitude amplification With the ordinary iterate, success probability \\(1-p\\) is guaranteed, but certainty generally is not: \\((2k+1)\\theta\\) hits \\(\\pi/2\\) exactly only for special \\(\\theta\\). When \\(p\\) is known exactly, we can fix this. One clean method of exact amplitude amplification lowers the angle slightly so that it divides evenly. Choose \\(k=\\lceil\\pi/(4\\theta)-1/2\\rceil\\) and set \\(\\theta'=\\pi/(4k+2)\\). Then \\((2k+1)\\theta'=\\pi/2\\) exactly, and the choice of \\(k\\) guarantees \\(\\theta'\\le\\theta\\). Now reduce the success probability from \\(p=\\sin^2\\theta\\) to \\(p'=\\sin^2\\theta'\\): add one flag qubit, rotate it to \\(\\sqrt{1-p'/p}\\,\\ket0+\\sqrt{p'/p}\\,\\ket1\\), and redefine success as “the original predicate holds and the flag is \\(1\\).” The new preparation \\(A'=A\\otimes R_{\\text{flag}}\\) succeeds with probability exactly \\(p\\cdot p'/p=p'\\). Amplify \\(A'\\) for \\(k\\) iterations; the success probability is \\(\\sin^2((2k+1)\\theta')=1\\). For \\(p=1/2\\): \\(\\theta=\\pi/4\\), \\(k=\\lceil1-\\frac12\\rceil=1\\), \\(\\theta'=\\pi/6\\), \\(p'=1/4\\). A fair flag halves the success probability to \\(1/4\\), and one ordinary iteration then succeeds with certainty. Without the flag, one iteration on \\(p=1/2\\) gives \\(\\sin^2(3\\pi/4)=1/2\\), no improvement at all. An alternative keeps the original preparation and replaces the phase \\(-1\\) in the last iteration by tuned phases, solving a two-by-two equation for them. Both methods need \\(p\\) exactly; with \\(p\\) only estimated, they inherit the estimation error. Exactness is also limited by how accurately the flag rotation or phases can be implemented. If \\(p=0\\), no phase sequence helps: amplification can only rescale a good component that \\(A\\) actually produces. Problems Apply the flag method to \\(p=0.3\\). Find \\(k\\), \\(\\theta'\\), and the flag probability. Why must \\(\\theta'\\le\\theta\\) for the flag method to work? Suppose \\(p\\) is believed to be \\(1/2\\) but is actually \\(0.45\\). What success probability does the flag method designed for \\(1/2\\) give?"}, {"title": "5.2.2 Fixed-point amplification", "chapter": "Amplitude Manipulation", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#topic-5-2-2", "text": "5.2.2 Fixed-point amplification § fixed-point amplification Overshoot comes from rotating by a fixed angle. Is there a way to move toward the good axis that never goes past it, whatever \\(p\\) is? Replace the two \\(-1\\) phases by \\(e^{i\\pi/3}\\). Define \\[R_0=I+(e^{i\\pi/3}-1)\\ket{0^n}\\bra{0^n},\\qquad R_G=I+(e^{i\\pi/3}-1)\\Pi_G,\\] and the new preparation \\(A_1=A\\,R_0\\,A^\\dagger\\,R_G\\,A\\). If \\(A\\) fails with probability \\(\\epsilon=1-p\\), then \\(A_1\\) fails with probability exactly \\(\\epsilon^3\\). (Expanding \\(A_1\\ket{0^n}\\) in the good–bad plane shows that its bad amplitude is the original bad amplitude \\(\\sqrt\\epsilon\\) multiplied by a factor of magnitude \\(\\epsilon\\), so its squared magnitude is \\(\\epsilon^3\\).) This is Grover’s fixed-point search (2005) . The failure probability never increases: \\(\\epsilon^3\\le\\epsilon\\) for every \\(\\epsilon\\in[0,1]\\). Recursion helps more: \\(A_2\\) built from \\(A_1\\) fails with \\(\\epsilon^9\\), and so on. This fixed-point amplification is robust when \\(p\\) is unknown or varies between inputs. The price is speed. Each level triples the number of calls to \\(A\\) and \\(A^\\dagger\\), while the failure only cubes. Starting from small \\(p\\), the failure \\(1-p\\) is close to \\(1\\), and cubing it barely helps: this recursion needs order \\(1/p\\) calls to reach constant success, losing the quadratic speedup . More sophisticated phase sequences ( Yoder, Low, and Chuang, 2014 ) achieve fixed-point behavior with the optimal \\(O(1/\\sqrt{p_{\\min}})\\) calls, given a known lower bound \\(p_{\\min}\\) on \\(p\\). Their phases are chosen by exactly the kind of polynomial design that Chapter 8 develops. Problems Apply one level of the \\(\\pi/3\\) construction when \\(p=1/2\\). Compare with one ordinary iteration. Starting from \\(p=0.01\\), how many recursion levels does the \\(\\pi/3\\) method need to reach success \\(0.5\\), and how many calls to \\(A\\) or \\(A^\\dagger\\)? Why can no amplification method that uses a fixed number of calls be both overshoot-free for every \\(p\\in(0,1]\\) and reach success \\(1/2\\) for arbitrarily small \\(p\\)?"}, {"title": "5.3.1 Eigenvalues of the iterate", "chapter": "Amplitude Manipulation", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#topic-5-3-1", "text": "5.3.1 Eigenvalues of the iterate § iterate eigenvalues A rotation of the plane by \\(2\\theta\\) has no real eigenvectors, but it has complex ones. For \\[Q=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos2\\theta&\\sin2\\theta\\\\-\\sin2\\theta&\\cos2\\theta\\end{pmatrix}\\], try \\(\\ket{v_\\pm}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket G\\pm i\\ket B)\\): \\[Q\\ket{v_\\pm}=e^{\\pm2i\\theta}\\ket{v_\\pm}.\\] (Check \\(\\ket{v_+}\\): the first coordinate is \\(\\cos2\\theta+i\\sin2\\theta=e^{2i\\theta}\\), the second is \\(-\\sin2\\theta+i\\cos2\\theta=ie^{2i\\theta}\\).) These iterate eigenvalues are the whole story: in the plane, \\(Q\\) has eigenphases \\(\\pm2\\theta\\), or \\(\\pm\\theta/\\pi\\) as fractions of a full turn. The prepared state is an equal-weight combination of the two eigenvectors: \\[\\ket\\psi=\\frac{-ie^{i\\theta}}{\\sqrt2}\\ket{v_+}+\\frac{ie^{-i\\theta}}{\\sqrt2}\\ket{v_-}.\\] Both coefficients have squared magnitude \\(\\frac12\\). By Section 4.2.4, phase estimation of \\(Q\\) on \\(\\ket\\psi\\) returns an estimate of either \\(\\theta/\\pi\\) or \\(1-\\theta/\\pi\\), each with probability \\(\\frac12\\). Either one gives \\(\\theta\\) and hence \\(p=\\sin^2\\theta\\), since \\(\\sin^2(\\pi-\\theta)=\\sin^2\\theta\\). We need not know \\(\\ket G\\) or \\(\\ket B\\): \\(\\ket\\psi=A\\ket{0^n}\\) is all we prepare. Problems Verify that \\[\\frac{-ie^{i\\theta}}{\\sqrt2}\\ket{v_+}+\\frac{ie^{-i\\theta}}{\\sqrt2}\\ket{v_-}=\\sin\\theta\\ket G+\\cos\\theta\\ket B\\]. For \\(p=1/4\\), what eigenphases (as fractions of a turn) does phase estimation see? What happens to the eigenphases when \\(p=0\\) or \\(p=1\\)? Is phase estimation still informative?"}, {"title": "5.3.2 Estimation by phase estimation", "chapter": "Amplitude Manipulation", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#topic-5-3-2", "text": "5.3.2 Estimation by phase estimation § amplitude estimation Amplitude estimation runs phase estimation with \\(m\\) control qubits on controlled powers of \\(Q\\), starting from \\(A\\ket{0^n}\\). With \\(M=2^m\\), an outcome \\(j\\) gives \\(\\tilde\\theta=\\pi j/M\\) and \\(\\tilde p=\\sin^2\\tilde\\theta\\). The phase is resolved to about \\(\\pi/M\\) in \\(\\theta\\). How does that translate into error in \\(p\\)? Since \\(\\frac{d}{d\\theta}\\sin^2\\theta=\\sin2\\theta=2\\sqrt{p(1-p)}\\), an error \\(\\delta\\) in \\(\\theta\\) gives error about \\(2\\sqrt{p(1-p)}\\,\\delta\\) in \\(p\\). The precise bound of Brassard, Høyer, Mosca, and Tapp says that with probability at least \\(8/\\pi^2\\), \\[|\\tilde p-p|\\le\\frac{2\\pi\\sqrt{p(1-p)}}{M}+\\frac{\\pi^2}{M^2}.\\] The cost is \\(M-1\\) applications of controlled-\\(Q\\), each containing one \\(A\\), one \\(A^\\dagger\\), and one oracle call. So precision \\(\\epsilon\\) costs order \\(1/\\epsilon\\) calls to \\(A\\). Classical sampling, running \\(A\\) and checking, needs order \\(p(1-p)/\\epsilon^2\\) runs for the same precision with constant confidence. Three conditions make the comparison fair. Amplitude estimation needs coherent access to \\(A\\) and \\(A^\\dagger\\), not just samples; it needs controlled-\\(Q\\), which in turn needs controlled \\(A\\) or a construction that avoids it; and its circuits are long and coherent, with depth of order \\(1/\\epsilon\\), whereas sampling uses many short independent runs. On noisy hardware the long coherent circuit is the hard part. Variants that replace the Fourier transform by classical post-processing of several shorter amplification runs (for example Suzuki et al., 2020 ) trade some of the coherence for more repetitions, keeping the \\(1/\\epsilon\\) scaling under suitable assumptions. Problems With \\(m=8\\) and \\(p\\approx0.3\\), estimate the error bound in \\(p\\). How many classical samples give the same precision (\\(0.011\\)) at a similar confidence, using the Chebyshev bound with the actual variance? Why does the error bound depend on \\(\\sqrt{p(1-p)}\\), and what does this mean for estimating very small probabilities?"}, {"title": "5.3.3 Mean estimation", "chapter": "Amplitude Manipulation", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#topic-5-3-3", "text": "5.3.3 Mean estimation § Monte Carlo estimation · quadratic speedup A common use is Monte Carlo estimation : computing the average of a function \\(v(x)\\in[0,1]\\) over a distribution \\(q(x)\\), such as an expected payoff in finance or an integral in physics. Classically, sample \\(x\\) from \\(q\\), evaluate \\(v\\), and average; precision \\(\\epsilon\\) needs order \\(1/\\epsilon^2\\) samples. To use amplitude estimation, turn the mean into a success probability. Suppose a circuit \\(P\\) prepares \\(\\sum_x\\sqrt{q(x)}\\ket x\\), and a clean circuit computes \\(v(x)\\) and rotates an extra qubit: \\[\\ket x\\ket0\\mapsto\\ket x\\big(\\sqrt{1-v(x)}\\ket0+\\sqrt{v(x)}\\ket1\\big),\\] with \\(v(x)\\) uncomputed afterwards. Define \\(A\\) as \\(P\\) followed by this rotation, and call the extra qubit being \\(1\\) a success. Then \\[p=\\sum_xq(x)\\,v(x)=\\mathbb E[v].\\] Amplitude estimation gives \\(\\mathbb E[v]\\) to precision \\(\\epsilon\\) with order \\(1/\\epsilon\\) uses of \\(A\\), a quadratic speedup over classical sampling in the number of samples. Montanaro (2015) extends this to random variables with bounded variance rather than bounded range. The speedup is in sample complexity, and the conditions matter. The distribution must be preparable coherently by a circuit \\(P\\), not merely sampled; loading an empirical distribution of \\(N\\) data points costs order \\(N\\). The function \\(v\\) must be computed reversibly and its value encoded in an amplitude, with arithmetic and rotation errors added to \\(\\epsilon\\). And the quadratic gain must outweigh the overhead of fault-tolerant execution. Resource studies for option pricing (for example Chakrabarti et al., 2021 ) find that the advantage requires substantially better hardware than exists today. A quadratic speedup is real, but it is the kind whose practical value depends on constants. Problems For \\(v(x)\\in\\{0,1\\}\\), show that the construction reduces to ordinary amplitude estimation of a predicate. Classical precision \\(10^{-4}\\) needs about \\(10^8\\) samples. About how many uses of \\(A\\) does amplitude estimation need, and what else must be counted? A dataset of \\(N=10^6\\) records is given as a classical file, and we want the average of a field. Does amplitude estimation give a speedup?"}, {"title": "5.4.1 Minimum finding", "chapter": "Amplitude Manipulation", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#topic-5-4-1", "text": "5.4.1 Minimum finding § minimum finding · threshold search Given a list of \\(N\\) values \\(T(0),\\ldots,T(N-1)\\), accessible through a clean oracle , find an index of the minimum. Classically this needs \\(N\\) queries. A quantum algorithm for minimum finding ( Dürr and Høyer, 1996 ) uses amplification inside a classical loop: Pick a random index \\(y\\) as the current candidate. Threshold search: use exponential search (Section 5.1.3) with the predicate \\(\\chi(x)=[T(x)<T(y)]\\) to find an index with a smaller value. If one is found, make it the new \\(y\\) and repeat; stop when the query budget is exhausted. Why is the total cost only \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\)? When the current candidate has rank \\(r\\) (there are \\(r-1\\) smaller values), threshold search costs about \\(\\sqrt{N/(r-1)}\\). The new candidate is uniformly distributed among the smaller values, so the rank roughly halves on average each round. A careful analysis shows that a given value of rank \\(r\\) is ever selected with probability \\(1/r\\), so the expected total is \\(\\sum_r\\frac1r\\sqrt{N/r}=O(\\sqrt N)\\). With \\(22.5\\sqrt N\\) queries the algorithm succeeds with probability at least \\(1/2\\). The predicate needs a comparator: a clean circuit for \\([T(x)<T(y)]\\) with \\(y\\) a classical constant in each round. Its cost, and the cost of the oracle for \\(T\\), multiply the query count. Minimum finding underlies quantum speedups for many optimization subroutines, always quadratic and always in a query model. Problems For \\(N=10^6\\), compare the classical query count with \\(22.5\\sqrt N\\). Why must each threshold search use exponential search rather than a fixed number of iterations? If the values have ties and we want all indices of the minimum, how does the cost change?"}, {"title": "5.4.2 Optimality and limits", "chapter": "Amplitude Manipulation", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#topic-5-4-2", "text": "5.4.2 Optimality and limits § query lower bound Can a cleverer quantum algorithm search faster than \\(O(\\sqrt{N})\\)? No. Bennett, Bernstein, Brassard, and Vazirani (1997) proved that any quantum algorithm finding a single marked item among \\(N\\) with bounded error needs \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) queries, and Zalka later showed Grover’s algorithm is optimal even in its constant. This query lower bound holds for every algorithm that accesses the list only through the oracle, which is what “unstructured” means. Three consequences follow. First, amplification cannot solve NP-complete problems in polynomial time by brute force: searching \\(2^n\\) assignments costs \\(2^{n/2}\\) queries, still exponential. Second, any further speedup must come from structure. Period finding is exponentially faster than search because periodicity is structure that the Fourier transform exploits; quantum walks (Chapter 6) exploit graph structure for problems like element distinctness . Third, the quadratic speedup over brute force may not beat the best classical algorithm, which usually uses structure too. For satisfiability, classical algorithms far better than \\(2^n\\) exist, and Grover-based speedups must be compared against them, often by amplifying a classical randomized algorithm’s success probability, as in Section 5.1.1. Amplification rotates within a plane fixed by two reflections. Quantum walks generalize this: they compose reflections determined by the local structure of a graph, and the speed of the resulting rotation depends on the graph’s spectral gap . That is the subject of Chapter 6. Problems A satisfiability instance has \\(n=60\\) variables. How many queries does Grover search over all assignments need, and how does that compare with a classical algorithm running in time \\(1.3^n\\)? Does the \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) lower bound imply that no quantum algorithm can solve some NP-complete problem in polynomial time? Amplitude estimation achieves error \\(\\epsilon\\) with \\(O(1/\\epsilon)\\) queries. Is that also optimal?"}, {"title": "Amplitude Manipulation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#exercise-1", "text": "A preparation succeeds with probability p = 1/9. What is the success probability after one and after two amplification iterations? 3θ ≈ 58.4°, so one iteration gives sin²(58.4°) ≈ 0.727. 5θ ≈ 97.4°, so two iterations give sin²(97.4°) ≈ 0.984. Two is optimal here: round(π/(4θ) − 1/2) = round(1.81) = 2."}, {"title": "Amplitude Manipulation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#exercise-2", "text": "Grover search runs over N=16 items with uniform preparation. (a) With one marked item, find θ, the optimal number of iterations, and the success probability. (b) With four marked items, how many iterations give certainty? (a) sinθ=1/4, so θ≈0.2527 and π/(4θ)−1/2≈2.61, giving k=3. The success probability is sin²(7θ)≈0.961. Classical guessing with checking needs about 16ln (1/0.039)≈52 tries for the same confidence, or 16 to be sure. (b) With t=4, θ=π/6 and one iteration reaches 3θ=π/2 exactly: certainty. A second iteration would give sin²(5π/6)=1/4, so the number of iterations must be matched to t."}, {"title": "Amplitude Manipulation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#exercise-3", "text": "A preparation already succeeds with probability p=0.9. Compare one ordinary amplification iteration with one level of the π/3 fixed-point construction. Which should you use if you only know that p is “probably large”? The ordinary iterate rotates by 2θ, and here θ≈1.249 is already close to π/2. One iteration overshoots to sin²(3θ)=0.9 (3−4×0.9)²=0.324: the success probability drops from 0.9 to about 0.32. The fixed-point construction turns failure 0.1 into 0.1³=0.001, giving success 0.999, at a cost of three calls to A or A†. When p is uncertain and possibly large, the fixed-point method is the safe choice: it never makes things worse. Its weakness is the small-p regime, where it loses the quadratic speedup."}, {"title": "Amplitude Manipulation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#exercise-4", "text": "Amplitude estimation with m = 6 control qubits returns j = 11. What is the estimate of p, and what does the error bound say if p is near this estimate? θ̃ = 11π/64 ≈ 0.540 rad, so p̃ = sin²(0.540) ≈ 0.264. With M = 64 and p ≈ 0.264, the bound is 2π√(0.194)/64 + π²/4096 ≈ 0.0433 + 0.0024 ≈ 0.046, with probability at least 8/π² ≈ 0.81. The estimate is p ∈ [0.22, 0.31] with that confidence, from 63 controlled-Q calls."}, {"title": "Amplitude Manipulation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#exercise-5", "text": "Apply exact amplification to p=0.1. Find θ, the number of iterations k, the reduced angle θ', the new success probability p', and the flag-qubit probability p'/p. Check that the final success probability is 1. θ=arcsin√0.1≈0.3218, so π/(4θ)−1/2≈1.94 and k=2. Then θ'=π/10≈0.3142≤θ, and p'=sin²(π/10)≈0.0955. The flag qubit is rotated to |1⟩ with probability p'/p≈0.955, so A'=A⊗ R_(flag) succeeds with probability exactly p'. Two iterations take the angle to 5θ'=π/2, so the success probability is sin²(π/2)=1. Without the flag, two iterations give sin²(5θ)≈sin²(1.609)≈0.9985. Exactness matters when the amplified routine is used inside a larger coherent algorithm, where a small failure amplitude would propagate."}, {"title": "Amplitude Manipulation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#exercise-6", "text": "Amplitude estimation with m=4 (M=16) is applied to a preparation with p=1/4. (a) Which eigenphases does phase estimation see, and which outcomes j are most likely? (b) List the estimates sin²(π j/M) available for j=0,dots,8 and find the estimate produced. (c) Compare its error with the stated bound. (a) θ=π/6, so the eigenphases are 1/6 and 5/6. Then M/6≈2.67 and 5M/6≈13.33, and the most likely outcomes are j=3 and j=13. (b) For j=0,dots,8 the estimates are 0, 0.038, 0.146, 0.309, 0.5, 0.691, 0.854, 0.962, 1. Both j=3 and j=13 give tilde p=sin²(3π/16)≈0.309. (c) The error is about 0.059. The bound is 2π√(3/16)/16+π²/256≈0.209, so the result lies comfortably inside it. The estimates are not evenly spaced in p: they are dense near 0 and 1 and sparse near 1/2, which is why the error bound contains √(p(1−p))."}, {"title": "Amplitude Manipulation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#exercise-7", "text": "Set up mean estimation for x uniform on {0,1,2,3} and v(x)=x/3. Describe the preparation A, compute the success probability p, and show that amplitude estimation with m=2 returns mathbb E[v] exactly. Let P=H^(⊗2) prepare 1/2Σ_x|x⟩. Then compute v(x), rotate a flag to √(1−v(x))|0⟩+√(v(x))|1⟩, and uncompute v(x). Success means the flag is 1, with probability p=1/4Σ_xv(x)=1/4(0+1/3+2/3+1)=1/2=mathbb E[v]. So θ=π/4, and Q has eigenphases θ/π=1/4 and 3/4. Both are exact grid points for M=4, so phase estimation returns j=1 or j=3 with certainty. Either gives tildeθ=π/4 or 3π/4, and tilde p=sin²tildeθ=1/2. The answer is exact here only because the phase happened to lie on the grid. In general, the rotation angles must also be computed to finite precision, and that error is added to the estimate."}, {"title": "Amplitude Manipulation — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student proposes to amplify an amplified procedure: first build A'=QᵏA, which succeeds with probability sin²((2k+1)θ), then amplify A'. “Each stage gives a square root, so two stages give a fourth-root speedup.” Count the total number of calls to A or A† needed to reach constant success, for small θ, and compare with direct amplification. Direct amplification needs about π/(4θ) iterations, each using A and A† once: about π/(2θ) calls. The nested scheme starts from angle θ'=(2k+1)θ and needs about π/(4θ') outer iterations. Each uses A' and A'†, so 2(2k+1) calls of A or A†. The total is π/(4(2k+1)θ)·2(2k+1)=π/2θ, exactly the same. Nesting only regroups the rotation into larger steps. It cannot beat the Ω(√N) lower bound, because the composite routine is itself an algorithm that calls the oracle."}, {"title": "Amplitude Manipulation — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#exercise-9", "text": "A classical randomized algorithm for a search problem runs in time T and succeeds with probability p. Describe how to obtain a quantum algorithm with success probability at least 1/2 and estimate its running time. What must be true of the classical algorithm? Treat the random bits as an input register prepared in uniform superposition; the reversible version of the algorithm (Chapter 2) is the preparation A, and checking the output is the predicate. Amplification with unknown p (exponential search) reaches success 1/2 with O(1/√p) calls to A and A†, for total time O(T/√p) versus O(T/p) classically. This requires a reversible implementation of the algorithm and a coherent checker, and the overhead of reversibility (garbage removal, roughly a factor of two plus workspace) is included in T."}, {"title": "Amplitude Manipulation — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-amplitude-manipulation.html#exercise-10", "text": "When p is small, the useful target is relative precision: |tilde p−p|≤ε_rp. (a) Show that classical sampling needs about 1/(ε_r²p) samples (using the variance p(1−p)), while amplitude estimation needs M of order 1/(ε_r√p). (b) Evaluate both for p=10⁻⁴ and ε_r=0.1, ignoring constant confidence factors. (c) What extra assumption does the quantum count carry? (a) Sampling has standard deviation √(p(1−p)/R)≈√(p/R). Setting this equal to ε_rp gives R≈1/(ε_r²p). For amplitude estimation the leading error is 2π√p/M, and setting it equal to ε_rp gives M≈2π/(ε_r√p). The π²/M² term is then negligible. (b) Sampling: 1/(0.01×10⁻⁴)=10⁶ samples. Amplitude estimation: M≈2π/(0.1×0.01)≈6.3×10³, so M=2¹³=8192 and about 8×10³ uses of A. The ratio grows as 1/(ε_r√p). (c) The quantum count assumes coherent, controllable access to A and A† and a circuit about 8000 iterations deep that stays coherent throughout, while sampling uses a million short independent runs. The comparison in calls is fair only after both sides price these differences."}, {"title": "6.1.1 Classical random walks", "chapter": "Quantum Walks", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#topic-6-1-1", "text": "6.1.1 Classical random walks § random walk · diffusive spreading Start a walker at position \\(0\\) on the integers. At each step flip a fair coin and move one step left or right. After \\(T\\) steps the position is a sum of \\(T\\) independent \\(\\pm1\\) steps, with mean \\(0\\) and variance \\(T\\). The typical distance from the start is \\(\\sqrt T\\). This \\(\\sqrt T\\) growth is diffusive spreading , the signature of a classical random walk : to travel distance \\(d\\) takes about \\(d^2\\) steps. On a graph, a random walk moves from a vertex to a uniformly random neighbour. It is described by a transition matrix \\(P\\), with \\(P_{xy}\\) the probability of moving from \\(x\\) to \\(y\\). The probability distribution after \\(t\\) steps is obtained by multiplying by \\(P\\) repeatedly. Two quantities govern how useful the walk is. The mixing time is how long until the distribution is close to its stationary limit. The hitting time is how long until the walker first reaches a target set. Both are controlled by the spectral gap of \\(P\\), which Section 6.3 makes precise. Why not simply make this walk coherent, replacing the coin flip by a superposition of left and right? On the line, the naive map \\(\\ket x\\mapsto\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket{x-1}+\\ket{x+1})\\) is not unitary. Apply it to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket2\\): both images contain \\(\\ket1\\) with amplitude \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\), so the images overlap, \\(\\frac12\\neq0\\), although \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket2\\) are orthogonal. A unitary must preserve orthogonality. The walker needs to remember something more than its position. Problems After \\(T=100\\) classical steps, what is the standard deviation of the position, and roughly how likely is the walker to be beyond \\(\\pm30\\)? Write the transition matrix for the random walk on a cycle of four vertices. What is its stationary distribution? Show that the map \\(\\ket x\\mapsto\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket{x-1}+\\ket{x+1})\\) on a cycle of \\(N=3\\) vertices is also not unitary."}, {"title": "6.1.2 Coin and shift registers", "chapter": "Quantum Walks", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#topic-6-1-2", "text": "6.1.2 Coin and shift registers § coined quantum walk · coin register · shift operator Give the walker a direction. Its state is \\(\\ket{c,x}\\), with a coin register \\(c\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) for left or right and a position register \\(x\\). One step of the coined quantum walk does two things: Coin: apply a unitary \\(C\\) to the coin, for example the Hadamard \\(H\\), creating a superposition of directions. Shift: move according to the coin, \\(S\\ket{0,x}=\\ket{0,x-1}\\) and \\(S\\ket{1,x}=\\ket{1,x+1}\\). The shift operator \\(S\\) is a permutation of the basis states \\(\\ket{c,x}\\), so it is unitary: the coin label tells us which way the walker came, so the move can be undone. The step is \\(W=S(C\\otimes I)\\). The coin is what fixes the obstruction of Section 6.1.1: the two contributions to position \\(x\\) now arrive with different coin labels, \\(\\ket{1,x}\\) from the left and \\(\\ket{0,x}\\) from the right, so the images of different basis states stay orthogonal. On a cycle of \\(N\\) vertices, positions are taken modulo \\(N\\) and the shift is a controlled increment or decrement, built from the reversible arithmetic of Section 2.1.3. Here is a four-cycle starting at \\(\\ket{0,0}\\), with Hadamard coin. One step gives \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket{0,3}+\\ket{1,1})\\). Two steps give \\[\\tfrac12\\big(\\ket{0,2}+\\ket{1,0}+\\ket{0,0}-\\ket{1,2}\\big).\\] Predict first. Compute the third step. Where is the walker? That deterministic arrival is interference among routes, which classical walks cannot have. It also shows a hazard. Measuring the position after every step would collapse each superposition and turn the quantum walk back into a classical random walk. The walk must run coherently, and only the final position is measured. Problems Verify the two-step state of the four-cycle walk from the one-step state. For a walk on a graph where every vertex has degree \\(d\\), how large must the coin register be? Replace the Hadamard coin by the identity. What does the walk do? By \\(X\\)?"}, {"title": "6.1.3 Ballistic spreading", "chapter": "Quantum Walks", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#topic-6-1-3", "text": "6.1.3 Ballistic spreading § ballistic spreading Run the Hadamard walk on the line for many steps from a symmetric initial coin \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0+i\\ket1)\\) at position \\(0\\). The distribution looks nothing like the classical bell curve. After \\(T=60\\) steps the quantum distribution has two sharp peaks near \\(\\pm T/\\sqrt2\\approx\\pm42\\) and is spread almost flat between them; its standard deviation is about \\(32.5\\approx0.54\\,T\\). The classical walk has standard deviation \\(\\sqrt{60}\\approx7.7\\). The quantum spread grows linearly in \\(T\\), ballistic spreading , like a particle moving at constant speed, while the classical spread grows like \\(\\sqrt T\\). Why? A rough answer comes from the Fourier picture of Section 6.2.3. Each Fourier mode of the walk on the line travels at a fixed speed set by how its eigenphase changes with momentum, and the walker’s state is a superposition of modes moving at speeds up to \\(1/\\sqrt2\\). The distribution spreads at those speeds. In the classical walk, by contrast, each step’s randomness is independent and errors accumulate like a sum of coin flips. Faster spreading is suggestive but is not an algorithm. An algorithm needs a problem, an input model, and a measurement with a high chance of answering it. The quadratic difference between \\(T\\) and \\(\\sqrt T\\) does foreshadow a recurring pattern, though: quantum walks often hit or mix in about the square root of the classical time. The rest of the chapter turns that pattern into theorems with stated assumptions. Problems How many steps does a classical walk need for a typical distance of \\(100\\)? A Hadamard walk? Why are the odd positions empty after an even number of steps, in both walks? Starting from coin \\(\\ket0\\) instead of the symmetric coin, the distribution is lopsided. Explain why the Hadamard coin treats the two directions differently."}, {"title": "6.2.1 Evolution generated by a graph", "chapter": "Quantum Walks", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#topic-6-2-1", "text": "6.2.1 Evolution generated by a graph § continuous-time quantum walk Take two vertices joined by an edge. Its adjacency matrix, with a coupling strength \\(\\gamma\\), is \\(H=\\gamma X\\). A continuous-time quantum walk evolves the state by \\[U(t)=e^{-iHt},\\] where the exponential of a matrix is defined by the same power series as for numbers: \\(e^{M}=I+M+M^2/2!+M^3/3!+\\cdots\\). For \\(H=\\gamma X\\) the series is easy, because \\(X^2=I\\): even powers of \\(X\\) are \\(I\\) and odd powers are \\(X\\). Grouping terms, \\[e^{-i\\gamma tX}=\\Big(1-\\tfrac{(\\gamma t)^2}{2!}+\\cdots\\Big)I-i\\Big(\\gamma t-\\tfrac{(\\gamma t)^3}{3!}+\\cdots\\Big)X=\\cos(\\gamma t)\\,I-i\\sin(\\gamma t)\\,X.\\] So \\(U(t)\\ket0=\\cos(\\gamma t)\\ket0-i\\sin(\\gamma t)\\ket1\\). The walker moves to the other vertex with probability \\(\\sin^2(\\gamma t)\\), completely at \\(t=\\pi/(2\\gamma)\\), and then back. Two remarks. First, \\(U(t)\\) is unitary because \\(H\\) is Hermitian , equal to its own conjugate transpose; for real symmetric matrices like adjacency matrices this holds automatically. Second, only the product \\(\\gamma t\\) matters. Doubling every coupling halves the time, so “time” alone is not a cost; the cost of simulating a walk on a quantum computer depends on \\(\\|H\\|t\\), as Chapter 9 makes precise. Comparing the classical continuous random walk, which spreads probability as \\(e^{-tL}\\) with the graph Laplacian \\(L\\), the quantum walk instead oscillates coherently forever. Problems At \\(t=\\pi/(4\\gamma)\\), what is the two-vertex state, and what are the probabilities? Check that \\(U(t)=\\cos(\\gamma t)I-i\\sin(\\gamma t)X\\) is unitary by computing \\(U(t)^\\dagger U(t)\\). For the classical continuous walk on two vertices, the probability of being at vertex 1 is \\(\\frac12(1-e^{-2t})\\). Contrast its long-time behavior with the quantum walk’s."}, {"title": "6.2.2 Matrix exponentials through eigenvalues", "chapter": "Quantum Walks", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#topic-6-2-2", "text": "6.2.2 Matrix exponentials through eigenvalues § Hamiltonian · spectral decomposition For larger graphs the power series is not summed so easily. Eigenvalues make it simple. If \\(H\\ket{v_j}=\\lambda_j\\ket{v_j}\\), then every power acts on \\(\\ket{v_j}\\) by \\(\\lambda_j^k\\), so the series acts by the ordinary exponential: \\[e^{-iHt}\\ket{v_j}=e^{-i\\lambda_jt}\\ket{v_j}.\\] A Hermitian matrix has real eigenvalues and an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors; this is the spectral decomposition \\(H=\\sum_j\\lambda_j\\ket{v_j}\\bra{v_j}\\). To evolve any state, expand it in eigenvectors, multiply each coefficient by its own phase \\(e^{-i\\lambda_jt}\\), and recombine. In physics a Hermitian generator of evolution is called a Hamiltonian , and its eigenvalues are energies. Check on the two-vertex graph. \\(X\\) has eigenvectors \\(\\ket+\\) (eigenvalue \\(1\\)) and \\(\\ket-\\) (eigenvalue \\(-1\\)). So \\[e^{-i\\gamma tX}\\ket0=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(e^{-i\\gamma t}\\ket++e^{i\\gamma t}\\ket-)=\\cos(\\gamma t)\\ket0-i\\sin(\\gamma t)\\ket1\\], as before. This picture explains what a continuous walk does: each eigenvector rotates its phase at a rate given by its eigenvalue, and interference among them produces the motion. Differences between eigenvalues set the timescales. The largest eigenvalue magnitude, \\(\\|H\\|\\), sets the fastest timescale, which is why it controls simulation cost. And it is the same picture as phase estimation : running phase estimation on \\(e^{-iHt}\\) samples the eigenvalues \\(\\lambda_j\\), weighted by the squared overlaps of the initial state. Problems Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of \\(H=Z\\), and write \\(e^{-iZt}\\) as a matrix. The path graph on three vertices has adjacency matrix with eigenvalues \\(\\sqrt2,0,-\\sqrt2\\). With \\(\\gamma=1\\), what is the shortest time at which every eigenvector phase returns to its starting value, up to a common phase? Why does the spectral decomposition not give an efficient quantum algorithm for computing \\(e^{-iHt}\\) directly?"}, {"title": "6.2.3 Walks on a cycle and Fourier modes", "chapter": "Quantum Walks", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#topic-6-2-3", "text": "6.2.3 Walks on a cycle and Fourier modes § Fourier mode On a cycle of \\(N\\) vertices, the adjacency matrix \\(A\\) sends \\(\\ket x\\) to \\(\\ket{x-1}+\\ket{x+1}\\). Translation invariance suggests trying the Fourier states of Chapter 4. Let \\(\\ket{\\tilde k}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt N}\\sum_x\\omega^{kx}\\ket x\\), with \\(\\omega=e^{2\\pi i/N}\\). Then \\[A\\ket{\\tilde k}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt N}\\sum_x\\omega^{kx}(\\ket{x-1}+\\ket{x+1})=(\\omega^{k}+\\omega^{-k})\\ket{\\tilde k}=2\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{2\\pi k}{N}\\right)\\ket{\\tilde k}.\\] Each Fourier mode is an eigenvector, with eigenvalue \\(2\\cos(2\\pi k/N)\\). The continuous walk on the cycle is therefore: Fourier transform, apply phases \\(e^{-2i\\gamma t\\cos(2\\pi k/N)}\\), inverse Fourier transform. The QFT of Chapter 4 implements the change of basis efficiently, so for the cycle, and for other graphs with this kind of symmetry, the walk can be simulated exactly with \\(O(n^2)\\) gates. The spectrum also explains ballistic spreading . A wave packet near momentum \\(k\\) moves at a speed given by the slope of the eigenvalue curve, \\(\\frac{d}{dk}2\\cos(2\\pi k/N)\\), which is bounded and nonzero for most \\(k\\). A packet built from many modes spreads at those speeds, linearly in time. The coined walk has an analogous dispersion relation, with maximum speed \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) for the Hadamard coin, which matches the peaks at \\(\\pm T/\\sqrt2\\) in Section 6.1.3. The cycle is special because its symmetry makes the eigenvectors known. For a general graph the eigenvectors are unknown, and a walk is implemented step by step from local access to the graph: given a vertex, list its neighbours. The next section builds walks from that local access. Problems For \\(N=4\\), list the eigenvalues of the cycle’s adjacency matrix. Which Fourier mode of the cycle is unchanged (up to a phase) by both the walk and a measurement of position probabilities? Explain. Why can the continuous walk on a cycle of \\(2^n\\) vertices be simulated exactly with \\(O(n^2)\\) gates, while the walk on an arbitrary graph of \\(2^n\\) vertices generally cannot?"}, {"title": "6.3.1 Szegedy’s walk", "chapter": "Quantum Walks", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#topic-6-3-1", "text": "6.3.1 Szegedy’s walk § Szegedy walk · Markov chain Let \\(P\\) be the transition matrix of a Markov chain on vertices \\(x\\in\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}\\). Work on pairs \\(\\ket x\\ket y\\), thought of as directed edges. For each \\(x\\), define the state that spreads from \\(x\\) according to \\(P\\): \\[\\ket{\\psi_x}=\\ket x\\otimes\\sum_y\\sqrt{P_{xy}}\\,\\ket y.\\] The states \\(\\ket{\\psi_x}\\) are orthonormal, since they have different first registers. Let \\(R_A=2\\sum_x\\ket{\\psi_x}\\bra{\\psi_x}-I\\) be the reflection about their span, and define \\(R_B\\) the same way with the roles of the two registers exchanged (spreading from \\(y\\) back to \\(x\\)). The Szegedy walk is the product \\[W=R_BR_A.\\] Each reflection is built as in Section 2.4.1: a circuit \\(V\\) with \\(V\\ket x\\ket0=\\ket{\\psi_x}\\), a reflection about \\(\\ket0\\) on the second register, and \\(V^\\dagger\\). So one walk step needs a coherent procedure that, given \\(x\\), prepares the superposition of its neighbours weighted by \\(\\sqrt{P_{xy}}\\). For a graph given by neighbour lists, that is a coherent neighbour query plus a uniform superposition over the degree. Why two reflections? Because their product is a rotation in each of a collection of two-dimensional planes, just as in amplification, with angles determined by overlaps between the two families of states. Those overlaps are the entries of the matrix \\(D_{xy}=\\sqrt{P_{xy}P_{yx}}\\), which for a symmetric chain equals \\(P\\). Szegedy’s construction ( Szegedy, 2004 ) turns a classical chain into a quantum walk whose behavior is determined by the classical chain’s spectrum, the precise relation given next. Problems For the walk on a complete graph of 3 vertices (move to each other vertex with probability \\(\\frac12\\)), write \\(\\ket{\\psi_1}\\). Show that \\(\\braket{\\psi_x}{\\psi_{x'}}=0\\) for \\(x\\neq x'\\) and \\(1\\) for \\(x=x'\\). Why does a graph given as a classical adjacency list of \\(N\\) vertices not automatically give an efficient walk step?"}, {"title": "6.3.2 Phase gap and spectral gap", "chapter": "Quantum Walks", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#topic-6-3-2", "text": "6.3.2 Phase gap and spectral gap § spectral gap · phase gap For a symmetric chain, write the eigenvalues of \\(P\\) as \\(1=\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\cdots\\). The spectral gap is \\(\\delta=1-\\lambda_2\\) (or, more carefully, one minus the second largest eigenvalue in absolute value). It controls classical mixing: the distribution approaches stationarity like \\((1-\\delta)^t\\), so mixing and hitting take order \\(1/\\delta\\) steps. Szegedy’s theorem relates the walk’s eigenvalues to \\(P\\)’s. Each eigenvalue \\(\\lambda=\\cos\\theta\\) of \\(D\\) gives a two-dimensional invariant plane on which \\(W\\) acts as a rotation by \\(2\\theta\\), with eigenvalues \\(e^{\\pm2i\\theta}\\). The eigenvalue \\(\\lambda_1=1\\) gives \\(\\theta=0\\), a stationary state. The next eigenvalue \\(\\lambda_2=1-\\delta\\) gives \\(\\cos\\theta=1-\\delta\\), so \\[\\theta\\approx\\sqrt{2\\delta}\\quad\\text{for small }\\delta,\\] using \\(\\cos\\theta\\approx1-\\theta^2/2\\). The phase gap , the smallest nonzero eigenphase of \\(W\\), is \\(2\\theta\\ge2\\sqrt\\delta\\). A gap of \\(\\delta\\) in the classical chain becomes a gap of order \\(\\sqrt\\delta\\) in the walk. This square root is the whole speedup. Phase estimation distinguishes the stationary eigenphase \\(0\\) from all others with precision of order \\(\\sqrt\\delta\\), which costs order \\(1/\\sqrt\\delta\\) walk steps, against \\(1/\\delta\\) classical steps. It is exactly the relation between amplitude amplification and repetition: \\(\\cos\\theta=1-\\delta\\) plays the role of \\(\\cos\\theta=\\sqrt{1-p}\\). Amplification is the special case of a walk on a two-state chain. Problems A chain has \\(\\lambda_2=0.99\\). Compare the classical \\(1/\\delta\\) with the quantum \\(1/\\sqrt\\delta\\). Compute the phase gap exactly for \\(\\lambda_2=0.5\\). Does a quadratically larger phase gap mean the quantum walk mixes to the uniform distribution quadratically faster?"}, {"title": "6.4.1 Searching with a walk", "chapter": "Quantum Walks", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#topic-6-4-1", "text": "6.4.1 Searching with a walk § walk search · hitting time Suppose a fraction \\(\\varepsilon\\) of the vertices of a graph are marked, and the classical walk has spectral gap \\(\\delta\\). Classically, a simple strategy is: walk for order \\(1/\\delta\\) steps to reach a nearly random vertex, check whether it is marked, and repeat about \\(1/\\varepsilon\\) times. Total cost about \\(\\frac1\\varepsilon\\cdot\\frac1\\delta\\) walk steps, an upper bound on the hitting time . The quantum version replaces both loops by their square roots. The framework of Magniez, Nayak, Roland, and Santha (2007) performs amplitude amplification in which the reflection about the stationary state is implemented by phase estimation on the walk \\(W\\), distinguishing phase \\(0\\) from phases at least \\(2\\sqrt\\delta\\). With setup cost \\(\\mathsf S\\) (prepare the stationary state), update cost \\(\\mathsf U\\) (one walk step), and checking cost \\(\\mathsf C\\) (test whether a vertex is marked), walk search finds a marked vertex at cost \\[\\mathsf S+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt\\varepsilon}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt\\delta}\\,\\mathsf U+\\mathsf C\\right).\\] Compare classical \\(\\mathsf S+\\frac1\\varepsilon\\big(\\frac1\\delta\\mathsf U+\\mathsf C\\big)\\). Both factors are square-rooted. On the complete graph, a random step already lands on a random vertex: \\(\\delta\\approx1\\), and with one marked item, \\(\\varepsilon=1/N\\). The formula gives \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\), and the walk reduces to Grover search. The value of the framework is on graphs where \\(\\delta\\) is small but steps are cheap and reuse previous work, which is exactly the situation in element distinctness . On a two-dimensional grid of \\(N\\) vertices, walk search finds a marked vertex in \\(O(\\sqrt N\\log N)\\) steps ( Ambainis, Kempe, and Rivosh, 2005 ), whereas a classical walk on the grid needs order \\(N\\log N\\). Problems A graph has \\(\\delta=10^{-2}\\) and \\(\\varepsilon=10^{-4}\\), with \\(\\mathsf U=\\mathsf C=1\\). Compare the classical and quantum costs (ignoring setup). Show that the formula reproduces Grover’s \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\) for the complete graph with one marked vertex. Why does the framework need the marked set to be checkable by a coherent procedure \\(\\mathsf C\\), not just recognizable after measurement?"}, {"title": "6.4.2 Element distinctness", "chapter": "Quantum Walks", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#topic-6-4-2", "text": "6.4.2 Element distinctness § element distinctness · Johnson graph Given oracle access to a list \\(T(1),\\ldots,T(N)\\), decide whether two entries are equal. This is element distinctness . Classically it needs order \\(N\\) queries. Grover search over all pairs needs \\(\\sqrt{N^2}=N\\) queries, no better. Ambainis found an algorithm with \\(O(N^{2/3})\\) queries ( Ambainis, 2004 ), and a matching lower bound of \\(\\Omega(N^{2/3})\\) is known. The walk is on the Johnson graph : vertices are subsets \\(S\\) of \\(\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}\\) of size \\(r\\), and two subsets are adjacent if they differ in one element. Each vertex stores the queried values \\(T(i)\\) for \\(i\\in S\\). A vertex is marked if its subset contains a colliding pair. The costs are: Setup: query \\(r\\) values: \\(\\mathsf S=r\\). Update: swap one element out and one in: \\(\\mathsf U=O(1)\\) queries, reusing the other \\(r-1\\) stored values. Check: the stored values already reveal a collision: \\(\\mathsf C=0\\) queries. Marked fraction: if one colliding pair exists, a random \\(r\\)-subset contains both with probability about \\((r/N)^2\\), so \\(\\varepsilon\\approx r^2/N^2\\). Spectral gap: the Johnson graph walk has \\(\\delta\\approx1/r\\). Substituting into the formula of Section 6.4.1, the total is \\[r+\\frac{N}{r}\\Big(\\sqrt r\\cdot O(1)+0\\Big)=r+\\frac{N}{\\sqrt r}.\\] Balancing the two terms with \\(r=N^{2/3}\\) gives \\(O(N^{2/3})\\) queries. The insight is reuse. Grover over pairs pays one query per check and finds nothing between checks. The walk keeps a large set of values in memory and changes it a little at a time, so each step is cheap and checking is free. The price is space: the algorithm stores \\(r=N^{2/3}\\) values coherently, and its gate count includes data-structure operations on that memory. The query count is optimal; the time and space costs depend on how that coherent memory is implemented. Problems For \\(N=10^6\\), compare \\(N\\), \\(N^{2/3}\\), and the subset size \\(r\\). What happens to the cost if we choose \\(r=\\sqrt N\\)? If \\(r=N\\)? Why does checking cost zero queries, and what does it cost in gates?"}, {"title": "6.4.3 Exponential speedups and limits", "chapter": "Quantum Walks", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#topic-6-4-3", "text": "6.4.3 Exponential speedups and limits § glued trees Most walk speedups are polynomial. One is exponential. In the glued trees problem, two binary trees of depth \\(n\\) are joined at their leaves by a random cycle, and we are given an oracle that returns the neighbours of a vertex, with vertices labelled by random strings. Starting at one root, find the other. A classical algorithm gets lost in the exponentially many middle vertices and provably needs exponentially many queries. A continuous-time quantum walk , viewed in a basis of “column” states that group vertices by distance from the root, behaves like a walk on a line of length \\(2n\\) and reaches the far root in polynomial time ( Childs et al., 2003 ). The glued-trees result is an oracle separation for a contrived problem; it has no known practical application. It shows that walks can exploit global structure, the symmetry of the graph, in a way classical exploration cannot. The limits are as important as the results. Walk algorithms are stated in query models with coherent neighbour access. Their time complexity includes the cost of implementing that access, the coherent data structures, and the precision of phase estimation inside the framework. A spectral gap must be known or bounded to set parameters. And on graphs that are classically easy to search, a walk cannot do better than a quadratic improvement in general. The walk operator \\(W\\) has an eigenvalue structure tied to a matrix \\(D\\) through \\(\\lambda=\\cos\\theta\\). In Chapter 8, that same relation, applied to a matrix encoded inside a unitary, becomes qubitization. First, Chapter 7 shows how to put a matrix that is not unitary inside a unitary at all. Problems Why do the glued trees defeat a classical algorithm that remembers every vertex it has visited? In the column basis, the walk behaves like a walk on a line of \\(2n+2\\) sites. Why does that give polynomial time? A claim states that a quantum walk solves a graph search problem “in \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\) time.” What should you check?"}, {"title": "Quantum Walks — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#exercise-1", "text": "For the coined Hadamard walk on the line starting at |0, 0⟩, compute the state after two steps and the position probabilities. After the coin on the one-step state: ½(|0,−1⟩ + |1,−1⟩ + |0,1⟩ − |1,1⟩). Shifting: ½(|0,−2⟩ + |1,0⟩ + |0,0⟩ − |1,2⟩). Positions −2, 0, 2 have probabilities 1/4, 1/2, 1/4, the same as classically. Differences from the classical walk appear from the third step on."}, {"title": "Quantum Walks — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#exercise-2", "text": "For the continuous walk on two vertices with H=X (γ=1), starting at vertex 0, find the first time at which the probability of vertex 1 equals 3/4. Can the classical continuous walk, with probability 1/2(1−e^(−2t)), ever reach 3/4? The quantum probability is sin²t, which equals 3/4 first at t=π/3 (sin(π/3)=√3/2). It reaches 1 at t=π/2 and then oscillates back. The classical probability 1/2(1−e^(−2t)) increases monotonically toward 1/2 and never exceeds it. The classical walk forgets its starting point and settles into the stationary distribution. The quantum walk is unitary, keeps oscillating, and has no stationary limit, so “run it long enough” is not a strategy. Quantum walk algorithms must choose their running time or randomize it."}, {"title": "Quantum Walks — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#exercise-3", "text": "List the eigenvalues of the adjacency matrix of the six-cycle, and of the simple random walk P=A/2 on it. Why does this classical walk never converge to the uniform distribution from a single starting vertex, and how does a lazy walk P'=1/2(I+P) fix it? The adjacency eigenvalues are 2,1,−1,−2,−1,1, so P has eigenvalues 1,1/2,−1/2,−1,−1/2,1/2. The eigenvalue −1 comes from the alternating mode k=3. The six-cycle is bipartite: from vertex 0 the walker is on even vertices after even steps and odd vertices after odd steps, so the distribution oscillates forever. The second-largest absolute eigenvalue is 1, so the gap is zero. The lazy walk has eigenvalues 1/2(1+λ)∈{1,3/4,1/4,0}, all nonnegative, with gap delta=1/4. Staying put half the time costs a factor of two in speed and removes the oscillation."}, {"title": "Quantum Walks — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#exercise-4", "text": "The continuous walk on a cycle of N = 4 vertices with γ = 1 starts at vertex 0. Using Fourier modes, find the amplitude at vertex 2 at time t. The amplitude is ¼ Σ_k (−1)^k e^{−iλ_k t} = ¼(e^{−2it} − 1 + e^{2it} − 1) = ½(cos 2t − 1) = −sin² t. So the probability at vertex 2 is sin⁴ t, reaching 1 at t = π/2: the walker transfers perfectly to the opposite vertex."}, {"title": "Quantum Walks — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#exercise-5", "text": "Run the Hadamard walk on the line for three steps from |0,0⟩, where coin 0 moves left. Find the position distribution and compare it with the classical walk. Compute mathbb E[x²] for both. What does the comparison say about using small examples to judge spreading speed? Applying H to the coin of the two-step state and shifting gives amplitudes at positions −3,−1,1,3 with probabilities 1/8,5/8,1/8,1/8. For example, |0,−1⟩ receives 1/2√2(1+1) from |1,0⟩ and |0,0⟩, giving amplitude 1/√2 and probability 1/2. The classical walk gives 1/8,3/8,3/8,1/8. The quantum walk is lopsided toward the left, because the Hadamard coin gives the |1⟩ branch a minus sign that causes cancellation on the right. Both distributions have mathbb E[x²]=3. After so few steps the ballistic T² growth is invisible: it emerges only for large T, where the quantum spread approaches 0.54 T against the classical √T. A few steps check the calculation; they do not show the scaling."}, {"title": "Quantum Walks — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#exercise-6", "text": "The path graph on three vertices 1−2−3 has adjacency eigenvalues √2,0,−√2, with eigenvectors 1/2(1,√2,1), 1/√2(1,0,−1), and 1/2(1,−√2,1). For the continuous walk e^(−iAt) starting at vertex 1, find the amplitude at vertex 3 as a function of t, and the first time it reaches probability 1. The overlaps of |1⟩ with the three eigenvectors are 1/2,1/√2,1/2. After time t, the third component is 1/2e^(−i√2t)·1/2+1/√2·(−1/√2)+1/2e^(i√2t)·1/2=1/2(cos√2t−1). The probability is 1/4(1−cos√2t)²=sin⁴(t/√2). It first equals 1 at t=π/√2, when cos√2t=−1: the walker is transferred from one end to the other with certainty. This perfect transfer happens because the eigenvalues are evenly spaced, so all relative phases align at once. On longer paths the eigenvalues are not commensurate, and transfer is imperfect."}, {"title": "Quantum Walks — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#exercise-7", "text": "Consider the lazy walk P=1/2I+1/4A on a cycle of N=16 vertices. Its eigenvalues are 1/2(1+cos(2π k/N)). Compute the spectral gap delta, the angle θ=arccosλ₂ of Szegedy’s walk, and compare 1/delta with 1/θ. How do the two scale with N? λ₂=1/2(1+cosπ/8)≈1/2(1+0.9239)=0.9619, so delta≈0.0381 and 1/delta≈26. Then θ=arccos0.9619≈0.277, close to √2delta≈0.276, and 1/θ≈3.6. For large N, delta≈π²/N², so classical mixing takes order N² steps, while θ≈√2π/N and phase estimation resolves the walk’s gap with order N steps. On the cycle the quadratic improvement is the familiar ballistic-versus-diffusive difference. It applies to resolving the stationary eigenphase, which is what walk search uses, not to producing a classical sample from the uniform distribution."}, {"title": "Quantum Walks — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#exercise-8", "text": "To “watch the quantum walk work,” a programmer measures the position after every step of the four-cycle Hadamard walk from |0,0⟩. The coherent walk reaches vertex 3 with certainty after three steps. What distribution over vertices does the monitored walk give after three steps, and why? Monitoring the position gives vertices 1 and 3 with probability 1/2 each, the same as the classical random walk. Each measurement keeps one position and erases the coherence between positions. The later steps then add probabilities along histories instead of amplitudes. The certainty of the coherent walk came from the cancellation at vertex 1 of the third step, which needs the two routes to vertex 1 to coexist as amplitudes. A walk algorithm must run coherently and measure only at the end; a measured walk is a classical walk with extra steps."}, {"title": "Quantum Walks — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#exercise-9", "text": "A walk on r-subsets of an N-element set has setup cost r, update cost 1, checking cost √r, marked fraction (r/N)², and spectral gap 1/r. Use the walk-search formula to find the optimal r and the total cost. The cost is r + (N/r)(√r·1 + √r) = r + 2N/√r, minimized when r ≈ N^{2/3}, giving O(N^{2/3}). Checking at cost √r does not change the order here because it is no larger than the update term √r·U. If checking cost grew faster than √r, it would dominate and change the optimum."}, {"title": "Quantum Walks — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-quantum-walks.html#exercise-10", "text": "Before the walk algorithm, element distinctness had a simpler quantum algorithm: query a random set S of r values and store them, then Grover-search the remaining items for one whose value appears in S, and wrap the whole procedure in amplitude amplification. (a) Estimate the success probability of one run when exactly one colliding pair exists. (b) Show that the amplified cost is about √(N/r) (r+√N) and optimize r. (c) Compare with the walk’s N^(2/3), and identify what the walk reuses that this algorithm does not. (a) The run succeeds if S contains one member of the colliding pair, which happens with probability about 2r/N; Grover search then finds the other member with about √N queries. (b) One run costs r+O(√N) queries, and amplification needs about √(N/r) repetitions. The total is √(N/r) (r+√N)=√Nr+N/√r. The two terms balance at r=√N, giving O(N^(3/4)) queries. (c) N^(3/4) lies between the classical N and the walk’s optimal N^(2/3). This algorithm pays the full setup cost r inside every amplification round. The walk instead keeps its stored set and changes it by one element per step, so it pays the setup cost r only once, and each update costs O(1)."}, {"title": "7.1.1 Matrices that are not unitary", "chapter": "Operator Embedding", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#topic-7-1-1", "text": "7.1.1 Matrices that are not unitary § non-unitary matrix Suppose we want to multiply a qubit’s amplitude vector by \\[A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&\\tfrac12\\end{pmatrix}.\\] On \\(\\ket0\\) it does nothing; on \\(\\ket1\\) it halves the amplitude. A unitary preserves the length of every vector, but \\(A\\ket1=\\frac12\\ket1\\) has length \\(\\frac12\\). So no unitary on this qubit equals \\(A\\), even up to a phase: \\(A\\) is a non-unitary matrix . Worse, \\(A\\) shrinks different inputs by different amounts: \\(A\\ket+=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0+\\frac12\\ket1)\\) has squared length \\(\\frac58\\). Normalizing the output afterwards does not make a linear operation. To describe how much a matrix can stretch, define the operator norm , introduced in Section 3.4.3 for errors: \\[\\|A\\|=\\max_{\\|\\psi\\|=1}\\|A\\ket\\psi\\|.\\] For our diagonal \\(A\\), \\(\\|A\\|=1\\), attained on \\(\\ket0\\). For a diagonal matrix it is the largest absolute entry; for a Hermitian matrix it is the largest absolute eigenvalue; in general it is the largest singular value , a notion Chapter 8 develops. A unitary has \\(\\|U\\|=1\\) and also shrinks nothing. The obstacle, then, is that we want to apply a linear map that changes lengths, using only length-preserving operations. The idea that resolves it is to let the missing length go somewhere else: into a branch of a larger system that we later discard or recycle. Problems Find \\(\\|A\\|\\) for \\(A=\\mathrm{diag}(3,-5)\\) and for \\(A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&2\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}\\). Show that if \\(A\\) is unitary then \\(\\|A\\psi\\|=\\|\\psi\\|\\) for every \\(\\psi\\), and give a non-unitary \\(A\\) with \\(\\|A\\|=1\\). A student proposes to “apply \\(A\\)” by applying a unitary \\(U\\) and then renormalizing. Why can no unitary \\(U\\) satisfy \\(U\\ket\\psi\\propto A\\ket\\psi\\) for every \\(\\psi\\), with \\(A=\\mathrm{diag}(1,\\frac12)\\)?"}, {"title": "7.1.2 Block encodings", "chapter": "Operator Embedding", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#topic-7-1-2", "text": "7.1.2 Block encodings § block encoding · subnormalization Add one ancilla qubit. Order the basis with the ancilla first, so a two-qubit matrix splits into four \\(2\\times2\\) blocks according to the ancilla’s input and output values. Take \\[U=\\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\\\B&-A\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&\\tfrac12\\end{pmatrix},\\quad B=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Because \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are diagonal they commute, and \\(A^2+B^2=I\\). Multiplying out, \\[U^2=\\begin{pmatrix}A^2+B^2&AB-BA\\\\BA-AB&B^2+A^2\\end{pmatrix}=I\\], so \\(U\\) is unitary (and its own inverse). On an input with the ancilla in \\(\\ket0\\), \\[U\\,\\ket0\\ket\\psi=\\ket0\\,A\\ket\\psi+\\ket1\\,B\\ket\\psi.\\] The ancilla-\\(0\\) branch carries exactly \\(A\\ket\\psi\\). The length that \\(A\\) removed has gone into the ancilla-\\(1\\) branch, where \\(B\\ket\\psi\\) supplies it: \\(\\|A\\psi\\|^2+\\|B\\psi\\|^2=1\\). In general, a unitary \\(U\\) on \\(a\\) ancilla qubits and a system is a block encoding of a matrix \\(A\\) if \\[(\\bra{0^a}\\otimes I)\\,U\\,(\\ket{0^a}\\otimes I)=\\frac{A}{\\alpha}.\\] The top-left block of \\(U\\) is \\(A/\\alpha\\). The number \\(\\alpha\\ge\\|A\\|\\) is the subnormalization : a block of a unitary cannot stretch any vector, so it must have norm at most \\(1\\), and a matrix with larger norm must be scaled down. If the block equals \\(A/\\alpha\\) only to within \\(\\epsilon\\) in operator norm , \\(U\\) is an \\((\\alpha,a,\\epsilon)\\)-block encoding. Every later chapter that processes matrices assumes block encodings as input, so \\(\\alpha\\), \\(a\\), \\(\\epsilon\\), and the gate cost of \\(U\\) are the parameters a resource estimate must report. Problems Verify \\(U\\ket0\\ket+\\) for the example, and check that the two branches have total squared length one. Construct a one-ancilla block encoding of \\(A=\\mathrm{diag}(a_0,a_1)\\) for any real \\(|a_0|,|a_1|\\le1\\). Can \\(A=2I\\) on one qubit have a block encoding with \\(\\alpha=1\\)? What is the smallest possible \\(\\alpha\\)?"}, {"title": "7.1.3 Postselection and success probability", "chapter": "Operator Embedding", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#topic-7-1-3", "text": "7.1.3 Postselection and success probability § postselection A block encoding applies \\(A/\\alpha\\) only on the ancilla-\\(0\\) branch. To obtain \\(A\\ket\\psi\\) as a state, measure the ancilla and keep the run if the outcome is \\(0\\). This is postselection . The probability of success is \\[p_{\\text{succ}}=\\left\\|\\frac{A\\ket\\psi}{\\alpha}\\right\\|^2,\\] and on success the system holds the normalized state \\(A\\ket\\psi/\\|A\\ket\\psi\\|\\). In the example with \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket+\\), success has probability \\(\\frac58\\) and leaves \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt5}(2\\ket0+\\ket1)\\). With input \\(\\ket1\\), success has probability \\(\\frac14\\) and leaves \\(\\ket1\\): the output state is the same as the input, but it was obtained only a quarter of the time. That input dependence is how a non-unitary map shows up in a unitary world: it is paid for in probability. Without further help, the expected number of attempts is \\(1/p_{\\text{succ}}=\\alpha^2/\\|A\\psi\\|^2\\), and each failure destroys the input state, which must be prepared again. If the preparation is a known circuit, amplitude amplification (Chapter 5) reduces the cost to order \\(\\alpha/\\|A\\psi\\|\\) uses of \\(U\\), the preparation, and their inverses. Either way, two quantities control the cost: the subnormalization \\(\\alpha\\), and how much \\(A\\) shrinks the particular input. A block encoding with an unnecessarily large \\(\\alpha\\) wastes success probability quadratically. Problems For the example, which input maximizes the success probability, and which minimizes it? A block encoding has \\(\\alpha=100\\), and the input satisfies \\(\\|A\\psi\\|=1\\). Compare repeat-until-success with amplitude amplification. “Postselection lets a quantum computer apply any linear map.” Explain why this is true but misleading."}, {"title": "7.2.1 PREPARE and SELECT", "chapter": "Operator Embedding", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#topic-7-2-1", "text": "7.2.1 PREPARE and SELECT § linear combination of unitaries · PREPARE · SELECT Let \\(A=\\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}a_jU_j\\) with unitaries \\(U_j\\) and coefficients \\(a_j>0\\) (signs and phases can be absorbed into the \\(U_j\\)). Put \\(\\alpha=\\sum_ja_j\\). Two circuits do the work: PREPARE acts on an ancilla register of \\(\\lceil\\log_2L\\rceil\\) qubits: \\(\\mathrm{PREP}\\ket0=\\sum_j\\sqrt{a_j/\\alpha}\\,\\ket j\\). SELECT applies the \\(j\\)th unitary controlled on the ancilla: \\(\\mathrm{SEL}=\\sum_j\\ket j\\bra j\\otimes U_j\\). The circuit \\(W=(\\mathrm{PREP}^\\dagger\\otimes I)\\,\\mathrm{SEL}\\,(\\mathrm{PREP}\\otimes I)\\) is a block encoding of \\(A/\\alpha\\). To see this, compute the ancilla-\\(0\\) block: \\[(\\bra0\\otimes I)W(\\ket0\\otimes I)=\\sum_{j,k}\\overline{\\braket{k}{\\mathrm{PREP}\\,0}}\\,\\braket{j}{\\mathrm{PREP}\\,0}\\,\\braket kj\\,U_j=\\sum_j\\frac{a_j}{\\alpha}U_j=\\frac A\\alpha.\\] The ancilla is prepared in a superposition of term labels; SELECT applies every term on its own branch; un-preparing projects back onto the starting state, and each term’s amplitude \\(\\sqrt{a_j/\\alpha}\\) appears twice, once going in and once coming out, giving weight \\(a_j/\\alpha\\). This is the linear combination of unitaries (LCU) method of Childs and Wiebe (2012) . Its subnormalization is \\(\\alpha=\\sum_j|a_j|\\), the 1-norm of the coefficients, which is at least \\(\\|A\\|\\) and can be much larger when terms partly cancel. Its cost is one PREPARE , one PREPARE\\(^\\dagger\\), and one SELECT. SELECT on \\(L\\) terms is a multiplexed controlled operation; built naively, it costs about \\(L\\) controlled unitaries plus the overhead of decoding \\(j\\). Problems For \\(A=0.6\\,U_0+0.2\\,U_1+0.2\\,U_2+0\\,U_3\\), give \\(\\alpha\\) and the PREPARE state. Why are the PREPARE amplitudes \\(\\sqrt{a_j/\\alpha}\\) rather than \\(a_j/\\alpha\\)? Two terms \\(U_0=I\\) and \\(U_1=-I\\) with \\(a_0=a_1=\\frac12\\). What is \\(A\\), and what is the subnormalization?"}, {"title": "7.2.2 A worked sum", "chapter": "Operator Embedding", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#topic-7-2-2", "text": "7.2.2 A worked sum § coherent sum Take \\(A=\\frac12(I+X)\\) on one system qubit, so \\(a_0=a_1=\\frac12\\), \\(\\alpha=1\\). PREPARE is \\(H\\) on one ancilla. SELECT is a controlled-\\(X\\) from ancilla to system. The block encoding is \\(H\\), CNOT, \\(H\\). Predict first. Input the system in \\(\\ket0\\). With what probability does the ancilla read \\(0\\), and what is the system state then? Track the state. \\(H\\) on the ancilla gives \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0+\\ket1)\\ket0\\). CNOT gives \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0\\ket0+\\ket1\\ket1)\\). \\(H\\) on the ancilla gives \\(\\frac12\\big(\\ket0(\\ket0+\\ket1)+\\ket1(\\ket0-\\ket1)\\big)\\). The ancilla-\\(0\\) branch is \\(\\frac12(\\ket0+\\ket1)=A\\ket0\\). Contrast this with choosing randomly: with probability \\(\\frac12\\) apply \\(I\\), otherwise apply \\(X\\). On \\(\\ket0\\) that produces \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket1\\) at random, a classical mixture, which a measurement in the \\(\\pm\\) basis gives \\(+\\) only half the time. The LCU output \\(\\ket+\\) gives \\(+\\) every time. The difference is that LCU adds amplitudes coherently, \\(\\frac12\\ket0+\\frac12\\ket1\\), while random selection adds probabilities. A coherent sum can interfere; a random choice cannot. Problems Apply the same circuit to the system input \\(\\ket-\\). What happens? Build a block encoding of \\(\\frac12(I-X)\\) by changing one gate. A colleague implements “\\(\\frac12(I+X)\\)” by flipping a classical coin to decide whether to apply \\(X\\), and reports that it matches LCU on computational-basis measurements. Why is the test insufficient?"}, {"title": "7.2.3 Hamiltonians as sums of Pauli strings", "chapter": "Operator Embedding", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#topic-7-2-3", "text": "7.2.3 Hamiltonians as sums of Pauli strings § Pauli string · 1-norm The most common application is a Hamiltonian written as a weighted sum of Pauli strings , tensor products of \\(I,X,Y,Z\\): \\[H=\\sum_jh_jP_j.\\] Every Hermitian matrix on \\(n\\) qubits can be written this way, with real \\(h_j\\), and physical Hamiltonians usually have only polynomially many terms. Each \\(P_j\\) is unitary and self-inverse. Write \\(h_jP_j=|h_j|\\cdot(\\mathrm{sign}(h_j)P_j)\\), absorbing the sign into the unitary. LCU gives a block encoding of \\(H/\\lambda\\) with \\[\\lambda=\\sum_j|h_j|,\\] the 1-norm of the coefficients. For example, \\(H=0.5\\,Z\\otimes Z-0.3\\,X\\otimes I\\) has \\(\\lambda=0.8\\). PREPARE maps \\(\\ket0\\) to \\(\\sqrt{5/8}\\ket0+\\sqrt{3/8}\\ket1\\); SELECT applies \\(Z\\otimes Z\\) on ancilla \\(0\\) and \\(-X\\otimes I\\) on ancilla \\(1\\). The 1-norm \\(\\lambda\\) is the quantity that every later algorithm pays for. Simulating \\(e^{-iHt}\\) by the methods of Chapters 8 and 9 costs about \\(\\lambda t\\) uses of the block encoding, and phase estimation of energies to precision \\(\\epsilon\\) costs about \\(\\lambda/\\epsilon\\). For molecules, \\(\\lambda\\) can be far larger than \\(\\|H\\|\\), and much research on quantum chemistry algorithms reduces \\(\\lambda\\) by better representations of the same Hamiltonian, for example by factorizing the two-electron terms. Problems Find \\(\\lambda\\) and \\(\\|H\\|\\) for \\(H=Z\\otimes I+I\\otimes Z\\). Find \\(\\lambda\\) and \\(\\|H\\|\\) for \\(H=X\\otimes X+Y\\otimes Y+Z\\otimes Z\\). Show that \\(H=X+Z\\) on one qubit has \\(\\lambda>\\|H\\|\\), and generalize to a sum of \\(k\\) mutually anticommuting Pauli strings with equal coefficients."}, {"title": "7.3.1 Products of encoded matrices", "chapter": "Operator Embedding", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#topic-7-3-1", "text": "7.3.1 Products of encoded matrices § product of block encodings Let \\(U_A\\) be a block encoding of \\(A/\\alpha\\) with \\(a\\) ancillas and \\(U_B\\) one of \\(B/\\beta\\) with \\(b\\) ancillas. Apply \\(U_B\\), then \\(U_A\\), each with its own ancilla register. On the branch where both registers are \\(0\\) at the end, \\[(\\bra{0^a}\\bra{0^b}\\otimes I)\\,U_AU_B\\,(\\ket{0^a}\\ket{0^b}\\otimes I)=\\frac{A}{\\alpha}\\cdot\\frac{B}{\\beta}.\\] The reason is that \\(U_A\\) does not touch the \\(b\\) register, so the projection onto \\(\\ket{0^b}\\) can be moved past it and applied right after \\(U_B\\), and similarly for \\(\\ket{0^a}\\). This is a product of block encodings , with subnormalization \\(\\alpha\\beta\\) and \\(a+b\\) ancillas. Reusing a single ancilla register for both is tempting, since it saves qubits, but it is generally wrong. After \\(U_B\\), the ancilla is not in \\(\\ket0\\) on the failure branch, and \\(U_A\\) can map part of that failure amplitude back into the ancilla-\\(0\\) block, adding unwanted terms to the product. (Qubitization in Chapter 8 does reuse ancillas, but only with a reflection in between that makes the reuse correct.) Subnormalizations multiply, so long products lose success probability quickly: a product of \\(k\\) encodings each with \\(\\alpha=2\\) has subnormalization \\(2^k\\). Algorithms that apply polynomials of a matrix avoid this by the more economical constructions of Chapter 8. Problems Using the example \\(U\\) of Section 7.1.2 twice, with separate ancillas, what matrix is encoded, and with what success probability on input \\(\\ket1\\)? Use the single-ancilla example \\(U\\) twice on the same ancilla (no measurement between). What is encoded in the ancilla-\\(0\\) block? Encode \\(A^{10}\\) for \\(A\\) with subnormalization \\(\\alpha=1.5\\) by repeated products. What is the resulting subnormalization, and why is that a problem?"}, {"title": "7.3.2 Error propagation", "chapter": "Operator Embedding", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#topic-7-3-2", "text": "7.3.2 Error propagation § approximation error Real block encodings are approximate: rotation angles are synthesized to finite precision, and PREPARE states are loaded approximately. If the blocks are \\(A/\\alpha+E\\) and \\(B/\\beta+F\\) with \\(\\|E\\|\\le\\epsilon\\) and \\(\\|F\\|\\le\\delta\\), the product block is \\[\\Big(\\frac A\\alpha+E\\Big)\\Big(\\frac B\\beta+F\\Big)=\\frac{AB}{\\alpha\\beta}+\\frac A\\alpha F+E\\frac B\\beta+EF.\\] Since \\(\\|A/\\alpha\\|,\\|B/\\beta\\|\\le1\\), the approximation error is at most \\(\\delta+\\epsilon+\\epsilon\\delta\\). For small errors, errors add, just as for unitaries in Section 3.4.3. Two error sources are easy to miss. First, a PREPARE state with amplitude errors changes the encoded coefficients: if each \\(\\sqrt{a_j/\\alpha}\\) is off by \\(\\eta\\), each weight \\(a_j/\\alpha\\) is off by about \\(2\\eta\\sqrt{a_j/\\alpha}\\), and the block error is bounded by summing these over terms. Second, errors relative to \\(A/\\alpha\\) are errors relative to \\(A\\) multiplied by \\(\\alpha\\): to apply \\(A\\) itself with error \\(\\epsilon_A\\), the block must be accurate to \\(\\epsilon_A/\\alpha\\). Large subnormalizations demand high precision as well as many repetitions. Problems Two block encodings each have error \\(10^{-3}\\). Bound the error of their product. A block encoding of \\(H/\\lambda\\) with \\(\\lambda=100\\) has block error \\(10^{-4}\\). What is the error in the encoded \\(H\\)? Why does the bound \\(\\|(A/\\alpha)F\\|\\le\\|F\\|\\) require \\(\\|A/\\alpha\\|\\le1\\), and what happens to the argument for a product of a block with an un-normalized matrix?"}, {"title": "7.3.3 Oblivious amplitude amplification", "chapter": "Operator Embedding", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#topic-7-3-3", "text": "7.3.3 Oblivious amplitude amplification § oblivious amplitude amplification Postselection fails sometimes, and amplitude amplification fixes that only if we can reflect about the input state, which requires a preparation circuit for it. What if the input is an unknown state handed to us by an earlier part of the algorithm? When the encoded matrix is proportional to a unitary, there is a way out. Suppose \\(W\\ket{0}\\ket\\psi=\\sin\\theta\\,\\ket0V\\ket\\psi+\\cos\\theta\\,\\ket{\\Phi^\\perp}\\) for a unitary \\(V\\), a fixed \\(\\theta\\), and some state \\(\\ket{\\Phi^\\perp}\\) with the ancilla orthogonal to \\(\\ket0\\), for every \\(\\psi\\). Then the reflection about the starting state can be replaced by the reflection \\(R=(2\\ket0\\bra0-I)\\otimes I\\) about the ancilla being \\(0\\), which does not depend on \\(\\psi\\). The iteration \\(-WRW^\\dagger R\\) rotates by \\(2\\theta\\) in a two-dimensional space that, remarkably, behaves the same for every input. This is oblivious amplitude amplification ( Berry, Childs, Cleve, Kothari, and Somma, 2014 ). A clean special case: if \\(\\sin\\theta=\\frac12\\), one iteration gives exactly \\(\\ket0V\\ket\\psi\\), since \\(3\\theta=\\pi/2\\). LCU makes this easy to arrange. If \\(V\\approx\\sum_ja_jU_j\\) with \\(\\sum_ja_j=2\\), the LCU block encoding has \\(\\sin\\theta=\\frac12\\), and one round of oblivious amplification applies \\(V\\) deterministically, with three uses of the LCU circuit. This is how truncated Taylor series simulate \\(e^{-iHt}\\) in short time steps: the series is a linear combination of unitaries whose sum is nearly unitary. Problems For \\(\\sin\\theta=\\frac12\\), verify that one iteration of a rotation by \\(2\\theta\\) reaches success probability one. Why is it essential that \\(V\\) be unitary (up to the scale \\(\\sin\\theta\\))? The encoded matrix is \\(\\frac12(V+E)\\) with \\(V\\) unitary and a small error \\(\\|E\\|\\le\\epsilon\\). What can be said about one round of oblivious amplification?"}, {"title": "7.4.1 Sparse access", "chapter": "Operator Embedding", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#topic-7-4-1", "text": "7.4.1 Sparse access § sparse access A matrix is \\(s\\)-sparse if each row and column has at most \\(s\\) nonzero entries. Sparse access provides two oracles: one that, given a row \\(i\\) and an index \\(k\\le s\\), returns the column of the \\(k\\)th nonzero entry in that row, and one that, given \\(i,j\\), returns the entry \\(H_{ij}\\) to some number of bits. For a matrix defined by a rule, such as a lattice Hamiltonian or a graph’s adjacency matrix, both oracles are efficient circuits. From these oracles a block encoding of \\(H/(s\\,\\|H\\|_{\\max})\\) can be built with a constant number of oracle calls, where \\(\\|H\\|_{\\max}\\) is the largest entry magnitude. The construction prepares, for each row, a uniform superposition over its \\(s\\) nonzero positions, and uses the entry oracle to rotate an ancilla by an angle encoding \\(H_{ij}\\). The subnormalization \\(s\\|H\\|_{\\max}\\) is at least \\(\\|H\\|\\) and can exceed it by a factor up to \\(s\\), for example when each row has one large entry and many tiny ones. Sparse access is the setting of many simulation and linear-systems results. It is natural when the matrix has a short description. It is not free when the matrix is stored as data: then the oracles must read memory coherently, which is the subject of the next topic. Problems The adjacency matrix of a graph where every vertex has degree at most 3 has entries \\(0\\) or \\(1\\). What block-encoding subnormalization does sparse access give, and what is \\(\\|H\\|\\) at most? For the one-dimensional Hamiltonian \\(H=\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}Z_jZ_{j+1}\\), compare the LCU 1-norm with \\(s\\|H\\|_{\\max}\\). Why must the entry oracle be implemented reversibly, and what does that require when \\(H_{ij}\\) is given by a formula?"}, {"title": "7.4.2 Classical data and dequantization", "chapter": "Operator Embedding", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#topic-7-4-2", "text": "7.4.2 Classical data and dequantization § QRAM · dequantization Suppose the matrix is an arbitrary \\(N\\times N\\) table of numbers stored classically. A block encoding needs coherent access to it: a circuit that, on a superposition of addresses, returns the stored values in superposition. Such a device is often called QRAM . Its circuit size grows at least linearly with the amount of data stored, though its depth can be logarithmic. Either way, loading the data once costs order \\(N^2\\) for a dense matrix, which already matches or exceeds many classical algorithms on it. Claims of exponential speedup for problems on classical data, such as recommendation systems, principal component analysis, or regression, usually assume that the data is already available through a QRAM-like structure in which it can also be sampled efficiently. Tang (2018) showed that when a classical algorithm is given the comparable sampling access to the same data, it can match the quantum recommendation algorithm up to polynomial factors. This dequantization program has since removed the exponential gap for several proposed quantum machine-learning algorithms with low-rank inputs, while leaving large polynomial gaps in some cases. The lesson is the one stated in Chapter 1: a speedup is a comparison between methods with compatible access. A block encoding is a precise and powerful interface, and it makes clear what an algorithm consumes. Whether supplying that interface is cheap depends on the matrix. With a block encoding in hand, the next chapter shows how to apply nearly any bounded function of the encoded matrix, polynomial by polynomial. Problems A quantum linear-systems algorithm runs in time polylogarithmic in \\(N\\) given a block encoding of a dense \\(N\\times N\\) matrix. Why is the end-to-end cost from a classical file at least order \\(N^2\\)? What sampling access did Tang’s classical algorithm assume, and why is it a fair comparison? Does dequantization show that quantum linear algebra offers no advantage?"}, {"title": "Operator Embedding — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#exercise-1", "text": "Give an LCU block encoding of A = (I + Z)/2 on one qubit and the success probability for input |+⟩. PREPARE = H on an ancilla, SELECT = controlled-Z from the ancilla to the system, then H on the ancilla; keep outcome 0. A|+⟩ = |0⟩/√2, so the success probability is 1/2, and the output is |0⟩."}, {"title": "Operator Embedding — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#exercise-2", "text": "The shear A=beginpmatrix1&1\\0&1endpmatrix has both eigenvalues equal to 1. Compute |A| by maximizing |Aψ|² over unit vectors, and state the smallest subnormalization α any block encoding of A can have. A† A=beginpmatrix1&1\\1&2endpmatrix has eigenvalues (3±√5)/2. So |A|²=(3+√5)/2≈2.618 and |A|=(1+√5)/2≈1.618. A block of a unitary has norm at most 1, so any block encoding needs α≥1.618. For a matrix that is not Hermitian (or, more generally, not normal), the eigenvalues do not determine the norm. Here every eigenvalue has magnitude 1, yet the matrix stretches some vectors by 62%. Resource estimates must use the norm, or a bound on it, not the eigenvalues."}, {"title": "Operator Embedding — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#exercise-3", "text": "Write H=beginpmatrix1&2\\2&−1endpmatrix as a sum of Pauli matrices. Find the LCU subnormalization λ and the norm |H|. By what factor does the excess subnormalization reduce the postselection success probability compared with an ideal encoding with α=|H|? H=2X+Z, so λ=2+1=3. The eigenvalues are ±√(4+1)=±√5, so |H|=√5≈2.236. Postselection succeeds with probability |Hψ|²/α², so the ratio is (λ/|H|)²=9/5=1.8: the LCU encoding succeeds 1.8 times less often on every input. The excess arises because X and Z anticommute and their contributions add in quadrature, not linearly."}, {"title": "Operator Embedding — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#exercise-4", "text": "A Hamiltonian H = 2 Z₁ + Z₁Z₂ − 0.5 X₂ is block-encoded by LCU. What is λ, what is the success probability of postselecting H|00⟩/λ, and what state results? λ = 2 + 1 + 0.5 = 3.5. H|00⟩ = 2|00⟩ + |00⟩ − 0.5|01⟩ = 3|00⟩ − 0.5|01⟩, with squared norm 9.25. The success probability is 9.25/12.25 ≈ 0.755, and the output is (3|00⟩ − 0.5|01⟩)/√9.25."}, {"title": "Operator Embedding — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#exercise-5", "text": "Expand the projector Pi=|00⟩⟨00| on two qubits as a sum of Pauli strings, using |0⟩⟨0|=1/2(I+Z). Give the LCU subnormalization and the PREPARE state. Then compute the postselection success probability on input |++⟩ and the resulting state. Pi=1/4(I⊗ I+I⊗ Z+Z⊗ I+Z⊗ Z). There are four terms of weight 1/4, so λ=1=|Pi| and the encoding is as efficient as possible. PREPARE is the uniform superposition over two label qubits, H⊗ H, and SELECT applies I, Z₂, Z₁, or Z₁Z₂ according to the label. On |++⟩, Pi|++⟩=1/2|00⟩, so postselection succeeds with probability 1/4 and leaves |00⟩. The same probability results from measuring |++⟩ in the computational basis. A block-encoded projector applied to a state behaves like a filtering measurement that keeps only the successful branch."}, {"title": "Operator Embedding — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#exercise-6", "text": "Let A=1/2(U+V) for unitaries U,V, encoded by LCU with one ancilla: H, controlled-U on ancilla 0 and controlled-V on ancilla 1, then H. Show that the success probability on input |ψ⟩ is 1/2(1+Re⟨ψ| U† V|ψ⟩). Which circuit from Chapter 3 is this, and when is the success probability 0? The success probability is |Aψ|²=1/4⟨ Uψ+Vψ,Uψ+Vψ⟩=1/4(2+2 Re⟨ψ| U† V|ψ⟩)=1/2(1+Re⟨ψ| U† V|ψ⟩). That is exactly Pr[0] of the Hadamard test applied to U† V on |ψ⟩. With U=I the circuit is the Hadamard test itself, so the Hadamard test is a two-term LCU in disguise. The success probability is 0 when V|ψ⟩=−U|ψ⟩: the two terms cancel completely on that input, and A|ψ⟩=0. Cancellation between terms is why |A| can be much smaller than the 1-norm."}, {"title": "Operator Embedding — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#exercise-7", "text": "An LCU encodes V/α for a unitary V with α=1.9. (a) What success probability does one round of oblivious amplitude amplification give? (b) Show how adding a cancelling pair of terms makes α=2 exactly without changing the encoded matrix, and state the cost. (a) sinθ=1/1.9≈0.526, so θ≈0.554, and one round gives sin²(3θ)≈sin²(1.663)≈0.992, not certainty. (b) Add the terms 0.05 I and 0.05 (−I). They sum to zero, so the encoded matrix is still V, but the 1-norm becomes 1.9+0.1=2. Then sinθ=1/2, 3θ=π/2, and one round succeeds exactly. The cost is two extra PREPARE amplitudes and two more (trivial) branches of SELECT. Deliberately increasing the subnormalization, normally something to avoid, here buys determinism, because oblivious amplification needs a particular angle."}, {"title": "Operator Embedding — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#exercise-8", "text": "After postselecting a block encoding of A=diag(1,1/2) on input |ψ⟩, a student writes “the register now holds A|ψ⟩.” For |ψ⟩=|1⟩, what does the register hold? Where did the factor 1/2 go, and why does this matter for an algorithm whose answer is the norm |Aψ|? A quantum state is always normalized, so the register holds A|1⟩/|A|1⟩|=|1⟩, the same as the input. The factor 1/2 appears only in the success probability, |A|1⟩|²=1/4. The scale of Aψ is not stored in the state; it shows up in how often the procedure succeeds. If an algorithm needs the norm, for example the length of a solution vector in a linear system, it must estimate the success probability, by repetition (order 1/ε² runs) or by amplitude estimation (order 1/ε). That cost belongs in the resource count."}, {"title": "Operator Embedding — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#exercise-9", "text": "The first-order Taylor approximation e^{−iHδ} ≈ I − iδH, with H = Σ h_j P_j and λδ small, is a linear combination of unitaries. Write its LCU coefficients and subnormalization, and explain why oblivious amplitude amplification can be used to apply it deterministically. I − iδH = 1·I + Σ_j δ|h_j|·(−i sign(h_j)P_j), a combination of unitaries with coefficients 1 and δ|h_j|, so α = 1 + δλ. The encoded matrix (I − iδH)/α is close to a unitary (e^{−iHδ}/α, up to O(δ²)), so its success amplitude is nearly input independent. Choosing the step so that α = 2 (or padding the combination to α = 2) makes one round of oblivious amplitude amplification succeed, up to the O(δ²) truncation error. Higher-order truncated Taylor series reduce this error, which is the basis of the Berry–Childs–Cleve–Kothari–Somma simulation method."}, {"title": "Operator Embedding — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-operator-embedding.html#exercise-10", "text": "The star graph has one centre joined to N leaves. (a) Find |A| for its adjacency matrix. (b) What subnormalization does the sparse-access construction give, with sparsity s=N and |A|_(max)=1? (c) Compute both for N=9 and explain the factor between them. What does this mean for using sparse access on graphs with a few high-degree vertices? (a) A vector with a on the centre and b on each leaf is mapped to Nb on the centre and a on each leaf. It is an eigenvector when Nb=λ a and a=λ b, so λ²=N, and |A|=√N. (b) Sparse access gives α=s|A|_(max)=N. (c) For N=9, |A|=3 while α=9. The construction spreads amplitude uniformly over up to s neighbours of every row, as if each row could be as heavy as the heaviest, so it overestimates by a factor √N. Success probabilities drop by a further factor N. Since simulation and phase-estimation costs scale with α, one high-degree vertex makes the encoding expensive for the whole graph. Splitting the matrix into pieces, or using encodings adapted to row norms, can recover most of the loss."}, {"title": "8.1.1 The signal rotation", "chapter": "Polynomial Transformations", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#topic-8-1-1", "text": "8.1.1 The signal rotation § signal operator · Chebyshev polynomial Let \\(x\\in[-1,1]\\) be an unknown number, the signal , and suppose we can apply the one-qubit unitary \\[W(x)=\\begin{pmatrix}x&i\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\\\i\\sqrt{1-x^2}&x\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Writing \\(x=\\cos\\theta\\), this is \\(W=e^{i\\theta X}\\), a rotation about the \\(X\\) axis by an angle that encodes \\(x\\). We call \\(W(x)\\) the signal operator . Its top-left entry, \\(\\bra0W\\ket0=x\\), is a one-qubit block encoding of the number \\(x\\). What does applying \\(W\\) twice encode? Multiply the matrices: the top-left entry of \\(W^2\\) is \\(x\\cdot x+(i\\sqrt{1-x^2})(i\\sqrt{1-x^2})=2x^2-1\\). It is not \\(x^2\\). The second application lets amplitude that left \\(\\ket0\\) on the first call return, and it returns with a minus sign. Since \\(W^d=e^{id\\theta X}\\), the top-left entry of \\(W^d\\) is \\(\\cos(d\\theta)\\): \\[\\bra0W(x)^d\\ket0=T_d(x)=\\cos(d\\arccos x).\\] These are the Chebyshev polynomials : \\(T_0=1\\), \\(T_1=x\\), \\(T_2=2x^2-1\\), \\(T_3=4x^3-3x\\), and in general \\(T_{d+1}=2xT_d-T_{d-1}\\). Each is a genuine polynomial in \\(x\\) of degree \\(d\\), bounded by \\(1\\) on \\([-1,1]\\), and oscillating between \\(\\pm1\\) there. So \\(d\\) uses of a block encoding of \\(x\\) give a block encoding of a degree-\\(d\\) polynomial of \\(x\\). Chebyshev polynomials are only one family. The next topic shows how inserting phase rotations between the calls reaches many others. Problems Verify \\(T_3(x)=4x^3-3x\\) from the recurrence, and check \\(T_3(\\cos\\theta)=\\cos3\\theta\\) at \\(\\theta=\\pi/3\\). Evaluate the top-left entry of \\(W^2\\) at \\(x=0,\\frac12,1\\). Why can no sequence of \\(d\\) calls to \\(W(x)\\), with any fixed unitaries between them, produce a top-left entry \\(2x\\) for all \\(x\\in[-1,1]\\)?"}, {"title": "8.1.2 Phase sequences", "chapter": "Polynomial Transformations", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#topic-8-1-2", "text": "8.1.2 Phase sequences § quantum signal processing · phase factors Insert \\(Z\\)-rotations \\(e^{i\\phi Z}=\\mathrm{diag}(e^{i\\phi},e^{-i\\phi})\\) between the signal calls. For two calls with one phase in the middle, \\[\\bra0W(x)\\,e^{i\\phi Z}\\,W(x)\\ket0=x^2e^{i\\phi}-(1-x^2)e^{-i\\phi}=\\cos\\phi\\,(2x^2-1)+i\\sin\\phi.\\] The phase reshapes the response. At \\(\\phi=0\\) we recover \\(T_2\\). At \\(\\phi=\\pi/2\\) the two paths combine to the constant \\(i\\): the signal dependence cancels entirely. In between, the real part is \\(T_2\\) scaled by \\(\\cos\\phi\\). With \\(d\\) signal calls and \\(d+1\\) phases \\(\\Phi=(\\phi_0,\\ldots,\\phi_d)\\), the sequence \\[U_\\Phi(x)=e^{i\\phi_0Z}\\prod_{k=1}^{d}W(x)\\,e^{i\\phi_kZ}\\] is called quantum signal processing , and the angles are its phase factors . Which polynomials can appear as \\(P(x)=\\bra0U_\\Phi(x)\\ket0\\)? The answer is a theorem ( Low and Chuang, 2017 ; Gilyén, Su, Low, and Wiebe, 2019 ). A polynomial \\(P\\) is achievable with \\(d\\) calls if and only if (i) \\(\\deg P\\le d\\); (ii) \\(P\\) has the parity of \\(d\\), even or odd; (iii) there is a polynomial \\(Q\\) of degree at most \\(d-1\\) and opposite parity with \\(|P(x)|^2+(1-x^2)|Q(x)|^2=1\\) on \\([-1,1]\\). Condition (iii) says that \\(P\\) can be completed to a unitary; \\(Q\\) is the off-diagonal entry. In practice one usually wants a real target, and then the condition simplifies: any real polynomial of degree \\(d\\) with parity \\(d\\) and \\(|P(x)|\\le1\\) on \\([-1,1]\\) is the real part of an achievable response, and the real part can be isolated with one extra qubit or by a different choice of measured basis. Phase factors for a given target are computed classically; numerically stable algorithms exist for degrees in the thousands. Problems Check the formula \\(\\cos\\phi\\,(2x^2-1)+i\\sin\\phi\\) at \\(x=0\\), \\(\\phi=\\pi/4\\) by multiplying the matrices. With one signal call and phases \\(\\phi_0,\\phi_1\\), what responses are achievable? Can \\(P(x)=x^2\\) be achieved with \\(d=2\\)? Use condition (iii)."}, {"title": "8.1.3 Parity and boundedness", "chapter": "Polynomial Transformations", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#topic-8-1-3", "text": "8.1.3 Parity and boundedness § polynomial approximation · parity constraint The theorem’s conditions shape what algorithms can do. Two matter constantly. Boundedness. The response must stay within \\([-1,1]\\) on the whole interval, not just where we care about it. To approximate an unbounded function like \\(1/x\\), we must restrict attention to a region where it is bounded after scaling, and accept an arbitrary bounded response elsewhere. Parity. With \\(d\\) calls the response is even or odd; this parity constraint follows from the structure of the sequence. A function with no symmetry, like \\(e^{-ixt}\\), must be split into even and odd parts, \\(\\cos(xt)\\) and \\(-i\\sin(xt)\\), each implemented separately and recombined with an LCU (Chapter 7), at a constant-factor cost in success probability . Within these constraints, the design problem is classical polynomial approximation : find a low-degree bounded polynomial close to the desired function on the relevant region. The degree is the number of signal calls, so it is the algorithm’s query cost. Chebyshev polynomials are the natural tool, because they are bounded on \\([-1,1]\\) and form a basis; truncating a function’s Chebyshev expansion gives near-optimal approximations. A smooth function on \\([-1,1]\\) needs low degree; a function with a sharp jump, or one that varies rapidly, needs high degree. Problems Split \\(f(x)=e^{-ixt}\\) into even and odd parts. Why does the parity constraint not prevent us from approximating \\(x^2+x\\)? The step function \\(\\mathrm{sign}(x)\\) jumps at \\(0\\). Why must any bounded polynomial approximating it within \\(\\epsilon\\) on \\(|x|\\ge\\Delta\\) have degree growing as \\(\\Delta\\to0\\)?"}, {"title": "8.2.1 Acting on eigenvalues", "chapter": "Polynomial Transformations", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#topic-8-2-1", "text": "8.2.1 Acting on eigenvalues § eigenvalue transformation Let \\(H\\) be Hermitian with spectral decomposition \\(H=\\sum_j\\lambda_j\\ket{v_j}\\bra{v_j}\\) (Section 6.2.2). A function of \\(H\\) is defined eigenvalue by eigenvalue: \\[f(H)=\\sum_jf(\\lambda_j)\\ket{v_j}\\bra{v_j}.\\] For a polynomial this agrees with the obvious definition, since \\(H^k\\ket{v_j}=\\lambda_j^k\\ket{v_j}\\). Applying \\(f(H)\\) to a state \\(\\ket\\psi=\\sum_jc_j\\ket{v_j}\\) multiplies each component by \\(f(\\lambda_j)\\), without measuring which component is present. An eigenvalue transformation is a circuit that block-encodes \\(f(H/\\alpha)\\) given a block encoding of \\(H/\\alpha\\). Three choices of \\(f\\) cover most applications. A step function that is \\(1\\) on some eigenvalues and \\(0\\) on others projects onto an eigenspace: a filter. The function \\(1/x\\) inverts \\(H\\), solving linear systems. The function \\(e^{-ixt}\\) gives time evolution. Phase estimation , by contrast, measures eigenvalues one sample at a time. Polynomial transformations process all eigenvalues coherently and keep the superposition. Problems For \\(H=Z\\), write \\(f(H)\\) for \\(f(x)=x^2\\) and for \\(f(x)=e^{-ixt}\\). Apply \\(f(H)=H^2\\) to \\(\\ket\\psi=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket{v_1}+\\ket{v_2})\\) with \\(\\lambda_1=0.9\\), \\(\\lambda_2=0.1\\). What fraction of the output’s squared length comes from \\(\\ket{v_1}\\)? Why can \\(f(H)\\) not be implemented, in general, by phase estimation followed by multiplying each outcome by \\(f(\\lambda)\\)?"}, {"title": "8.2.2 Qubitization", "chapter": "Polynomial Transformations", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#topic-8-2-2", "text": "8.2.2 Qubitization § qubitization Suppose \\(U\\) is a block encoding of \\(H/\\alpha\\) that is also Hermitian, \\(U^2=I\\), as LCU encodings with Hermitian SELECT are. Define \\[W=R\\,U,\\qquad R=(2\\ket{0^a}\\bra{0^a}-I)\\otimes I,\\] the block encoding followed by a reflection about the ancilla being zero. For each eigenvector \\(\\ket{v_j}\\) with eigenvalue \\(\\lambda_j\\), the two states \\(\\ket{0^a}\\ket{v_j}\\) and \\(U\\ket{0^a}\\ket{v_j}\\) span a plane that \\(W\\) maps to itself. In that plane, \\(W\\) is a rotation with eigenvalues \\(e^{\\pm i\\theta_j}\\), where \\[\\cos\\theta_j=\\frac{\\lambda_j}{\\alpha}.\\] Each eigenvalue of \\(H\\) has its own two-dimensional “qubit”, which is the origin of the name qubitization ( Low and Chuang, 2019 ). Within each plane, \\(W\\) behaves exactly like the signal operator \\(W(x)\\) with \\(x=\\lambda_j/\\alpha\\). So interleaving \\(W\\) with phase rotations on the ancilla implements \\(P(\\lambda_j/\\alpha)\\) on every eigenvector at once: a quantum signal processing sequence acting on all planes in parallel. The relation \\(\\cos\\theta=\\lambda/\\alpha\\) is the same one we met for Szegedy’s walk in Section 6.3.2, and \\(W\\) is in fact a quantum walk built from the block encoding. Phase estimation on \\(W\\) estimates \\(\\theta_j\\), hence \\(\\lambda_j=\\alpha\\cos\\theta_j\\), without ever implementing \\(e^{-iHt}\\). This is the basis of efficient energy estimation for chemistry: the cost per step is one block encoding, and precision \\(\\epsilon\\) in \\(\\lambda\\) needs about \\(\\alpha/\\epsilon\\) steps. Problems For a two-qubit Hamiltonian with \\(\\lambda=0.8\\) and eigenvalues \\(\\pm\\sqrt{0.34}\\approx\\pm0.583\\), what eigenphases does the qubitized walk have? Why must the ancilla reflection \\(R\\) appear between uses of \\(U\\)? What goes wrong with \\(U^2\\)? If \\(\\alpha\\) is much larger than \\(\\|H\\|\\), all \\(\\lambda_j/\\alpha\\) are close to zero. What happens to the eigenphases, and to the cost of resolving them?"}, {"title": "8.2.3 Singular value transformation", "chapter": "Polynomial Transformations", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#topic-8-2-3", "text": "8.2.3 Singular value transformation § singular value · quantum singular value transformation Not every matrix is Hermitian, or even square. Every matrix has a singular value decomposition: orthonormal input directions \\(\\ket{w_j}\\), orthonormal output directions \\(\\ket{u_j}\\), and nonnegative stretch factors \\(\\sigma_j\\), with \\[A\\ket{w_j}=\\sigma_j\\ket{u_j}.\\] The \\(\\sigma_j\\) are the singular values; the largest is \\(\\|A\\|\\). For a Hermitian matrix they are the absolute eigenvalues. Geometrically, \\(A\\) rotates the input directions to output directions and stretches each by \\(\\sigma_j\\). The quantum singular value transformation (QSVT) of Gilyén, Su, Low, and Wiebe (2019) applies a polynomial to the singular values. Given a block encoding \\(U\\) of \\(A/\\alpha\\), alternate \\(U\\) and \\(U^\\dagger\\) with phase rotations controlled by the projectors that define the block. For an odd polynomial \\(P\\) of degree \\(d\\), the result block-encodes \\[P^{(SV)}(A/\\alpha)=\\sum_jP(\\sigma_j/\\alpha)\\ket{u_j}\\bra{w_j},\\] which maps input directions to output directions with stretch factors transformed by \\(P\\). For even \\(P\\), it maps input directions back to input directions, \\(\\sum_jP(\\sigma_j/\\alpha)\\ket{w_j}\\bra{w_j}\\). The parity decides which space the result lives in. QSVT unifies the earlier techniques. Amplitude amplification is QSVT applied to a \\(1\\times1\\) block, the single number \\(\\sin\\theta\\), with the Chebyshev polynomial that maps \\(\\sin\\theta\\) toward \\(1\\). Fixed-point amplification uses a polynomial that approximates a step. Phase estimation, Hamiltonian simulation , and linear-system solving are further polynomial choices. Learning one framework, its conditions, and its costs gives a common language for all of them. As with every theorem in this course, small worked cases build intuition; the general statement is a cited result with stated assumptions. Problems Give the singular values of \\(A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&2\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}\\) and the directions \\(\\ket{w},\\ket{u}\\). Explain how ordinary amplitude amplification is a singular value transformation of the number \\(\\sin\\theta\\). An algorithm needs \\(A^{-1}\\) for a non-square \\(A\\). What does QSVT provide instead, and when is it meaningful?"}, {"title": "8.3.1 Filters and projectors", "chapter": "Polynomial Transformations", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#topic-8-3-1", "text": "8.3.1 Filters and projectors § spectral filter Suppose the eigenvalues of \\(H/\\alpha\\) are known to avoid an interval \\((\\mu-\\Delta,\\mu+\\Delta)\\), and we want to project a state onto the eigenvectors with eigenvalues below \\(\\mu\\). The ideal function is a step: \\(1\\) below \\(\\mu\\), \\(0\\) above. A spectral filter is a bounded polynomial that approximates this step within \\(\\epsilon\\) outside the gap. Smoothed step functions (built from the error function, for example) can be approximated with degree \\[d=O\\!\\left(\\frac1\\Delta\\log\\frac1\\epsilon\\right).\\] A narrower gap requires a steeper polynomial and higher degree; higher precision costs only logarithmically. Filters prepare ground states. If a trial state has overlap \\(\\gamma\\) with the ground state and the ground energy is separated from the rest by a gap, filtering below the midpoint of the gap leaves the ground state with success amplitude about \\(\\gamma\\). With amplitude amplification the total cost is about \\(\\frac1\\gamma\\cdot\\frac{\\alpha}{\\Delta_E}\\log\\frac1\\epsilon\\) block encodings, where \\(\\Delta_E\\) is the energy gap ( Lin and Tong, 2020 ). The same two quantities as in Section 4.2.4 appear: the overlap of the initial state, and the spectral resolution required. Filtering does not remove the need for a good trial state; it uses it more efficiently than repeated phase estimation. Problems A filter must separate eigenvalues \\(0.1\\) and \\(0.12\\) of \\(H/\\alpha\\). How does the required degree compare with separating \\(0.1\\) and \\(0.3\\), at the same \\(\\epsilon\\)? Why is a filter polynomial’s value inside the gap irrelevant? If the gap assumption is false and an eigenvalue sits inside it, what does the filter do to that component?"}, {"title": "8.3.2 Matrix inversion", "chapter": "Polynomial Transformations", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#topic-8-3-2", "text": "8.3.2 Matrix inversion § quantum linear systems · condition number The quantum linear systems problem: given a block encoding of an invertible matrix \\(A\\) and a preparation circuit for \\(\\ket b\\), produce a state proportional to \\(A^{-1}\\ket b\\). Assume \\(A\\) has singular values in \\([1/\\kappa,1]\\) after scaling; \\(\\kappa\\) is the condition number , the ratio of largest to smallest singular value . The function \\(1/x\\) is unbounded, so approximate the scaled function \\(\\frac{1}{\\kappa x}\\) on \\(1/\\kappa\\le|x|\\le1\\), where it lies in \\([1/\\kappa,1]\\), and let the polynomial do anything bounded on \\(|x|<1/\\kappa\\). An odd polynomial of degree \\(O(\\kappa\\log(\\kappa/\\epsilon))\\) suffices. Applying it gives a block encoding of \\(A^{-1}/\\kappa\\) (roughly), and postselection succeeds with probability \\(\\|A^{-1}b\\|^2/\\kappa^2\\), at least \\(1/\\kappa^2\\). Amplitude amplification brings the number of repetitions to order \\(\\kappa\\), for a total of order \\(\\kappa^2\\log(\\kappa/\\epsilon)\\) block-encoding calls; more refined methods reach the optimal order \\(\\kappa\\log(1/\\epsilon)\\). The first algorithm of this kind was Harrow, Hassidim, and Lloyd (2009) , using phase estimation; polynomial methods improved its dependence on precision. The output is a quantum state, and the caveats of Chapter 1 apply in full. Reading all \\(N\\) entries of the solution would cost order \\(N\\) repetitions. The algorithm is useful when the next step needs only a few numbers, such as an expectation value \\(\\bra xM\\ket x\\), and when \\(A\\) has an efficient block encoding with small \\(\\kappa\\) and \\(\\ket b\\) is cheap to prepare. Aaronson’s “read the fine print” ( Nature Physics , 2015) lists these conditions; each one can erase the advantage. Problems For \\(A=\\mathrm{diag}(1,0.1)\\) and \\(\\ket b=\\ket+\\), what is the normalized solution state, and what is \\(\\kappa\\)? In that example, what is the postselection success probability with the block encoding of \\(A^{-1}/\\kappa\\)? An application wants all \\(N=10^6\\) entries of the solution to accuracy \\(10^{-3}\\). Why does the quantum algorithm not help?"}, {"title": "8.3.3 Hamiltonian simulation by polynomials", "chapter": "Polynomial Transformations", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#topic-8-3-3", "text": "8.3.3 Hamiltonian simulation by polynomials § Jacobi–Anger expansion · optimal simulation To simulate \\(e^{-iHt}\\), apply \\(f(x)=e^{-i\\alpha tx}\\) to the block encoding of \\(H/\\alpha\\). Split it into \\(\\cos(\\alpha tx)\\) and \\(-i\\sin(\\alpha tx)\\), each bounded and of definite parity. Their Chebyshev expansions are known in closed form, the Jacobi–Anger expansion : \\[\\cos(\\tau x)=J_0(\\tau)+2\\sum_{k\\ge1}(-1)^kJ_{2k}(\\tau)T_{2k}(x),\\qquad\\sin(\\tau x)=2\\sum_{k\\ge0}(-1)^kJ_{2k+1}(\\tau)T_{2k+1}(x),\\] where \\(J_k\\) are Bessel functions. Their crucial property is that \\(J_k(\\tau)\\) becomes tiny once \\(k\\) exceeds \\(\\tau\\) by a modest margin: it decays faster than exponentially. Truncating at degree \\(d\\approx e\\tau/2+\\log(1/\\epsilon)\\) gives error \\(\\epsilon\\). With \\(\\tau=\\alpha t\\), \\[d=O\\!\\left(\\alpha t+\\log\\frac1\\epsilon\\right).\\] This additive dependence, time plus log precision rather than a product, is optimal: a lower bound shows simulation for time \\(t\\) needs order \\(\\alpha t\\) queries, and order \\(\\log(1/\\epsilon)/\\log\\log(1/\\epsilon)\\) for precision. Qubitization with QSP achieves it with only a few ancillas ( Low and Chuang ). The cost is stated in block-encoding calls; each call’s gate count, the subnormalization \\(\\alpha\\), and the phase precision must be added for a full count. Chapter 9 compares this optimal simulation with the simpler product formulas, which use no block encodings at all and remain competitive in practice for many systems. Problems For \\(\\alpha t=100\\) and \\(\\epsilon=10^{-10}\\), estimate the degree. Why does the degree depend on \\(\\alpha t\\) rather than on \\(t\\) alone? Explain why simulation for time \\(t\\) cannot in general use fewer than order \\(\\alpha t\\) block-encoding calls."}, {"title": "Polynomial Transformations — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#exercise-1", "text": "What is the top-left entry of W(x)³, and its value at x = 0.5? T₃(0.5) = 4(0.125) − 1.5 = −1. Equivalently, x = cos(π/3), and cos(3·π/3) = cos π = −1."}, {"title": "Polynomial Transformations — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#exercise-2", "text": "Which of these real targets can be the real part of a QSP response with exactly the stated number of signal calls? (a) 0.5x with d=1; (b) x³ with d=2; (c) 1.2x²−0.2 with d=2; (d) 1.5x with d=1. Give the reason in each case. (a) Yes: degree 1, odd like d=1, and |0.5x|≤0.5. (b) No: the degree 3 exceeds d, and the parity is odd while d is even. (c) Yes: degree 2, even, and on [−1,1] it ranges from −0.2 (at x=0) to 1 (at x=±1), so it is bounded by 1. (d) No: |1.5x| reaches 1.5 at x=±1. A response is an entry of a unitary, so it can never exceed 1 anywhere on the interval, even at points the algorithm does not care about."}, {"title": "Polynomial Transformations — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#exercise-3", "text": "A Hermitian block encoding of H/α with α=2 is qubitized. H has eigenvalues 1 and −1. Find the eigenphases of the walk W=RU in each eigenvalue’s plane, and the phase separation that phase estimation must resolve to tell the eigenvalues apart. cosθ=1/2 gives θ=π/3, and cosθ=−1/2 gives θ=2π/3. So the walk has eigenvalues e^(± iπ/3) in the first plane and e^(±2π i/3) in the second. The nearest phases from different planes, π/3 and 2π/3, differ by π/3, a sixth of a full turn. Phase estimation resolves that with only a few walk steps. Each estimate tildeθ gives λ=αcostildeθ; the sign ambiguity ±θ does not matter, since cosine is even."}, {"title": "Polynomial Transformations — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#exercise-4", "text": "A block encoding of H/α has α = 50, and the eigenvalues of H of interest are 1.0 and 1.2. Phase estimation on the qubitized walk is used to distinguish them. Estimate the walk eigenphase difference and the number of walk steps needed. θ₁ = arccos(0.02) and θ₂ = arccos(0.024), differing by about 0.004 radians. Phase estimation must resolve 0.004, which needs on the order of 1/0.004 = 250 walk steps (a few times more for good confidence). Equivalently, α/Δλ = 50/0.2 = 250."}, {"title": "Polynomial Transformations — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#exercise-5", "text": "The two-call sequence W(x)e^(iφ Z)W(x) has top-left entry P_φ(x)=cosφ (2x²−1)+isinφ. (a) Choose φ so the real part is 0.6 T₂(x), and give the imaginary part. (b) Show that averaging the sequences with phases φ and −φ by a two-term LCU isolates the real part. (c) What is the subnormalization of the result? (a) With cosφ=0.6, the real part is 0.6T₂(x) and the imaginary part is the constant sinφ=0.8, so |P_φ|²=0.36T₂²+0.64. Measuring would mostly reflect the unwanted constant. (b) The sequence with −φ gives P_(−φ)=0.6T₂−0.8i. An LCU with weights 1/2,1/2 block-encodes 1/2(P_φ+P_(−φ))=0.6 T₂(x), the real part alone. (c) The LCU coefficients sum to 1, so the subnormalization is 1: the encoded number is exactly 0.6T₂(x). The price is one extra ancilla and controlled phase rotations, and the success probability is 0.36 T₂(x)². This is the extra-qubit trick the theorem refers to when it promises real targets."}, {"title": "Polynomial Transformations — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#exercise-6", "text": "Let A=beginpmatrix0&1\\1/2&0endpmatrix. (a) Find its singular values and the input and output directions. (b) Compute P^((SV))(A) for the odd polynomial P=T₃ and the even version ΣⱼP(sigmaⱼ)|wⱼ⟩⟨wⱼ| for P=T₂. Which spaces do the two results map between? (a) A|1⟩=1·|0⟩ and A|0⟩=1/2|1⟩. So sigma₁=1 with |w₁⟩=|1⟩, |u₁⟩=|0⟩, and sigma₂=1/2 with |w₂⟩=|0⟩, |u₂⟩=|1⟩. (b) For T₃, T₃(1)=1 and T₃(1/2)=4·1/8−3/2=−1, so T₃^((SV))(A)=|0⟩⟨1|−|1⟩⟨0|. It maps input directions to output directions, like A itself, with new stretch factors. For T₂, T₂(1)=1 and T₂(1/2)=−1/2, giving |1⟩⟨1|−1/2|0⟩⟨0|. It maps the input space to itself, as A† A does. The odd transformation changed the singular value 1/2 to magnitude 1: QSVT can enlarge singular values, provided the polynomial stays bounded."}, {"title": "Polynomial Transformations — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#exercise-7", "text": "For tau=α t=10, the Jacobi–Anger coefficients are J₁₀(10)≈0.21, J₁₅(10)≈4.5×10⁻³, J₂₀(10)≈1.2×10⁻⁵, and J₂₅(10)≈7×10⁻⁹. (a) Estimate the truncation degree for error 10⁻⁶ from the rule d≈ etau/2+ln (1/ε). (b) Using the listed values, judge where the series can actually be cut. (c) How does the degree change if tau doubles, or if ε becomes 10⁻¹²? (a) e·10/2+ln 10⁶≈13.6+13.8≈27. (b) The coefficients fall from 0.2 at k=10 to about 10⁻⁵ at k=20 and below 10⁻⁸ at k=25. The tail beyond degree 22 or so is already below 10⁻⁶, so the rule is a safe overestimate. Up to k≈tau the coefficients are not small; beyond it they decay faster than exponentially. (c) Doubling tau roughly doubles the degree, because the cutoff sits a little beyond k≈tau. Squaring the precision to 10⁻¹² adds only a few more terms, because of the super-exponential decay. This is the additive scaling O(α t+log(1/ε)) in concrete numbers."}, {"title": "Polynomial Transformations — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student reasons: “Every QSP response satisfies |P(x)|≤1, so a polynomial transformation can only shrink singular values; it can never turn a small singular value into a large one.” Test this with P(x)=3x−4x³=−T₃(x). What does it do to x=1/2, and which earlier algorithm is this? P(1/2)=3/2−1/2=1, so a singular value 1/2 becomes 1. On [−1,1], P(sinθ)=sin3θ, so |P|≤1 everywhere. The bound limits the output, not the ratio of output to input. Near 0, P(x)≈3x, so small values are tripled. Writing the input as sinθ, the transformation sinθ↦sin3θ is exactly one round of amplitude amplification. Polynomials can amplify small values as long as they stay bounded on the whole interval; boundedness is why the amplification has to slow down near 1."}, {"title": "Polynomial Transformations — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#exercise-9", "text": "A linear system has κ = 100. Estimate the number of block-encoding calls for the polynomial-inversion algorithm with amplitude amplification, taking the polynomial degree to be κ ln(κ/ε) with ε = 10⁻³, and the amplification count to be κ. Then state what output the algorithm produces. Degree ≈ 100 × 11.5 ≈ 1150; amplification needs about 100 rounds, each using the polynomial sequence and its inverse, so about 2 × 1150 × 100 ≈ 2.3 × 10⁵ block-encoding calls (methods with optimal κ log(1/ε) scaling reduce this to about 10³–10⁴). The output is a quantum state proportional to A⁻¹|b⟩, not the vector of solution entries; extracting a classical quantity such as ⟨x|M|x⟩ to precision δ costs further repetitions of order 1/δ with amplitude estimation."}, {"title": "Polynomial Transformations — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-polynomial-transformations.html#exercise-10", "text": "A trial state has overlap γ=0.1 with the ground state of H. The block encoding has α=100, the gap is Delta_E=0.5, and the target error is ε=10⁻³. Compare, up to constant factors: (a) repeated phase estimation on the qubitized walk, which succeeds with probability γ² per run and needs about α/Delta_E walk steps to resolve the gap; and (b) a spectral filter of degree (α/Delta_E)ln (1/ε) combined with amplitude amplification. What does each method output, and which assumption do both share? (a) Each run costs about α/Delta_E=200 walk steps and succeeds with probability γ²=0.01, so about 100×200=2×10⁴ calls are expected (more for high confidence). (b) The filter has degree about 200ln 10³≈1.4×10³. Its success amplitude is about γ, so amplitude amplification needs about 1/γ=10 rounds: about 1.4×10⁴ calls. The advantage of (b) grows as γ falls, since 1/γ replaces 1/γ². Its ln (1/ε) factor is the cost of a sharp filter. Method (a) outputs an energy estimate and a collapsed state; method (b) outputs the ground state coherently, to precision ε, ready for later processing. Both assume a trial state with known nonnegligible overlap and a known gap; neither helps if preparing such a state is the hard part."}, {"title": "9.1.1 The simulation problem", "chapter": "Evolution Engineering", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#topic-9-1-1", "text": "9.1.1 The simulation problem § Hamiltonian simulation · no fast-forwarding The input to Hamiltonian simulation is a Hamiltonian on \\(n\\) qubits, a time \\(t\\), and an error tolerance \\(\\epsilon\\). The output is a circuit \\(V\\) with \\(\\|V-e^{-iHt}\\|\\le\\epsilon\\). The Hamiltonian is usually given as a sum of terms that are individually simple, \\[H=\\sum_{j=1}^{L}H_j,\\] for example Pauli strings with coefficients (Section 7.2.3), or a lattice model where each term acts on two neighbouring sites. Each \\(e^{-iH_j\\tau}\\) is easy to implement exactly, as Section 9.2.1 will show. The whole \\(e^{-iHt}\\) is not, because the terms do not commute. How must the cost grow with \\(t\\)? Generically at least linearly. If an arbitrary Hamiltonian could be simulated for time \\(t\\) with far fewer than \\(t\\) steps, one could compute things known to require many queries, such as parities of many bits, by encoding them in a Hamiltonian and evolving. This no fast-forwarding theorem ( Berry, Ahokas, Cleve, and Sanders, 2007 ) says that long-time dynamics cannot in general be shortcut. Some special Hamiltonians, those that a classical change of basis diagonalizes, can be fast-forwarded; that is the exception, and its absence is what makes simulation hard classically and valuable quantumly. The comparison with classical computation is the reason for interest. A classical computer storing the state of \\(n\\) qubits needs \\(2^n\\) amplitudes. For many physical systems, no known classical method avoids this exponential cost at the accuracy required, while a quantum simulation needs \\(n\\) qubits and a gate count polynomial in \\(n\\), \\(t\\), and \\(1/\\epsilon\\). This is a comparison with known classical methods, which exploit structure such as low entanglement or approximate symmetry, and it must be made for each target system. Problems A lattice of \\(100\\) spins has a Hamiltonian with nearest-neighbour terms on a line. How many terms \\(L\\), and how many amplitudes would a classical state vector store? Give a Hamiltonian that can be fast-forwarded, and say why. Why is the error measured in operator norm rather than as an error in one output state?"}, {"title": "9.1.2 Commuting and noncommuting terms", "chapter": "Evolution Engineering", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#topic-9-1-2", "text": "9.1.2 Commuting and noncommuting terms § commutator · product formula Start with two terms, \\(H=A+B\\). If \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) commute, \\(AB=BA\\), then the exponential factors exactly: \\(e^{-i(A+B)t}=e^{-iAt}e^{-iBt}\\), just as for numbers. The proof expands both sides as power series; commutation lets the terms be rearranged freely. If they do not commute, the factorization fails. Take \\(A=X\\), \\(B=Z\\). Because \\(XZ=-ZX\\), the cross terms in \\((X+Z)^2=X^2+XZ+ZX+Z^2\\) cancel, and \\((X+Z)^2=2I\\). So \\(X+Z\\) behaves like \\(\\sqrt2\\) times a matrix squaring to \\(I\\), and the power-series argument of Section 6.2.1 gives \\[e^{-it(X+Z)}=\\cos(\\sqrt2t)\\,I-i\\,\\frac{\\sin(\\sqrt2t)}{\\sqrt2}\\,(X+Z).\\] This is not \\(e^{-itX}e^{-itZ}\\), which is a rotation about a different axis by a different angle. How big is the difference for a short time \\(\\delta\\)? Expand both to second order: \\[e^{-i\\delta A}e^{-i\\delta B}=I-i\\delta(A+B)-\\tfrac{\\delta^2}{2}(A^2+2AB+B^2)+O(\\delta^3),\\] \\[e^{-i\\delta(A+B)}=I-i\\delta(A+B)-\\tfrac{\\delta^2}{2}(A^2+AB+BA+B^2)+O(\\delta^3).\\] They agree to first order and differ at second order by \\(-\\frac{\\delta^2}{2}(AB-BA)\\). The commutator \\([A,B]=AB-BA\\) measures exactly how far the terms are from commuting, and it controls the error. Splitting \\(e^{-iHt}\\) into \\(r\\) short steps, each approximated by a product of term exponentials, is a product formula , also called Trotterization. Problems Compute \\([X,Z]\\) and its operator norm. Do \\(Z_1Z_2\\) and \\(X_1X_2\\) commute? Use the rule that two Pauli strings commute when they anticommute on an even number of positions. Verify \\(e^{-it(X+Z)}\\) at \\(t=\\pi/(2\\sqrt2)\\). What gate is it, up to phase?"}, {"title": "9.1.3 First-order error and step selection", "chapter": "Evolution Engineering", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#topic-9-1-3", "text": "9.1.3 First-order error and step selection § Trotter error · Trotter steps The first-order product formula uses \\(r\\) steps of length \\(\\delta=t/r\\): \\[S_1(t)=\\big(e^{-i\\delta A}e^{-i\\delta B}\\big)^r.\\] A careful version of the expansion above, with the remainder bounded by integrating the commutator rather than dropped, shows that one step has error at most \\(\\frac{\\delta^2}{2}\\|[A,B]\\|\\). Errors of \\(r\\) steps add, as for any product of unitaries. So the Trotter error satisfies \\[\\big\\|S_1(t)-e^{-i(A+B)t}\\big\\|\\le r\\cdot\\frac{\\delta^2}{2}\\|[A,B]\\|=\\frac{t^2\\,\\|[A,B]\\|}{2r}.\\] To reach error \\(\\epsilon\\), choose \\(r\\ge t^2\\|[A,B]\\|/(2\\epsilon)\\) Trotter steps . For \\(A=X\\), \\(B=Z\\), \\(\\|[A,B]\\|=2\\) and the bound is \\(t^2/r\\). The figure compares the bound with the actual error for \\(t=1\\). The bound is valid and of the right shape, \\(1/r\\), but about 40% loose here: the actual error is close to \\(0.7/r\\). That is typical. Worst-case commutator bounds are often loose by constant or even polynomial factors for physical systems, and tighter bounds that use the structure of all the nested commutators ( Childs, Su, Tran, Wiebe, and Zhu, 2021 ) or empirical error estimates can reduce the step count substantially. For a general sum of \\(L\\) terms, the first-order error bound is \\(\\frac{t^2}{2r}\\sum_{j<k}\\|[H_j,H_k]\\|\\): only noncommuting pairs contribute. Problems For \\(H=X+Z\\), \\(t=1\\), how many first-order steps does the bound require for \\(\\epsilon=0.01\\)? About how many suffice in fact? Doubling the simulation time \\(t\\) at fixed \\(\\epsilon\\) multiplies the number of first-order steps by what factor? The total gate count? If \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) commute, what does the bound say, and what does that mean for a Hamiltonian whose terms all commute?"}, {"title": "9.1.4 Higher-order formulas", "chapter": "Evolution Engineering", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#topic-9-1-4", "text": "9.1.4 Higher-order formulas § symmetric Trotter formula · Suzuki formulas The first-order error came from an asymmetry: \\(A\\) always acts before \\(B\\). Symmetrize. The symmetric Trotter formula (also called Strang splitting) uses \\[S_2(\\delta)=e^{-i\\delta A/2}\\,e^{-i\\delta B}\\,e^{-i\\delta A/2}.\\] Expanding to second order, the commutator terms cancel, and the first difference from \\(e^{-i\\delta(A+B)}\\) appears at third order, with nested commutators \\([A,[A,B]]\\) and \\([B,[B,A]]\\). Over \\(r\\) steps the error is \\(O(t^3/r^2)\\). In the X+Z example, \\(r=10\\) symmetric steps give error about \\(0.003\\), against \\(0.07\\) for first order. The cost per step is almost the same, since adjacent half-steps \\(e^{-i\\delta A/2}e^{-i\\delta A/2}\\) merge into one. Suzuki showed how to build formulas of any even order \\(2k\\) recursively from \\(S_2\\) (the Suzuki formulas ), with error \\(O(t^{2k+1}/r^{2k})\\). The number of steps for error \\(\\epsilon\\) is then about \\(t^{1+1/(2k)}/\\epsilon^{1/(2k)}\\), approaching linear in \\(t\\) as \\(k\\) grows. The price is that each step of order \\(2k\\) contains \\(5^{k-1}\\) symmetric steps, so very high orders are wasteful; in practice orders two to six are used. Problems For the symmetric formula, estimate how many steps are needed for \\(\\epsilon=10^{-4}\\) at \\(t=1\\), using the figure’s values. Why does the symmetric formula cost little more than the first-order one over many steps? A fourth-order Suzuki formula has error \\(O(t^5/r^4)\\) and uses five symmetric steps per step. For \\(t=100\\), compare the scaling of required steps with second order, ignoring constants."}, {"title": "9.2.1 Pauli exponentials as circuits", "chapter": "Evolution Engineering", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#topic-9-2-1", "text": "9.2.1 Pauli exponentials as circuits § Pauli exponential · parity circuit Every term exponential in a Pauli-sum Hamiltonian has the form \\(e^{-i\\theta P}\\) for a Pauli string \\(P\\). Start with \\(P=Z\\otimes Z\\). On a basis state \\(\\ket{b_1b_2}\\), \\(Z\\otimes Z\\) has eigenvalue \\((-1)^{b_1\\oplus b_2}\\), so \\(e^{-i\\theta Z\\otimes Z}\\) multiplies by \\(e^{-i\\theta(-1)^{b_1\\oplus b_2}}\\): a phase depending only on the parity. The circuit computes the parity into the second qubit with a CNOT, applies \\(R_z(2\\theta)=e^{-i\\theta Z}\\) there, and uncomputes the parity with a second CNOT. This is a parity circuit , the clean-computation pattern of Chapter 2 once more. For a longer string such as \\(Z_1Z_2Z_3Z_4\\), a ladder of CNOTs computes the parity of all four bits into one qubit, and its mirror image uncomputes it. For strings containing \\(X\\) or \\(Y\\), first rotate those qubits so the Pauli becomes \\(Z\\): \\(H\\) maps \\(X\\) to \\(Z\\), and \\(S^\\dagger\\) followed by \\(H\\) maps \\(Y\\) to \\(Z\\). Then apply the \\(Z\\)-string circuit and undo the basis changes. A Pauli exponential on a string of weight \\(w\\) (the number of non-identity positions) therefore costs \\(2(w-1)\\) CNOTs, one rotation, and up to \\(2w\\) single-qubit basis changes. A first-order step for a Hamiltonian with \\(L\\) terms of weight at most \\(w\\) costs \\(O(Lw)\\) gates. In fault-tolerant settings, each arbitrary rotation \\(R_z\\) must itself be synthesized from \\(T\\) and Clifford gates, costing about \\(3\\log_2(1/\\epsilon_{\\mathrm{rot}})\\) \\(T\\) gates for rotation error \\(\\epsilon_{\\mathrm{rot}}\\), which adds to the total error budget. Problems Verify that CNOT, \\(R_z(2\\theta)\\) on the target, CNOT implements \\(e^{-i\\theta Z\\otimes Z}\\) on \\(\\ket{01}\\). Give the circuit for \\(e^{-i\\theta X\\otimes Y}\\). A Hamiltonian has \\(L=1000\\) Pauli terms of weight at most \\(4\\). Estimate the CNOT count of \\(r=100\\) first-order Trotter steps."}, {"title": "9.2.2 Comparing methods", "chapter": "Evolution Engineering", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#topic-9-2-2", "text": "9.2.2 Comparing methods § fair comparison We now have three families of simulation methods, each with a cost formula. Product formulas cost about \\(L\\cdot r\\) term exponentials, with \\(r\\) depending on commutators, time, and order. Linear combinations of unitaries with truncated Taylor series cost about \\(\\lambda t\\log(\\lambda t/\\epsilon)\\) uses of a block encoding . Qubitization with signal processing costs about \\(\\lambda t+\\log(1/\\epsilon)\\) uses of a block encoding, where \\(\\lambda\\) is the 1-norm and each block encoding uses a PREPARE and SELECT over all \\(L\\) terms. Asymptotically, qubitization is optimal. In practice the comparison depends on constants and structure. Product formulas need no ancillas, have simple circuits, and benefit from commutator structure: for lattice models with local interactions, their errors can be far smaller than worst-case bounds suggest. Block-encoding methods pay for PREPARE and SELECT circuits, but their error dependence on precision is exponentially better. An empirical comparison for spin systems ( Childs, Maslov, Nam, Ross, and Su, 2018 ) found that high-order product formulas with tight error estimates used the fewest gates for the instances studied, while qubitization was preferable for very high precision. For quantum chemistry, where \\(L\\) is large and the terms are nonlocal, block-encoding methods with factorized Hamiltonians currently give the lowest resource estimates. A fair comparison fixes the same Hamiltonian, the same time, the same error in the same norm, and the same gate set, and counts everything: ancillas, rotation synthesis, and classical precomputation. Comparing the degree of a polynomial with a number of Trotter steps is not a comparison of costs. Problems For \\(\\lambda t=1000\\) and \\(\\epsilon=10^{-3}\\), compare the number of block-encoding calls for Taylor-series LCU and qubitization, ignoring constants. Why might a product formula beat qubitization for a one-dimensional lattice model with \\(n=100\\) sites, despite worse asymptotic scaling? A paper reports “simulation with \\(O(\\log(1/\\epsilon))\\) dependence on precision.” What must be checked before comparing it with a product formula?"}, {"title": "9.2.3 Errors beyond the circuit", "chapter": "Evolution Engineering", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#topic-9-2-3", "text": "9.2.3 Errors beyond the circuit § model error · observable estimation A simulation circuit with operator-norm error \\(\\epsilon\\) is one source of error among several. A full account lists them separately. Model error. The Hamiltonian is a model of a physical system: a lattice discretization of a continuum, a truncated basis set for a molecule, a restriction to a few orbitals. Its accuracy is a property of the model, not of the circuit. Algorithmic error. The product-formula or polynomial approximation error, bounded as above. Synthesis error. Each rotation approximated from a finite gate set adds its error. Hardware error. Physical noise, mitigated or corrected at further cost. Statistical error. The output is usually an expectation value, estimated from repeated measurements: order \\(1/\\delta^2\\) runs for precision \\(\\delta\\) by sampling, or order \\(1/\\delta\\) with amplitude or phase estimation. Observable estimation also changes what “error \\(\\epsilon\\)” should mean. If the goal is an expectation value \\(\\bra\\psi O\\ket\\psi\\) with \\(\\|O\\|\\le1\\), an operator-norm error \\(\\epsilon\\) in the evolution changes it by at most \\(2\\epsilon\\). Energies are estimated by phase estimation on \\(e^{-iHt}\\) or on the qubitized walk of Section 8.2.2, which needs a good initial state as discussed in Section 4.2.4; the evolution error and the phase-estimation resolution must both be budgeted. Rescaling \\(H\\) to shorten \\(t\\) changes none of this, since only \\(\\lambda t\\) and \\(\\epsilon\\) enter the costs. Problems A target energy accuracy is \\(10^{-3}\\) in units where \\(\\|H\\|\\approx10\\). The model error is estimated at \\(5\\times10^{-4}\\). How much remains for the algorithm and for statistics? Show that \\(|\\bra\\psi V^\\dagger OV\\ket\\psi-\\bra\\psi U^\\dagger OU\\ket\\psi|\\le2\\|O\\|\\,\\|V-U\\|\\) for unitaries \\(U,V\\). Why does a quantum simulation of a molecule not automatically give chemically accurate energies?"}, {"title": "9.3.1 Following an eigenstate", "chapter": "Evolution Engineering", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#topic-9-3-1", "text": "9.3.1 Following an eigenstate § adiabatic evolution · ground state Consider the one-qubit family \\[H(s)=-(1-s)\\,X-s\\,Z,\\qquad0\\le s\\le1.\\] At \\(s=0\\), \\(H=-X\\), whose lowest-energy state, the ground state , is \\(\\ket+\\). At \\(s=1\\), \\(H=-Z\\), with ground state \\(\\ket0\\). In between, \\(H(s)\\) has eigenvalues \\(\\pm\\sqrt{(1-s)^2+s^2}\\), and its ground state rotates smoothly from \\(\\ket+\\) to \\(\\ket0\\). Adiabatic evolution prepares \\(\\ket+\\), then evolves under \\(H(s)\\) with \\(s\\) increasing from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\) over a total time \\(T\\). If \\(T\\) is large, the state stays close to the instantaneous ground state and ends near \\(\\ket0\\). The separation between the two eigenvalues is the gap, \\(g(s)=2\\sqrt{(1-s)^2+s^2}\\), smallest at \\(s=\\frac12\\) with value \\(\\sqrt2\\). The gap is what protects the state: transitions to the excited state are suppressed when the Hamiltonian changes slowly compared with the energy scale set by the gap. A gap is an energy, not a probability of error; the error depends on how the gap compares with the rate of change. Problems Verify that \\(H(s)^2=\\big((1-s)^2+s^2\\big)I\\), and hence the eigenvalues. If the evolution is instantaneous (\\(T=0\\)), what is the probability of ending in \\(\\ket0\\)? Why does the adiabatic method need the initial state to be the ground state of \\(H(0)\\), rather than any easy state?"}, {"title": "9.3.2 Gap and runtime", "chapter": "Evolution Engineering", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#topic-9-3-2", "text": "9.3.2 Gap and runtime § adiabatic theorem · minimum gap Simulate the one-qubit example for several total times \\(T\\), with \\(s=t/T\\). The probability of ending in \\(\\ket0\\): For \\(T=0.5\\) the result is barely better than the sudden \\(0.5\\); by \\(T=4\\) it is \\(0.986\\); by \\(T=8\\), \\(0.9997\\). The transition from failure to success happens when \\(T\\) becomes large compared with the inverse square of the gap. The adiabatic theorem makes this quantitative. A commonly quoted sufficient condition is \\[T\\gg\\frac{\\max_s\\|dH/ds\\|}{g_{\\min}^2},\\] where \\(g_{\\min}\\) is the minimum gap along the path. Rigorous versions add terms involving the second derivative and integrals of \\(1/g^3\\), and require the gap to stay open. The scaling \\(1/g_{\\min}^2\\) is the important part: halving the gap quadruples the time. The schedule matters too. Moving slowly only near the minimum gap and quickly elsewhere, a local adiabatic schedule , can reduce the time to about \\(1/g_{\\min}\\) in favourable cases. Adiabatic evolution can itself be simulated on a gate-based computer by product formulas, with \\(H(s)\\) changing from step to step, so it is also a method for preparing ground states within the circuit model. Conversely, adiabatic computation with general Hamiltonians is polynomially equivalent to the circuit model ( Aharonov et al., 2004 ): it is a different presentation of the same computational power, not more. Problems For the example, \\(\\|dH/ds\\|=\\|X-Z\\|=\\sqrt2\\) and \\(g_{\\min}=\\sqrt2\\). What does the heuristic condition suggest? A family of problems has \\(g_{\\min}=2^{-n/2}\\). What runtime does the adiabatic condition suggest, and what does this mean? Why can the adiabatic runtime not be estimated by computing \\(g_{\\min}\\) for a large instance on a classical computer before running?"}, {"title": "9.3.3 Adiabatic optimization and its limits", "chapter": "Evolution Engineering", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#topic-9-3-3", "text": "9.3.3 Adiabatic optimization and its limits § adiabatic optimization An optimization problem with cost function \\(C(x)\\) on bit strings can be encoded in a diagonal Hamiltonian \\(H_C=\\sum_xC(x)\\ket x\\bra x\\), whose ground state is the optimal \\(x\\). Adiabatic optimization evolves from \\(H(0)=-\\sum_jX_j\\), with ground state \\(\\ket+^{\\otimes n}\\), to \\(H(1)=H_C\\) ( Farhi, Goldstone, Gutmann, and Sipser, 2000 ). Measuring at the end gives a low-cost string if the evolution was slow enough. What can be proved? For unstructured search, \\(H_C\\) marks one string among \\(N=2^n\\), and the minimum gap is of order \\(1/\\sqrt N\\) (exactly \\(1/\\sqrt N\\) for the standard choice of initial and final Hamiltonians as projectors). A linear schedule then takes time of order \\(N\\), no better than classical search; the local schedule of Roland and Cerf (2002) achieves order \\(\\sqrt N\\), matching Grover. For hard optimization problems, there are instances where the gap is exponentially small, sometimes because of avoided crossings late in the evolution where the ground state switches abruptly between distant configurations. No proof shows that adiabatic optimization solves NP-hard problems efficiently, and the evidence suggests it does not in general. The honest summary is that slow evolution is a design principle with a clear condition for success, a large enough gap, that is hard to verify for large instances. When an algorithm’s guarantee depends on an unknown gap, the next option is to give up guarantees altogether and search for good parameters empirically. That is the territory of variational methods, Chapter 10. Problems Write \\(H_C\\) for MaxCut on a single edge between two qubits, with cost \\(-1\\) for a cut edge and \\(0\\) otherwise. For search with \\(N=2^{20}\\), compare the runtimes of linear and local adiabatic schedules with the gap \\(1/\\sqrt N\\). An experiment reports that an adiabatic device found optimal solutions faster than a classical heuristic on a set of instances. What would establish a genuine algorithmic advantage?"}, {"title": "Evolution Engineering — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#exercise-1", "text": "For H = Z₁Z₂ + X₁, compute the commutator [Z₁Z₂, X₁] and its norm, and give the first-order step count for t = 2, ε = 0.01. Z₁Z₂X₁ − X₁Z₁Z₂ = (Z₁X₁ − X₁Z₁)Z₂ = 2iY₁Z₂, with norm 2. So r ≥ 4·2/(2·0.01) = 400 first-order steps by the bound."}, {"title": "Evolution Engineering — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#exercise-2", "text": "Using the rule that Pauli strings commute when they anticommute on an even number of positions, decide which pairs commute among X₁X₂, Y₁Y₂, Z₁Z₂, and X₁Z₂. What is the first-order Trotter error for the Heisenberg pair Hamiltonian H=X₁X₂+Y₁Y₂+Z₁Z₂? X₁X₂, Y₁Y₂, and Z₁Z₂ differ from each other at both positions, so every pair anticommutes twice and commutes. X₁Z₂ anticommutes once with X₁X₂ (position 2) and once with Z₁Z₂ (position 1), so those pairs anticommute; with Y₁Y₂ it differs at both positions and commutes. For the Heisenberg pair all commutators vanish, so e^(−iHt)=e^(−iX₁X₂t)e^(−iY₁Y₂t)e^(−iZ₁Z₂t) exactly, with no Trotter error for any t. A single step suffices, which is fast-forwarding. On a chain of many sites, neighbouring bonds share a qubit and do not commute, and the error returns."}, {"title": "Evolution Engineering — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#exercise-3", "text": "For H=X+Z and target error ε=10⁻³, how many first-order steps does the commutator bound require at t=1 and at t=10? How does the second-order formula’s step count, of order t^(3/2)/ε^(1/2), scale between the same two times? The first-order bound needs r≥ t²/ε: 1000 steps at t=1 and 100 000 at t=10, a factor of 100 for ten times longer evolution. The second-order count grows by 10^(3/2)≈32. Each first-order step has fixed cost, so the first-order gate count grows as t². The no-fast-forwarding theorem requires only linear growth, so the first-order formula pays an extra factor of t. Higher-order formulas, or polynomial methods, close most of that gap."}, {"title": "Evolution Engineering — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#exercise-4", "text": "Write a circuit for e^{−iθ Y₁ Y₂} and count its CNOTs and single-qubit gates. On each qubit apply S† then H (mapping Y to Z); CNOT from qubit 1 to qubit 2; R_z(2θ) on qubit 2; CNOT from qubit 1 to 2; then H then S on each qubit. Two CNOTs, one rotation, and eight Clifford single-qubit gates (some of which may cancel with neighbouring terms in a full Trotter step)."}, {"title": "Evolution Engineering — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#exercise-5", "text": "For H=X+Z, a single first-order step of length delta=0.1 has actual error about 0.00998, while ten such steps (total time 1) have actual error about 0.070. (a) Compare each with the bounds delta²/2|[X,Z]| and t²/2r|[X,Z]|. (b) Why is the one-step bound nearly tight while the ten-step bound is loose? (c) Using the actual 1/r scaling, estimate the steps needed for error 0.01. (a) The one-step bound is 0.01·2/2=0.01, matching 0.00998 almost exactly. The ten-step bound is 1·2/20=0.1, against an actual 0.070. (b) For a single short step the leading error term −delta²/2[X,Z] dominates and the bound captures it. Over many steps the bound adds error norms as if every step erred in the worst direction, but the step errors are rotated by the evolution in between and partly cancel, so the total grows more slowly than the sum. (c) The actual error is about 0.70/r, so r≈70 steps reach 0.01, against 100 from the bound. That difference translates directly into gate count."}, {"title": "Evolution Engineering — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#exercise-6", "text": "Adiabatic search over N items uses H(s)=I−(1−s)|u⟩⟨u|−s|m⟩⟨m|, where |u⟩ is the uniform superposition and |m⟩ the marked item. For N=4, compute the gap at s=0, s=1/4, and s=1/2 using g(s)=√(1−4(1−1/N)s(1−s)). Compare the runtimes suggested by the heuristic 1/g_(min)² for N=4 and N=2²⁰. For N=4: g(0)=1, g(1/4)=√(1−3·3/16)=√(7/16)≈0.661, and g(1/2)=√(1−3/4)=1/2=1/√N. The heuristic 1/g_(min)²=N suggests time of order 4 here and 2²⁰≈10⁶ for N=2²⁰, no better than classical search. The gap is small only in a window near s=1/2, of width about 1/√N. A schedule that slows down only there, the local adiabatic schedule, needs time of order √N≈10³, matching Grover search. The improvement comes from knowing where the gap is small."}, {"title": "Evolution Engineering — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#exercise-7", "text": "A first-order simulation has L=50 Pauli terms and r=1000 steps, so 5×10⁴ arbitrary rotations. The total error budget is 10⁻³, half allotted to rotation synthesis. Using about 3log₂(1/ε_(rot)) T gates per rotation, find ε_(rot), the T count per rotation, and the total T count. How would halving the number of rotations change the answer? The rotation errors add, so ε_(rot)≤5×10⁻⁴/(5×10⁴)=10⁻⁸. Each rotation then costs about 3×26.6≈80 T gates, and the total is about 5×10⁴×80=4×10⁶ T gates. Halving the number of rotations halves the count and also relaxes each rotation’s budget to 2×10⁻⁸, saving about 3 T gates per rotation: roughly 2×10⁶ in total. The logarithmic dependence means precision is cheap per gate, but the count of rotations, set by L and r, drives the cost."}, {"title": "Evolution Engineering — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student rescales a Hamiltonian, H'=H/10, and argues that e^(−iH't') with t'=10t equals e^(−iHt) while every commutator becomes 100 times smaller, so Trotterization gets much cheaper. Check the first-order step count and the qubitization cost before and after rescaling. For first order, r≥ t'²|[A',B']|/(2ε)=(10t)²·|[A,B]|/100/(2ε)=t²|[A,B]|/(2ε): unchanged. For qubitization, λ'=λ/10 and λ't'=λ t: unchanged. The evolution operator depends only on the product Ht, and so does every honest cost formula. Rescaling changes units, not difficulty. A claimed speedup from “simulating for less time” must be checked against |H|t or λ t, the dimensionless quantities the costs depend on."}, {"title": "Evolution Engineering — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#exercise-9", "text": "An adiabatic algorithm for a family of instances has minimum gap g(n) = 1/n² for n qubits. Estimate the runtime with a linear schedule and with a local schedule, and explain whether this would give a polynomial-time algorithm. What would you need to know to trust the gap estimate? Linear: T ~ n⁴; local: T ~ n². Both are polynomial, so if the gap really scales as 1/n² for the whole family, the adiabatic algorithm runs in polynomial time (and can be simulated by a polynomial-size circuit). To trust it, one needs a proof of the gap bound for all instances, or at least strong evidence across sizes, because numerical gaps on small n can miss exponentially small avoided crossings that appear only at larger n."}, {"title": "Evolution Engineering — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-evolution-engineering.html#exercise-10", "text": "Derive the gap formula used in adiabatic search. (a) Show that H(s)=I−(1−s)|u⟩⟨u|−s|m⟩⟨m| acts as I on states orthogonal to both |u⟩ and |m⟩, so the gap is set by the two-dimensional block they span. (b) In that block, find the trace and determinant of M=(1−s)|u⟩⟨u|+s|m⟩⟨m| using |⟨u|m⟩|²=1/N. (c) Deduce g(s)=√(1−4(1−1/N)s(1−s)) and its minimum. (a) A state orthogonal to |u⟩ and |m⟩ is annihilated by both projectors, so H(s) gives eigenvalue 1. The other eigenvalues live in the span of |u⟩,|m⟩, where H=I−M. (b) trM=(1−s)+s=1. For the determinant, write M in an orthonormal basis {|m⟩,|m^(perp)⟩} with |u⟩=1/√N|m⟩+√(1−1/N)|m^(perp)⟩; a direct calculation gives det M=s(1−s)(1−1/N). (c) The eigenvalues of M differ by √(1−4s(1−s)(1−1/N)), and so do those of H=I−M. The smaller eigenvalue of H, 1/2(1−√(⋯)), is the ground energy. The larger, 1/2(1+√(⋯))≤1, is the first excited energy, below the eigenvalue 1 of the orthogonal states. So the gap is g(s)=√(1−4(1−1/N)s(1−s)), minimized at s=1/2 with g_(min)=1/√N."}, {"title": "10.1.1 Trial states and expressiveness", "chapter": "Variational Methods", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#topic-10-1-1", "text": "10.1.1 Trial states and expressiveness § parameterized circuit · ansatz · expressiveness A parameterized circuit \\(U(\\theta)\\) is a fixed gate sequence in which some rotation angles \\(\\theta=(\\theta_1,\\ldots,\\theta_m)\\) are adjustable. Applied to a simple input, it produces a family of trial states \\(\\ket{\\psi(\\theta)}=U(\\theta)\\ket{0^n}\\). The choice of circuit structure is called an ansatz , a German word for an educated guess about the form of the answer. Here is the running example. Apply \\(R_y(\\theta)=e^{-i\\theta Y/2}\\) to the first of two qubits in \\(\\ket{00}\\), then a CNOT: \\[\\ket{\\psi(\\theta)}=\\cos\\tfrac\\theta2\\,\\ket{00}+\\sin\\tfrac\\theta2\\,\\ket{11}.\\] At \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\) this is the Bell state \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})\\). But the family contains only states with real, nonnegative-ratio amplitudes on \\(\\ket{00}\\) and \\(\\ket{11}\\). It cannot produce \\(\\ket{01}\\), or \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket{00}-\\ket{11})\\), or any state outside a one-dimensional curve in the space of two-qubit states. The expressiveness of an ansatz is the set of states it can reach. A more expressive ansatz, with more gates and parameters, is more likely to contain a good answer, but it costs more to run, has more parameters to optimize, and, as Section 10.2.3 shows, can become harder to train. The ansatz is an assumption about where good answers lie. If that assumption is wrong, no optimizer can compensate. Physically motivated ansätze, built from the structure of the problem’s Hamiltonian, encode this assumption deliberately; hardware-efficient ansätze, built from whatever gates a device does well, make it implicitly. Problems Verify the trial state by applying \\(R_y(\\theta)\\) and CNOT to \\(\\ket{00}\\). Which value of \\(\\theta\\) gives \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket{00}-\\ket{11})\\)? Is it in the family? An ansatz on \\(n\\) qubits has \\(m\\) real parameters. Why can it not reach every \\(n\\)-qubit state if \\(m<2^{n+1}-2\\)?"}, {"title": "10.1.2 Objectives as expectation values", "chapter": "Variational Methods", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#topic-10-1-2", "text": "10.1.2 Objectives as expectation values § cost function · expectation value · measurement basis change A variational algorithm minimizes a cost function , usually the expectation value of a Hamiltonian in the trial state: \\[E(\\theta)=\\bra{\\psi(\\theta)}H\\ket{\\psi(\\theta)}.\\] The quantum computer prepares the state and measures; the classical computer averages. For \\(H=\\sum_jh_jP_j\\), each Pauli string is measured separately: rotate each qubit so its Pauli becomes \\(Z\\) (a measurement basis change : \\(H\\) for \\(X\\), \\(S^\\dagger\\) then \\(H\\) for \\(Y\\)), measure in the computational basis, and multiply the outcomes \\(\\pm1\\). The average of that product estimates the expectation value \\(\\langle P_j\\rangle\\). Take \\(H=-Z\\otimes Z-X\\otimes X\\), whose ground state is the Bell state with energy \\(-2\\). In the trial state, \\(Z\\otimes Z\\) gives \\(+1\\) on both \\(\\ket{00}\\) and \\(\\ket{11}\\), so \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle=1\\). And \\(X\\otimes X\\) swaps \\(\\ket{00}\\leftrightarrow\\ket{11}\\), so \\(\\langle XX\\rangle=2\\cos\\frac\\theta2\\sin\\frac\\theta2=\\sin\\theta\\). Therefore \\[E(\\theta)=-1-\\sin\\theta,\\] minimized at \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\), where \\(E=-2\\): the trial family contains the exact ground state. Pauli strings that commute qubit by qubit, such as \\(Z_1Z_2\\) and \\(Z_1\\), can be measured together from the same shots. Grouping terms this way reduces the number of distinct circuits. For molecules with thousands of terms, grouping and other measurement strategies change the cost by orders of magnitude. Problems Compute \\(E(\\theta)\\) at \\(\\theta=0,\\pi/2,\\pi\\). Which measurement circuits estimate \\(\\langle Z_1Z_2\\rangle\\) and \\(\\langle X_1X_2\\rangle\\)? Can they share shots? Is \\(E(\\theta)\\) ever below the true ground energy? Why not?"}, {"title": "10.1.3 Measurement budgets", "chapter": "Variational Methods", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#topic-10-1-3", "text": "10.1.3 Measurement budgets § shot noise · shot allocation Each measurement of a Pauli string gives \\(\\pm1\\), so its variance is \\(1-\\langle P\\rangle^2\\le1\\). Averaging \\(R\\) independent shots gives an estimate with variance at most \\(1/R\\). This statistical uncertainty is shot noise . For a sum \\(H=\\sum_jh_jP_j\\) estimated term by term with \\(R_j\\) shots for term \\(j\\), \\[\\mathrm{Var}(\\hat E)=\\sum_j\\frac{h_j^2\\,\\sigma_j^2}{R_j},\\qquad\\sigma_j^2=1-\\langle P_j\\rangle^2.\\] How should a fixed total \\(R=\\sum_jR_j\\) be divided? Minimizing the variance gives \\(R_j\\propto|h_j|\\sigma_j\\), and then \\[\\mathrm{Var}(\\hat E)=\\frac{\\big(\\sum_j|h_j|\\sigma_j\\big)^2}{R}\\le\\frac{\\lambda^2}{R},\\] with \\(\\lambda=\\sum_j|h_j|\\), the same 1-norm that set the block-encoding cost in Chapter 7. To reach precision \\(\\epsilon\\) in the energy takes about \\(\\lambda^2/\\epsilon^2\\) shots. This shot allocation rule is the measurement analogue of choosing PREPARE amplitudes proportional to \\(\\sqrt{|h_j|}\\). The \\(1/\\epsilon^2\\) scaling is the price of measuring instead of using phase estimation , which reaches \\(1/\\epsilon\\) with long coherent circuits (Section 4.2.3). For chemistry, with \\(\\lambda\\) in the tens or hundreds of hartree and target precision around \\(10^{-3}\\) hartree, \\(\\lambda^2/\\epsilon^2\\) can reach \\(10^{9}\\) to \\(10^{11}\\) shots per energy evaluation, and an optimization needs many evaluations. Measurement cost is often the bottleneck of variational methods. Problems For \\(H=-ZZ-XX\\), with 1000 shots on each term, bound the standard deviation of \\(\\hat E\\). A Hamiltonian has two terms with \\(|h_1|=3\\), \\(|h_2|=1\\), and \\(\\sigma_1=\\sigma_2=1\\). With \\(R=4000\\) shots, compare equal allocation with the optimal one. How many shots are needed to estimate an energy to \\(\\epsilon=10^{-3}\\) for a Hamiltonian with \\(\\lambda=50\\), by the bound above? How does phase estimation compare?"}, {"title": "10.2.1 Parameter-shift gradients", "chapter": "Variational Methods", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#topic-10-2-1", "text": "10.2.1 Parameter-shift gradients § parameter-shift rule · gradient descent Gradient descent updates \\(\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta-\\eta\\,\\partial E/\\partial\\theta\\) with a step size \\(\\eta\\). How do we get the derivative from measurements? Finite differences, \\((E(\\theta+h)-E(\\theta-h))/2h\\), are biased for finite \\(h\\) and amplify shot noise as \\(h\\to0\\). There is something better. Suppose \\(\\theta\\) enters the circuit once, through a gate \\(e^{-i\\theta P/2}\\) with \\(P^2=I\\) (any Pauli rotation). Then \\(E(\\theta)\\) has the form \\(a+b\\cos\\theta+c\\sin\\theta\\): the gate contributes \\(\\cos\\frac\\theta2\\) and \\(\\sin\\frac\\theta2\\) to the amplitudes, and the expectation value is quadratic in those, hence a trigonometric polynomial of frequency one. For such a function, the parameter-shift rule holds exactly: \\[\\frac{dE}{d\\theta}=\\frac{E(\\theta+\\pi/2)-E(\\theta-\\pi/2)}{2}.\\] Check on our example: \\[\\frac12\\big(-1-\\sin(\\theta+\\frac\\pi2)+1+\\sin(\\theta-\\frac\\pi2)\\big)=\\frac12(-\\cos\\theta-\\cos\\theta)=-\\cos\\theta\\], which is the derivative of \\(-1-\\sin\\theta\\). The shifts are large, \\(\\pm\\pi/2\\), so the two evaluations differ by a quantity of order one and shot noise is not amplified. Each parameter needs two circuit evaluations per gradient component, so a gradient of \\(m\\) parameters costs \\(2m\\) energy estimates, each with its own shot budget. If one parameter controls several gates, apply the rule to each occurrence separately and add the results (the chain rule); applying a single shift to the shared parameter would be wrong. Problems Use the parameter-shift rule at \\(\\theta=0\\) for \\(E=-1-\\sin\\theta\\). Which way should \\(\\theta\\) move? Show that the rule is exact for \\(E(\\theta)=a+b\\cos\\theta+c\\sin\\theta\\). A parameter appears in two gates, \\(e^{-i\\theta X_1/2}\\) and \\(e^{-i\\theta X_2/2}\\). Why does the simple rule fail, and what is the correct procedure?"}, {"title": "10.2.2 Shot noise and hardware noise", "chapter": "Variational Methods", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#topic-10-2-2", "text": "10.2.2 Shot noise and hardware noise § hardware noise · stochastic optimization A gradient estimated from finitely many shots is a random variable. Gradient descent with noisy gradients is stochastic optimization : on average it moves downhill, but each step jitters. Near a minimum, the true gradient becomes small and the noise dominates, so the optimizer wanders. A sensible stopping rule halts when the estimated improvement is smaller than its uncertainty, rather than chasing digits the measurements cannot resolve. Increasing shots as the optimization proceeds is a common compromise. Hardware noise is different in kind. Decoherence and gate errors bias the measured energy, typically toward the energy of a mixed state, and more shots do not remove a bias. They only estimate the biased value more precisely. Error mitigation techniques, such as extrapolating results from deliberately amplified noise back to zero noise, reduce the bias at a cost in shots that grows rapidly with circuit size. They do not provide the guarantees of error correction. Short circuits are the reason variational methods were proposed for noisy devices: fewer gates means less accumulated error. But short circuits limit expressiveness, and the measurement overhead of Section 10.1.3, multiplied by the number of optimizer iterations and gradient components, can be enormous. These pressures pull in opposite directions, and their balance is problem dependent. Problems A gradient component is estimated with standard deviation \\(0.05\\), and the optimizer uses step size \\(\\eta=0.1\\). How much does the parameter jitter per step from noise alone? A noisy device reports \\(E=-1.8\\) at the optimum of our example, with standard error \\(0.001\\). The exact minimum is \\(-2\\). What does the discrepancy indicate? Why does a variational energy that is below the exact ground energy indicate an error, while one above it does not by itself indicate success?"}, {"title": "10.2.3 Barren plateaus and trainability", "chapter": "Variational Methods", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#topic-10-2-3", "text": "10.2.3 Barren plateaus and trainability § barren plateau · trainability For many ansätze, the landscape \\(E(\\theta)\\) becomes extraordinarily flat as the number of qubits grows. McClean, Boixo, Smelyanskiy, Babbush, and Neven (2018) showed that for sufficiently deep random circuits, the variance of any gradient component over random parameter choices decays exponentially in \\(n\\). At a random starting point, the gradient is then exponentially small with high probability, and estimating it to useful accuracy requires exponentially many shots. Such a landscape is a barren plateau . The intuition is concentration of measure. In a space of dimension \\(2^n\\), a random state has nearly the same expectation value for any fixed observable, close to its average over all states. Circuits deep enough to scramble their input behave like random states, so the energy barely depends on the parameters. Barren plateaus also arise from global cost functions (measuring all qubits at once), from too much entanglement with unmeasured qubits, and from hardware noise , which flattens landscapes even for shallow circuits. Remedies include problem-inspired ansätze, local cost functions, careful initialization near a known good state, and layer-by-layer training. Trainability , whether the optimum can be found with reasonable resources, is a separate property from expressiveness, whether the optimum is in the family at all. A recent line of work also suggests that ansätze provably free of barren plateaus often have landscapes that can be estimated efficiently classically, which raises the question of whether they offer any quantum advantage. Problems If gradient components have standard deviation \\(2^{-n/2}\\) across the landscape, how many shots are needed to resolve one at \\(n=40\\)? Our two-qubit example has gradient \\(-\\cos\\theta\\). Why does it not suffer a barren plateau? Initializing parameters so the circuit is close to the identity avoids a barren plateau at the start. Does it guarantee finding the global minimum?"}, {"title": "10.3.1 The variational eigensolver", "chapter": "Variational Methods", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#topic-10-3-1", "text": "10.3.1 The variational eigensolver § variational principle · variational quantum eigensolver The variational principle is the guarantee behind the method. For any normalized state \\(\\ket\\psi=\\sum_kc_k\\ket{E_k}\\) expanded in eigenstates of \\(H\\), \\[\\bra\\psi H\\ket\\psi=\\sum_k|c_k|^2E_k\\ge E_0,\\] with equality exactly when \\(\\ket\\psi\\) is a ground state. So minimizing the energy over a family of trial states gives an upper bound on the ground energy, and the minimizer is the family’s best approximation in energy. The variational quantum eigensolver ( Peruzzo et al., 2014 ) combines a parameterized circuit , the measurement procedure of Section 10.1.2, and a classical optimizer. In our example the family contains the exact ground state and the optimizer finds it. In general three gaps separate the answer from the truth: the expressiveness gap (the best state in the family has energy above \\(E_0\\)), the optimization gap (the optimizer finds a local minimum or stops early), and the estimation gap (shot noise and hardware bias in the reported energy). The variational principle bounds the energy, not the state. A trial state with energy close to \\(E_0\\) can still be far from the ground state if the gap to the first excited state is small: energy error \\(\\delta\\) implies ground-state overlap at least \\(1-\\delta/\\Delta\\), where \\(\\Delta\\) is the gap. Properties other than energy computed from a variational state inherit this uncertainty. Problems A trial state has \\(|c_0|^2=0.9\\) and \\(|c_1|^2=0.1\\) on the ground and first excited states, with \\(E_0=-2\\), \\(E_1=0\\). What is its energy? Prove the overlap bound: if \\(\\bra\\psi H\\ket\\psi\\le E_0+\\delta\\) and all excited energies are at least \\(E_0+\\Delta\\), then \\(|c_0|^2\\ge1-\\delta/\\Delta\\). Why can the variational eigensolver not, by itself, certify that it has found the ground state?"}, {"title": "10.3.2 The quantum approximate optimization algorithm", "chapter": "Variational Methods", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#topic-10-3-2", "text": "10.3.2 The quantum approximate optimization algorithm § QAOA · mixer For a combinatorial problem with cost \\(C(x)\\) on bit strings, the quantum approximate optimization algorithm ( QAOA , Farhi, Goldstone, and Gutmann, 2014 ) prepares \\[\\ket{\\gamma,\\beta}=e^{-i\\beta_pB}e^{-i\\gamma_pC}\\cdots e^{-i\\beta_1B}e^{-i\\gamma_1C}\\ket+^{\\otimes n},\\qquad B=\\sum_jX_j,\\] with \\(2p\\) parameters, and measures to sample bit strings. The cost layer \\(e^{-i\\gamma C}\\) is diagonal: it only changes phases, so by itself it cannot change any measurement probability. The mixer \\(e^{-i\\beta B}\\) does not commute with \\(C\\), and it converts phase differences into probability differences, the same partnership of phase and interference that drove every algorithm since Chapter 1. As \\(p\\to\\infty\\), suitable parameters approximate adiabatic evolution (Section 9.3), so QAOA can do at least as well as the adiabatic algorithm in that limit. Take MaxCut on one edge: \\(C=\\frac12(I-Z_1Z_2)\\), which is \\(1\\) when the bits differ. At depth \\(p=1\\), a direct calculation gives the expected cut \\[F(\\gamma,\\beta)=\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12\\sin(4\\beta)\\sin\\gamma,\\] which equals \\(1\\) at \\(\\gamma=\\pi/2\\), \\(\\beta=\\pi/8\\): one layer finds the cut with certainty. For larger graphs, what is proved is modest. On 3-regular graphs, depth-1 QAOA guarantees a cut at least \\(0.6924\\) times the maximum, below the classical Goemans–Williamson guarantee of about \\(0.878\\). At higher depth, performance improves, but proven guarantees are few, and the parameters must be optimized for each instance or transferred from similar ones. Optimizing the expected cost and producing a single high-quality string are related but different goals: the distribution of samples matters, and the best of many samples is what a user keeps. Problems Show that \\(e^{-i\\gamma C}\\) alone leaves the uniform distribution unchanged. Evaluate \\(F(\\gamma,\\beta)\\) at \\(\\gamma=\\pi/2\\), \\(\\beta=\\pi/8\\) and at \\(\\gamma=\\pi/2\\), \\(\\beta=3\\pi/8\\). Interpret the second. Why does a high expected cost not guarantee that the best sampled string is optimal?"}, {"title": "10.3.3 Evidence and benchmarking", "chapter": "Variational Methods", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#topic-10-3-3", "text": "10.3.3 Evidence and benchmarking § benchmarking · heuristic Variational algorithms are heuristics : procedures that may work well in practice without a proof that they do. Heuristics are valuable. Most of classical optimization runs on them. But claims about them must rest on evidence, and the evidence must be of the right kind. Good benchmarking compares against the best classical methods for the same instances, including tuned heuristics and specialized solvers, not a convenient baseline. It reports how performance scales with problem size, since a fixed-size advantage can be overtaken. It counts total resources: shots, optimizer iterations, calibration, and classical preprocessing, and reports time-to-solution or cost at matched solution quality. It uses instance families not chosen to favour the method. And it states what was simulated, what was run on hardware, and what was extrapolated. Two further cautions. Shallow circuits on restricted geometries can sometimes be simulated classically, for example with tensor networks, so small instances showing good quantum results do not show a quantum advantage. And the absence of proven guarantees means that negative results matter as much as positive ones. Variational methods have taught much about devices and problems; whether they offer an asymptotic advantage for any practical task is an open question. Throughout, measurement has served as the last step, or as feedback to a classical optimizer between circuits. Chapter 11 moves measurement inside the computation: outcomes chosen mid-circuit steer later operations, reduce coherent resources, and even perform the computation itself. Problems A study reports that QAOA at depth 3 beat a greedy classical algorithm on 20-node graphs. List three things missing before concluding an advantage. Why can classical simulation of the variational circuit be part of good benchmarking, rather than a threat to it? “The variational method found the exact ground state of a 4-qubit Hamiltonian.” What does this demonstrate, and what does it not?"}, {"title": "Variational Methods — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#exercise-1", "text": "For the ansatz cos(θ/2)|00⟩ + sin(θ/2)|11⟩, compute ⟨Z₁⟩ and ⟨Z₁Z₂⟩ as functions of θ. ⟨Z₁⟩ = cos²(θ/2) − sin²(θ/2) = cos θ, and ⟨Z₁Z₂⟩ = 1 for every θ. So a Hamiltonian H = −Z₁ would have E(θ) = −cos θ, minimized at θ = 0 (the state |00⟩)."}, {"title": "Variational Methods — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#exercise-2", "text": "In the running example at θ=π/6, ⟨ X₁X₂⟩=sinθ=1/2. Find the variance of one ±1 shot of X₁X₂ and the standard deviation of the average of 1000 shots. Why does ⟨ Z₁Z₂⟩ need no shots at all for this ansatz? The single-shot variance is 1−1/4=3/4, so 1000 shots give standard deviation √(0.75/1000)≈0.027. Every trial state is a combination of |00⟩ and |11⟩, both with Z₁Z₂=+1, so every shot returns +1. The variance is 0 and ⟨ Z₁Z₂⟩=1 is known exactly. Variances depend on the state, which is why the optimal shot allocation Rⱼpropto|hⱼ|sigmaⱼ can save far more than the worst-case bound λ²/ε² suggests."}, {"title": "Variational Methods — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#exercise-3", "text": "For depth-1 QAOA on a single edge, set γ=π. Compute the state after the cost layer, starting from |++⟩, and explain why no mixer angle β can then improve the expected cut beyond 1/2. Compare with the formula F=1/2+1/2sin4βsinγ. After the cost layer the state is 1/2(|00⟩−|01⟩−|10⟩+|11⟩)=|−⟩|−⟩. That is an eigenstate of X₁+X₂ (eigenvalue −2), so the mixer only multiplies it by a global phase. The measured distribution stays uniform, with expected cut 1/2 for every β. The formula agrees: sinπ=0. The cost phases e^(−iγ C) must create relative phases that the mixer can turn into probability differences; at γ=π the phases form a pattern the mixer cannot use."}, {"title": "Variational Methods — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#exercise-4", "text": "Use the parameter-shift rule to compute the gradient of E(θ) = −1 − sin θ at θ = π/3, and take one gradient-descent step with η = 0.5. E(π/3 + π/2) = −1 − cos(π/3) = −1.5 and E(π/3 − π/2) = −1 + cos(π/3) = −0.5, so dE/dθ = (−1.5 + 0.5)/2 = −0.5 = −cos(π/3). The step gives θ = π/3 + 0.25 ≈ 1.297, closer to the optimum π/2 ≈ 1.571; the energy falls from −1.866 to about −1.963."}, {"title": "Variational Methods — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#exercise-5", "text": "Add a field to the example: H=−Z₁Z₂−X₁X₂+0.5 Z₁. (a) Show that for the ansatz cosθ/2|00⟩+sinθ/2|11⟩, E(θ)=−1−sinθ+0.5cosθ. (b) Find the minimum and its location. (c) Show that H maps the span of |00⟩,|11⟩ to itself and find its lowest eigenvalue there. Does the ansatz contain the ground state? (a) As before ⟨ Z₁Z₂⟩=1 and ⟨ X₁X₂⟩=sinθ, and ⟨ Z₁⟩=cosθ, giving E=−1−sinθ+0.5cosθ. (b) E=−1−√1.25sin(θ−varphi) with varphi=arctan0.5≈0.464. The minimum is −1−√1.25≈−2.118 at θ=π/2+varphi≈2.03. (c) Each term maps |00⟩ and |11⟩ into their span. In the basis (|00⟩,|11⟩), H=beginpmatrix−0.5&−1\\−1&−1.5endpmatrix, with eigenvalues −1±√1.25. The odd sector {|01⟩,|10⟩} has eigenvalues 1±√1.25, the lowest being −0.118. So the true ground energy is −2.118, and the ansatz attains it. It succeeds because it covers every real state in the symmetry sector that contains the ground state. Without that structure, the family could easily miss the ground state."}, {"title": "Variational Methods — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#exercise-6", "text": "A variational run reports energy −1.95 for a Hamiltonian with E₀=−2. (a) If every excited energy is at least 0 (gap Delta=2), bound the ground-state overlap |c₀|². (b) Repeat for a gap Delta=0.1. (c) An observable O with |O|≤1 is computed from the variational state. Using |c₀|², roughly how far can its value be from the ground-state value in each case? (a) |c₀|²≥1−0.05/2=0.975. (b) |c₀|²≥1−0.05/0.1=0.5, so up to half the weight may lie on excited states. (c) For a pure state, the distance from the ground state is √(1−|c₀|²). This is at most about 0.16 in case (a) and up to about 0.71 in case (b), and expectation values of O can be off by twice that. The same energy error means very different things depending on the gap. A small gap makes energy a poor certificate of the state, so a reported variational energy does not validate other computed properties unless a gap bound is known."}, {"title": "Variational Methods — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#exercise-7", "text": "Verify the depth-1 QAOA formula for one edge at γ=π/2 by working in the two-dimensional space spanned by |Φ⁺⟩=1/√2(|00⟩+|11⟩) and |Psi⁺⟩=1/√2(|01⟩+|10⟩). (a) Write the state after the cost layer in this basis. (b) Show that B=X₁+X₂ acts as 2sigma_x there. (c) Compute the probability of an uncut outcome and the expected cut as a function of β. (a) |++⟩=1/√2(|Φ⁺⟩+|Psi⁺⟩); after the cost layer it is 1/√2(|Φ⁺⟩−i|Psi⁺⟩). (b) X₁+X₂ sends |00⟩+|11⟩ to 2(|01⟩+|10⟩), so B|Φ⁺⟩=2|Psi⁺⟩ and B|Psi⁺⟩=2|Φ⁺⟩. (c) Applying cos2β I−isin2β sigma_x gives the |Φ⁺⟩ amplitude 1/√2(cos2β−sin2β). The uncut probability is 1/2(cos2β−sin2β)²=1/2(1−sin4β), so the expected cut is F=1/2(1+sin4β), matching the formula with sinγ=1. At β=π/8 the uncut amplitude vanishes and F=1. The symmetry of the problem reduced a four-dimensional calculation to a rotation in a plane."}, {"title": "Variational Methods — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#exercise-8", "text": "An optimizer for E(θ)=−1−sinθ starts at θ₀=3π/2, computes an exact gradient of zero, and stops, reporting convergence. What has it found? What would happen with shot noise, and what does this say about using a zero gradient as a certificate? E(3π/2)=−1+1=0, the maximum of the landscape, not the minimum −2 at θ=π/2. The gradient vanishes at every stationary point: minima, maxima, and saddles. With shot noise the estimated gradient would be slightly nonzero, and the optimizer would drift away from this unstable point, so noise happens to help here. In larger landscapes, local minima are stable and noise does not rescue the run. A small gradient shows only that the optimizer has stopped moving. It says nothing about global optimality, and in a barren plateau it may simply reflect a landscape too flat to measure."}, {"title": "Variational Methods — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#exercise-9", "text": "A Hamiltonian has λ = 20, and each optimizer iteration estimates the energy and a 50-component gradient by parameter shift, each energy to precision 10⁻². Estimate the shots per iteration, and comment on 500 iterations. One energy: 400/10⁻⁴ = 4 × 10⁶ shots. Gradient: 100 energies, 4 × 10⁸ shots. Per iteration about 4 × 10⁸ shots; 500 iterations need about 2 × 10¹¹ shots. At 10⁴ shots per second that is about 230 days, illustrating why measurement cost dominates variational methods and why shot-frugal optimizers and measurement grouping matter."}, {"title": "Variational Methods — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "10-variational-methods.html#exercise-10", "text": "For H=−Z₁Z₂−X₁X₂ at θ=π/3, use the optimal allocation Rⱼpropto|hⱼ|sigmaⱼ. (a) Compute sigma₁,sigma₂ and the shots needed for standard error 10⁻³ in the energy. (b) Compare with the worst-case estimate λ²/ε². (c) The allocation uses the very expectation values being estimated. Propose a practical procedure, and say what it costs. (a) sigma₁=√(1−1)=0 and sigma₂=√(1−0.75)=0.5. The optimal allocation gives all shots to X₁X₂, and Var=(0.5)²/R. Standard error 10⁻³ needs R=0.25/10⁻⁶=2.5×10⁵ shots. (b) The worst case λ²/ε²=4/10⁻⁶=4×10⁶ is sixteen times larger. (c) Spend a small pilot budget on every term to estimate each sigmaⱼ, then allocate the remaining shots proportionally to |hⱼ|hatsigmaⱼ, keeping a minimum for every term in case a pilot estimate was misleading. The pilot costs a modest fraction of the total. Since sigmaⱼ changes slowly as θ moves during optimization, earlier estimates can be reused."}, {"title": "11.1.1 Branches and classical records", "chapter": "Adaptive Measurement", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#topic-11-1-1", "text": "11.1.1 Branches and classical records § mid-circuit measurement · classical record · feedforward A mid-circuit measurement on part of a register splits the computation into branches, one per outcome. For a computational-basis measurement of one qubit, the branch operators are the projectors \\(M_0=\\ket0\\bra0\\otimes I\\) and \\(M_1=\\ket1\\bra1\\otimes I\\). Outcome \\(b\\) occurs with probability \\[p_b=\\|M_b\\ket\\psi\\|^2,\\] and leaves the normalized state \\(M_b\\ket\\psi/\\sqrt{p_b}\\). The outcome is stored as a classical record , a bit that later operations can read. Using it to choose a later gate \\(V_b\\) is feedforward : the branch with outcome \\(b\\) continues as \\(V_bM_b\\ket\\psi/\\sqrt{p_b}\\). Two features distinguish this from coherent computation. First, branches with different records never interfere again: the record is a which-path record held in a classical register, and classical registers do not go into superposition. Probabilities of branches add; their amplitudes do not. Second, if we ignore the record, the state is described by the mixture of branches, written as a density operator \\(\\sum_bV_bM_b\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi M_b^\\dagger V_b^\\dagger\\), a description that weights each branch by its probability. Example: prepare \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})\\), measure the first qubit, and if the outcome is \\(1\\) apply \\(X\\) to the second. Both branches end with the second qubit in \\(\\ket0\\). Feedforward has turned a random outcome into a deterministic state, which is the essential trick of teleportation and of everything in Section 11.3. Problems For \\(\\ket\\psi=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\ket{00}+\\sqrt{\\frac23}\\ket{11}\\), give \\(p_0\\), \\(p_1\\), and the post-measurement states when the first qubit is measured. Measure the first qubit of \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket{0}+\\ket{1})\\ket0\\), then apply \\(H\\) to the first qubit. Compare with applying \\(H\\) without measuring. Why can a zero-probability outcome be ignored when writing the conditional states?"}, {"title": "11.1.2 Deferred measurement", "chapter": "Adaptive Measurement", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#topic-11-1-2", "text": "11.1.2 Deferred measurement § deferred measurement principle A measurement followed by a classically controlled gate can be replaced by a quantum-controlled gate with the measurement moved to the end. If a qubit is measured in the computational basis and the outcome controls \\(V\\) on other qubits, then using that qubit as the control of a controlled-\\(V\\), and measuring it afterwards, gives the same joint distribution of all final outcomes. This is the deferred measurement principle . Why does it hold? The controlled gate acts as \\(\\ket0\\bra0\\otimes I+\\ket1\\bra1\\otimes V\\). It never changes the control qubit’s computational-basis value, so the final measurement of that qubit returns the same statistics it would have earlier, and on each value the target receives exactly the same operation as under feedforward. Since nothing afterwards acts on the control qubit, the two procedures cannot be distinguished by their outputs. The principle is a statement about output statistics, not about resources. The deferred version keeps the measured qubit alive until the end, so it cannot be reset and reused; it needs a controlled gate, which may be more expensive than a classically controlled one; and it must remain coherent longer. The measured version can recycle the qubit immediately but requires fast classical feedback during the computation. Which is cheaper depends on the hardware. Section 11.2 uses the principle in reverse: it starts from a coherent circuit and replaces quantum controls by measurements and classical control, saving qubits. Problems Apply the deferred version of the Bell example: CNOT from the first qubit to the second instead of measure-then-\\(X\\). What is the final state before measuring the first qubit? Does deferred measurement apply if, after the measurement, a gate acts on the measured qubit itself, for example a Hadamard? A device has 10 qubits and fast mid-circuit measurement. An algorithm in coherent form needs 20 qubits, 10 of which are used only as controls and then measured. How does deferred measurement in reverse help?"}, {"title": "11.1.3 Measurement-dependent corrections", "chapter": "Adaptive Measurement", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#topic-11-1-3", "text": "11.1.3 Measurement-dependent corrections § Pauli correction · repeat-until-success Feedforward is most useful when every outcome leads to the intended result after a cheap fix. Here is a small example. In the Hadamard-test circuit of Section 3.1.2, take the controlled unitary to be \\(U=e^{i\\theta X}\\). The two outcomes apply \\[\\text{outcome }0:\\ \\frac{I+U}{2}=\\cos\\tfrac\\theta2\\,e^{i\\theta X/2},\\qquad\\text{outcome }1:\\ \\frac{I-U}{2}=-i\\sin\\tfrac\\theta2\\,e^{i\\theta X/2}X.\\] Both branches apply the half-angle rotation \\(e^{i\\theta X/2}\\), with an extra \\(X\\) on outcome \\(1\\). Since \\(X\\) commutes with \\(e^{i\\theta X/2}\\), applying \\(X\\) again after outcome \\(1\\) gives \\(e^{i\\theta X/2}\\) deterministically, for every input. The outcome probabilities are \\(\\cos^2\\frac\\theta2\\) and \\(\\sin^2\\frac\\theta2\\), independent of the input, so the measurement reveals nothing about the data. A fix that is a Pauli gate is a Pauli correction , and Pauli corrections are cheap in nearly every hardware and error-correction scheme. A related pattern is repeat-until-success . Some circuits apply the desired gate on one outcome and, on the other, a known unitary that can be undone cheaply. On failure, undo and try again. The number of attempts is random, but the final result is exact, and the expected cost can be lower than a deterministic decomposition. For single-qubit rotations in Clifford+\\(T\\) circuits, repeat-until-success constructions reduce the expected \\(T\\) count below that of the best deterministic synthesis by a constant factor ( Paetznick and Svore, 2014 ). Postselection without a way to undo failures, as in Section 7.1.3, is not of this kind: there a failure destroys the input. Problems Verify \\((I+e^{i\\theta X})/2=\\cos\\frac\\theta2\\,e^{i\\theta X/2}\\). For \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\), what are the outcome probabilities, and what gate is applied after correction? A repeat-until-success circuit succeeds with probability \\(3/4\\) per attempt and costs 4 \\(T\\) gates per attempt. What is the expected \\(T\\) count?"}, {"title": "11.2.1 Iterative phase estimation", "chapter": "Adaptive Measurement", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#topic-11-2-1", "text": "11.2.1 Iterative phase estimation § iterative phase estimation Let \\(U\\ket u=e^{2\\pi i\\phi}\\ket u\\), with \\(\\phi=0.b_1b_2\\cdots b_m\\) exactly in binary. We learn the bits one at a time, from the last to the first, with one control qubit that is measured and reused. At stage \\(k\\) (running from \\(m\\) down to \\(1\\)), prepare the control in \\(\\ket+\\) and apply controlled-\\(U^{2^{k-1}}\\). The kicked-back phase is \\(2\\pi\\cdot2^{k-1}\\phi\\) modulo \\(2\\pi\\), which is \\(2\\pi\\,(0.b_kb_{k+1}\\cdots b_m)\\): multiplying by \\(2^{k-1}\\) shifts the binary point past the first \\(k-1\\) digits, which contribute whole turns. The bits after \\(b_k\\) are already known from earlier stages. Remove their contribution with a phase gate on the control, \\[P(-\\omega_k),\\qquad\\omega_k=2\\pi\\,(0.0\\,b_{k+1}\\cdots b_m),\\] leaving phase \\(2\\pi\\,(0.b_k)=\\pi b_k\\): the control is \\(\\ket+\\) if \\(b_k=0\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) if \\(b_k=1\\). A Hadamard and measurement return \\(b_k\\) with certainty. This is iterative phase estimation , the deferred-measurement inverse of the textbook circuit (Section 11.1.2). The resources compare as follows. Qubits: one control instead of \\(m\\). Controlled-\\(U\\) uses: \\(2^m-1\\) in total, exactly as before, since stage \\(k\\) still needs \\(U^{2^{k-1}}\\). Coherence: each stage needs coherence only for its own controlled power, not for the whole computation. Classical feedback: the correction angle \\(\\omega_k\\) depends on earlier outcomes, so the controller must compute it between stages. Iterative estimation saves width, not total evolution. Problems For \\(\\phi=1/4=0.01_2\\) with \\(m=2\\), carry out both stages. What happens at stage 1 if the correction is omitted, for \\(\\phi=1/4\\)? Why are the bits measured from least significant to most significant, rather than the other way?"}, {"title": "11.2.2 A worked example with feedback", "chapter": "Adaptive Measurement", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#topic-11-2-2", "text": "11.2.2 A worked example with feedback § semiclassical Fourier transform Take \\(\\phi=3/8=0.011_2\\), so \\(b_1=0\\), \\(b_2=1\\), \\(b_3=1\\), and \\(m=3\\). Stage 3 , with \\(U^4\\): phase \\(2\\pi\\cdot4\\cdot\\frac38=2\\pi\\cdot\\frac32\\equiv\\pi\\). No correction is needed. The control is \\(\\ket-\\); outcome \\(b_3=1\\). Stage 2 , with \\(U^2\\): phase \\(2\\pi\\cdot\\frac34\\). Subtract \\(\\omega_2=2\\pi\\cdot(0.01)=2\\pi\\cdot\\frac14\\), leaving \\(\\pi\\). Outcome \\(b_2=1\\). Stage 1 , with \\(U\\): phase \\(2\\pi\\cdot\\frac38\\). Subtract \\(\\omega_1=2\\pi\\cdot(0.011)=2\\pi\\cdot\\frac38\\), leaving \\(0\\). Outcome \\(b_1=0\\). The acquisition order is \\(b_3,b_2,b_1\\), but the phase is \\(0.b_1b_2b_3=0.011\\). Recording bits in the wrong order is an easy implementation error that the problems explore. The same structure appears inside the textbook circuit. The inverse QFT’s controlled phase gates subtract the contributions of bits already determined, then a Hadamard reads the next bit. If each qubit of the QFT output is measured as soon as it is final, and the measured value classically controls the remaining phase gates, the output statistics are unchanged, by deferred measurement. This is the semiclassical Fourier transform of Griffiths and Niu (1996) . It needs only single-qubit gates, measurements, and classical control, no two-qubit gates at all, and it is how Shor’s algorithm can run its \\(2n\\)-qubit control register on a single recycled qubit. Problems Repeat the procedure for \\(\\phi=5/8=0.101_2\\). A program stores the bits in acquisition order and reports \\(0.b_3b_2b_1\\). What does it report for \\(\\phi=3/8\\)? How many two-qubit gates does the semiclassical inverse QFT on \\(m\\) qubits use, and what replaces them?"}, {"title": "11.2.3 Precision limits and robust estimation", "chapter": "Adaptive Measurement", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#topic-11-2-3", "text": "11.2.3 Precision limits and robust estimation § Heisenberg limit · standard quantum limit · robust phase estimation The exact examples assumed \\(\\phi\\) has \\(m\\) binary digits. A general phase does not, so each stage’s outcome is probabilistic, and a wrong early bit corrupts the correction for every later one. Practical adaptive schemes therefore repeat stages, use the outcomes as statistical evidence about \\(\\phi\\), and choose later experiments to be informative given what is already known. Bayesian methods keep a probability distribution over \\(\\phi\\) and update it after each outcome; robust phase estimation ( Kimmel, Low, and Yoder, 2015 ) uses a fixed schedule of powers with enough repetitions per power to bound the error even with noise. How precise can any such scheme be? Count resources as total uses of \\(U\\), \\(N\\). Repeating a single Hadamard test \\(N\\) times estimates \\(\\phi\\) with error of order \\(1/\\sqrt N\\): the standard quantum limit , the same \\(1/\\sqrt N\\) as classical sampling. Using powers \\(U^{2^j}\\) and adaptive or Fourier processing reaches error of order \\(1/N\\): the Heisenberg limit . No strategy does better, for the same reason that amplitude estimation cannot beat \\(1/\\epsilon\\) queries (Section 5.4.2). The improvement comes from coherence across many uses of \\(U\\), concentrated in long controlled powers . In the presence of noise that limits how long a controlled power can stay coherent, the Heisenberg scaling holds only up to that coherence time; beyond it, repetition returns to standard scaling. Adaptive estimation is the setting in which this trade-off is managed: use the longest powers that remain reliable, and repeat them. Problems To estimate a phase to \\(10^{-4}\\), roughly how many uses of \\(U\\) does each limit require? Why does an error in an early bit (\\(b_m\\)) of iterative phase estimation damage every later stage? A device keeps controlled powers coherent only up to \\(U^{64}\\). How should an estimate to \\(10^{-5}\\) be organized?"}, {"title": "11.3.1 Teleporting a gate", "chapter": "Adaptive Measurement", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#topic-11-3-1", "text": "11.3.1 Teleporting a gate § gate teleportation · one-bit teleportation Take an input \\(\\ket\\psi=a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\) and an auxiliary qubit in \\(\\ket+\\). Apply a controlled-\\(Z\\) between them, giving \\(a\\ket0\\ket++b\\ket1\\ket-\\). Now measure the input qubit in the basis \\[\\ket{\\pm_\\alpha}=\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\big(\\ket0\\pm e^{i\\alpha}\\ket1\\big).\\] Projecting onto \\(\\ket{\\pm_\\alpha}\\) gives, for outcome \\(m\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) (\\(m=0\\) for \\(+\\)), the unnormalized auxiliary state \\[\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\big(a\\ket++(-1)^me^{-i\\alpha}b\\ket-\\big)=\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\,X^mHP(-\\alpha)\\ket\\psi,\\qquad P(\\beta)=\\mathrm{diag}(1,e^{i\\beta}).\\] Each outcome has probability \\(\\frac12\\), whatever \\(\\ket\\psi\\) is. The state has moved to the auxiliary qubit, and on the way the gate \\(HP(-\\alpha)\\) has been applied, followed by a known Pauli \\(X^m\\) that depends on the outcome. This is one-bit teleportation , and the general idea of applying a gate by teleporting through a suitably prepared resource is gate teleportation . The measurement basis chooses the gate: different \\(\\alpha\\) give different rotations. The Pauli byproduct can be corrected immediately, or tracked classically and accounted for later. Nothing is cloned, since the input qubit is consumed by the measurement, and nothing is sent faster than light, since the byproduct cannot be interpreted without the outcome bit. Problems For \\(\\alpha=0\\), what gate is applied, and what correction does outcome \\(m=1\\) need? Verify the formula for \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket0\\), \\(\\alpha=0\\), outcome \\(m=0\\). Show that the outcome probabilities are independent of \\(\\ket\\psi\\), and explain why that matters."}, {"title": "11.3.2 Cluster states and adaptive bases", "chapter": "Adaptive Measurement", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#topic-11-3-2", "text": "11.3.2 Cluster states and adaptive bases § measurement-based quantum computation · cluster state · Pauli frame Chain the construction. Prepare a line of qubits in \\(\\ket+\\), apply controlled-\\(Z\\) between neighbours, and place the input at one end. Measuring the first qubit with angle \\(\\alpha_1\\) teleports \\(HP(-\\alpha_1)\\ket\\psi\\) onto the second; measuring the second with \\(\\alpha_2\\) teleports \\(HP(-\\alpha_2)HP(-\\alpha_1)\\ket\\psi\\) onto the third; and so on. Since \\(H\\) and \\(P\\) generate all single-qubit gates, a line can apply any single-qubit unitary. On a two-dimensional grid of such entangled qubits, a cluster state , measurements can also apply entangling gates. This is measurement-based quantum computation ( Raussendorf and Briegel, 2001 ): all entanglement is prepared in advance, and the computation consists of single-qubit measurements in adaptively chosen bases. Adaptivity is essential. A byproduct \\(X\\) from one step does not commute with the next step’s phase gate; instead \\(P(\\beta)X=e^{i\\beta}XP(-\\beta)\\), so pushing the \\(X\\) through flips the sign of the next angle. The classical controller therefore chooses each measurement basis using earlier outcomes, flipping signs as needed, and keeps track of the remaining Pauli byproducts, a record called the Pauli frame , to reinterpret the final outcomes. Measurements whose bases do not depend on others can be done simultaneously, so the depth of the computation is set by the chain of classical dependencies. The model is equivalent in power to the circuit model, not more powerful. Its appeal is architectural: photonic platforms, where two-qubit gates are probabilistic but entangled resource states can be built and checked in advance, use it naturally. Its costs are the size of the resource state, the precision of measurement angles, and fast classical feedforward. Problems Two steps with angles \\(\\alpha_1,\\alpha_2\\) and outcomes \\(m_1=0\\), \\(m_2=0\\). What unitary is applied to \\(\\ket\\psi\\)? Verify \\(P(\\beta)X=e^{i\\beta}XP(-\\beta)\\) by applying both sides to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\). If the first outcome is \\(m_1=1\\), how must the second measurement angle be changed to apply the intended \\(HP(-\\alpha_2)HP(-\\alpha_1)\\) up to Paulis?"}, {"title": "11.3.3 Magic-state injection", "chapter": "Adaptive Measurement", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#topic-11-3-3", "text": "11.3.3 Magic-state injection § magic state · state injection · magic-state distillation Error-correcting codes make Clifford gates (\\(H\\), \\(S\\), CNOT) comparatively easy to apply fault-tolerantly, but Clifford gates alone are not universal and can be simulated efficiently classically. The \\(T\\) gate supplies the missing power, and in many codes it cannot be applied directly. Gate teleportation solves this by moving the difficulty into a state. Prepare the magic state \\(\\ket A=T\\ket+=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0+e^{i\\pi/4}\\ket1)\\). With data \\(\\ket\\psi=a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\), apply a CNOT from the data to the magic qubit, and measure the magic qubit: \\[\\text{outcome }0:\\ a\\ket0+e^{i\\pi/4}b\\ket1=T\\ket\\psi,\\qquad\\text{outcome }1:\\ e^{i\\pi/4}\\big(a\\ket0+e^{-i\\pi/4}b\\ket1\\big)\\propto T^\\dagger\\ket\\psi.\\] On outcome \\(1\\), apply \\(S=T^2\\): \\(ST^\\dagger=T\\). Either way the data ends in \\(T\\ket\\psi\\), using only a CNOT, a measurement, and a classically controlled \\(S\\), all Clifford operations. This is state injection . The non-Clifford difficulty has moved into preparing \\(\\ket A\\) with very high fidelity. Magic-state distillation ( Bravyi and Kitaev, 2005 ) takes many noisy copies of \\(\\ket A\\) and, using only Clifford operations and measurements, outputs fewer copies with much lower error; for example, one standard protocol turns 15 copies with error \\(p\\) into one with error about \\(35p^3\\). Distillation dominates the cost of many fault-tolerant resource estimates, which is why algorithms are often costed in \\(T\\) gates or Toffolis (Section 2.3.1), and why the rotation-synthesis costs of Chapters 3, 4, and 9 matter. This closes the technique sequence. Algorithm design, as Chapter 1 promised, has meant choosing compatible access, encoding the property that matters, engineering interference, constructing a readout, proving correctness, and counting every resource. Measurement and feedback, introduced here last, turn out to touch every stage: they shorten circuits, recycle qubits, and even supply the gates. The next course, Quantum Programming, turns these contracts and calculations into executable, tested circuits. Problems Verify the outcome-\\(0\\) branch of state injection for \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket1\\). Why is the correction on outcome \\(1\\) an \\(S\\) gate and not a Pauli? A 15-to-1 distillation protocol maps input error \\(p\\) to about \\(35p^3\\). Starting from \\(p=10^{-3}\\), what error after one round and after two? How many raw states does two rounds consume per output?"}, {"title": "Adaptive Measurement — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#exercise-1", "text": "In one-bit teleportation with α = π/2, what gate is applied (after correcting the byproduct)? HP(−π/2) = HS†. On outcome 1, apply X to remove the byproduct; the auxiliary qubit then holds HS†|ψ⟩."}, {"title": "Adaptive Measurement — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#exercise-2", "text": "Prepare 1/√2(|00⟩+|11⟩) and measure the first qubit in the {|+⟩,|−⟩} basis. What is the second qubit’s state for each outcome, and which feedforward correction makes it |+⟩ in both cases? Since 1/√2(|00⟩+|11⟩)=1/√2(|++⟩+|−−⟩), outcome + leaves the second qubit in |+⟩ and outcome − leaves |−⟩, each with probability 1/2. Applying Z after outcome − gives |+⟩ deterministically. The choice of measurement basis determines which states the partner can be steered to; the correction is a Pauli gate chosen by the record."}, {"title": "Adaptive Measurement — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#exercise-3", "text": "A qubit carries a pending Pauli byproduct X in the Pauli frame. The program then applies H and S, and measures in the computational basis. How does the byproduct propagate, and must the measured bit be flipped? What if no H had been applied? Moving the byproduct through the gates: SHX=SZH=ZSH, so just before the measurement the frame holds Z. A Z does not change computational-basis probabilities, so the measured bit is reported unchanged. Without the H, SX=YS, and Y=iXZ contains an X, which flips a computational-basis outcome, so the classical controller must invert the measured bit. Tracking the frame lets the hardware skip the correction gates; only the interpretation of later outcomes changes."}, {"title": "Adaptive Measurement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#exercise-4", "text": "Run iterative phase estimation for φ = 7/8 = 0.111 in binary, giving each stage’s phase, correction, and outcome. Stage 3: phase 2π·(28/8) ≡ 2π·½, no correction, b₃ = 1. Stage 2: phase 2π·(14/8) ≡ 2π·¾, subtract 2π·¼, leaving π: b₂ = 1. Stage 1: phase 2π·⅞, subtract 2π·(0.011)₂ = 2π·⅜, leaving 2π·½ = π: b₁ = 1. Result 0.111 = 7/8."}, {"title": "Adaptive Measurement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#exercise-5", "text": "Run two-stage iterative phase estimation on an eigenstate with φ=1/3, which has no finite binary expansion. (a) Compute the probability of each outcome at stage 2 (controlled-U², no correction). (b) For each stage-2 outcome, apply the correction P(−omega₁) with omega₁=2π(0.0b₂) and compute the stage-1 probabilities. (c) Tabulate the probabilities of the four estimates 0.b₁b₂ and compare them with textbook phase estimation with M=4. (a) Stage 2 sees phase 4π/3, so Pr[b₂=0]=(1+cos4π/3)/2=1/4 and Pr[b₂=1]=3/4. (b) If b₂=0, there is no correction and stage 1 sees 2π/3: Pr[b₁=0]=1/4. If b₂=1, omega₁=π/2 and stage 1 sees 2π/3−π/2=π/6: Pr[b₁=0]=(1+cosπ/6)/2≈0.933. (c) The estimates 0.00,0.01,0.10,0.11 (that is 0,1/4,1/2,3/4) have probabilities 1/4·1/4=0.0625, 3/4×0.933≈0.700, 1/4·3/4=0.1875, and 3/4×0.067≈0.050. Textbook phase estimation with M=4 gives exactly the same four numbers. The two procedures are related by deferred measurement, so their output statistics agree even when the phase is not exact."}, {"title": "Adaptive Measurement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#exercise-6", "text": "In teleportation, the state just before Alice measures is 1/2Σ_(m₁,m₂)|m₁m₂⟩ X^(m₂)Z^(m₁)|ψ⟩. Apply the deferred-measurement principle: replace the classically controlled corrections by a CNOT from Alice’s second qubit and a controlled-Z from her first qubit to Bob’s. Find the final three-qubit state, and explain what Alice’s later measurements reveal. On the branch |m₁m₂⟩, the controlled gates apply Z^(m₁)X^(m₂) to Bob’s qubit, turning X^(m₂)Z^(m₁)|ψ⟩ into |ψ⟩. The final state is 1/2Σ_(m₁,m₂)|m₁m₂⟩|ψ⟩=|+⟩|+⟩|ψ⟩. Bob holds |ψ⟩, unentangled with Alice, and Alice’s qubits are in a fixed state that carries no information about ψ. Measuring them gives two uniformly random bits. The deferred version gives the same statistics as the original, but it needs quantum control from Alice’s qubits to Bob’s, so it is no longer a protocol between distant parties. Classical communication is what lets teleportation work at a distance."}, {"title": "Adaptive Measurement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#exercise-7", "text": "On a three-qubit line cluster, the input sits on qubit 1. Measuring qubit 1 with angle α₁ and qubit 2 with angle α₂ applies X^(m₂)HP(−α₂)X^(m₁)HP(−α₁) to the input, which ends on qubit 3. (a) Choose α₁,α₂ to apply T, up to byproducts, when both outcomes are 0. (b) For m₁=1, m₂=0, find the byproduct on the output and say whether the second angle needs adapting. (a) With α₂=0, the composite is HHP(−α₁)=P(−α₁). Choosing α₁=−π/4 gives P(π/4)=T. (b) With m₁=1, the operation is HP(0)XHT=HXHT=ZT, so the output is ZT|ψ⟩. A Z byproduct is recorded in the Pauli frame and corrected later, or absorbed into the interpretation of a final measurement. Because α₂=0, flipping its sign changes nothing, so no adaptation is needed here. For a general α₂, the outcome m₁=1 would require measuring with −α₂ instead, since P(β)X=e^(iβ)XP(−β)."}, {"title": "Adaptive Measurement — Misconception", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student writes: “Iterative phase estimation uses one control qubit and m measurements, so it needs only m applications of controlled-U instead of 2^m−1.” Correct the claim, state what iterative phase estimation actually saves, and say when the number of controlled-U applications really is small. Stage k applies controlled-U^(2ᵏ⁻¹). Built from repeated controlled-U, the stages cost 1+2+⋯+2^(m−1)=2^m−1 applications in total, exactly as in the textbook circuit. Iterative estimation saves width: one recycled control qubit instead of m, and no coherent inverse QFT. It also shortens the required coherence time of the control. The count of controlled powers is small only when U^(2ʲ) has a shortcut, as for modular multiplication, where U_a^(2ʲ)=U_(a^(2ʲ) mod  L) costs the same as U_a. For Hamiltonian evolution there is generally no such shortcut, and the total evolution time grows as 2^m, as the Heisenberg limit requires."}, {"title": "Adaptive Measurement — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#exercise-9", "text": "A fault-tolerant algorithm needs 10⁸ T gates with total failure probability below 1%. Raw magic states have error 10⁻³, and the 15-to-1 protocol maps error p to 35p³. How many rounds of distillation are needed, and roughly how many raw magic states per T gate? Per-gate error must be below about 10⁻²/10⁸ = 10⁻¹⁰. One round gives 3.5 × 10⁻⁸, not enough; two rounds give about 1.5 × 10⁻²¹, more than enough. Two rounds consume about 15² = 225 raw states per output T gate (more with failed rounds), so about 2 × 10¹⁰ raw magic states in total. In practice protocols are mixed and tuned to avoid overshooting the target, which is why T counts dominate resource estimates."}, {"title": "Adaptive Measurement — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "11-adaptive-measurement.html#exercise-10", "text": "A circuit contains 10⁶ repeat-until-success gadgets, each succeeding with probability p=3/4 per attempt, all running in parallel. (a) What is the expected total number of attempts? (b) For one gadget, how many attempts guarantee success with probability 1−10⁻⁹? (c) Roughly how many attempts does the slowest of the 10⁶ gadgets typically need, and why does this matter for scheduling? (a) Each gadget needs 1/p=4/3 attempts on average, so about 1.33×10⁶ attempts in total, which is the quantity that sets the expected T count. (b) 4⁻ᵏ≤10⁻⁹ needs k≥9log₄10≈15 attempts. (c) The slowest gadget exceeds k attempts with probability about 10⁶·4⁻ᵏ, which is of order one at k≈log₄10⁶≈10. So the slowest gadget typically needs about 10 attempts, although the average is 1.33. If later gates must wait for all gadgets, latency is set by this maximum, not the mean, and idle qubits decohere while they wait. Resource estimates for adaptive circuits should therefore report latency or depth separately from expected gate counts."}, {"title": "1-norm", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#1-norm", "text": "For a Hamiltonian written as Σ h_j P_j, the sum λ = Σ |h_j|, which is the LCU subnormalization and controls simulation cost."}, {"title": "Adiabatic evolution", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#adiabatic-evolution", "text": "Evolution under a slowly varying Hamiltonian H(s), which keeps a system near the instantaneous ground state when the change is slow compared with the gap."}, {"title": "Adiabatic optimization", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#adiabatic-optimization", "text": "Encoding an optimization problem’s cost in a final Hamiltonian and evolving adiabatically from an easy initial Hamiltonian."}, {"title": "Adiabatic theorem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#adiabatic-theorem", "text": "A result giving conditions, in terms of the gap and the rate of change of H(s), under which evolution follows an instantaneous eigenstate."}, {"title": "Amplitude amplification", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#amplitude-amplification", "text": "Repeated application of Q = R_ψ S_G, which rotates a prepared state by 2θ toward the good subspace, raising success from sin²θ to sin²((2k+1)θ)."}, {"title": "Amplitude estimation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#amplitude-estimation", "text": "Phase estimation applied to the Grover iterate, estimating p to precision ε with O(1/ε) calls to A and A†."}, {"title": "Ancilla", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#ancilla", "text": "An auxiliary qubit, usually initialized to |0⟩, used as scratch space by a subroutine."}, {"title": "Ansatz", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#ansatz", "text": "The chosen structure of a parameterized circuit, encoding an assumption about where good answers lie."}, {"title": "Approximate QFT", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#approximate-qft", "text": "The Fourier transform circuit with small-angle rotations omitted. Keeping rotations down to 2π/2^b gives error below 2πn/2^b with O(nb) gates."}, {"title": "Approximation error", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#approximation-error", "text": "The operator-norm distance between an implemented operation or block and the intended one."}, {"title": "Balanced function", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#balanced-function", "text": "A Boolean function that takes the value 1 on exactly half of its inputs."}, {"title": "Ballistic spreading", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#ballistic-spreading", "text": "Growth of a walk’s typical distance linearly in time, as in quantum walks on a line."}, {"title": "Barren plateau", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#barren-plateau", "text": "A parameter landscape whose gradients are exponentially small in the number of qubits, making training require exponentially many shots."}, {"title": "Benchmarking", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#benchmarking", "text": "Comparing an algorithm’s performance with strong alternatives across instance sizes, with full resource accounting."}, {"title": "Bernstein–Vazirani algorithm", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#bernstein-vazirani-algorithm", "text": "A one-query algorithm that recovers a hidden string s from an oracle for f(x) = s·x by applying Hadamards, a phase query, and Hadamards. Classical algorithms need n queries."}, {"title": "Binary fraction", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#binary-fraction", "text": "A number 0.b₁b₂…b_m = Σ b_j 2^{−j}. A phase e^{2πi·0.b₁b₂…} splits into one factor per bit."}, {"title": "Bit oracle", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#bit-oracle", "text": "The oracle U_f|x⟩|y⟩ = |x⟩|y ⊕ f(x)⟩, which adds the function value into a target register."}, {"title": "Bitwise inner product", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#bitwise-inner-product", "text": "x·z = x₁z₁ ⊕ … ⊕ xₙzₙ, the parity of the positions where two bit strings both have a 1."}, {"title": "Block encoding", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#block-encoding", "text": "A unitary U whose top-left block, with ancillas in |0⟩, equals A/α for a matrix A and subnormalization α ≥ ‖A‖."}, {"title": "Chebyshev polynomial", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#chebyshev-polynomial", "text": "T_d(x) = cos(d arccos x), a degree-d polynomial bounded by 1 on [−1, 1]; the top-left entry of W(x)^d."}, {"title": "Chebyshev’s inequality", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#chebyshev-s-inequality", "text": "The bound Pr[|X − μ| > ε] ≤ Var(X)/ε². For an average of R independent bits it gives error probability at most 1/(4Rε²), so precision ε costs on the order of 1/ε² repetitions."}, {"title": "Classical baseline", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#classical-baseline", "text": "The best known classical method for the same task, input model, and success criterion, against which a quantum method is compared."}, {"title": "Classical post-processing", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#classical-post-processing", "text": "The classical computation that turns measurement samples into the algorithm’s answer, such as solving equations or applying continued fractions."}, {"title": "Classical record", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#classical-record", "text": "The bit string of measurement outcomes, stored classically and available to later operations."}, {"title": "Clean oracle", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#clean-oracle", "text": "A coherent evaluation |x⟩|y⟩|0^w⟩ ↦ |x⟩|y ⊕ f(x)⟩|0^w⟩ that preserves its input, adds the answer into a target, and returns its workspace to zero on every basis input with no input-dependent phase."}, {"title": "Cluster state", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#cluster-state", "text": "An entangled resource state made by preparing qubits in |+⟩ and applying controlled-Z between neighbours on a graph."}, {"title": "Coherent access", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#coherent-access", "text": "Access in which one use of the input acts linearly on a superposition of questions, as a unitary operation. It is an assumption of the problem, not automatic."}, {"title": "Coherent sum", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#coherent-sum", "text": "A superposition-based combination of operations that adds amplitudes, as opposed to a random choice that adds probabilities."}, {"title": "Coin register", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#coin-register", "text": "The register in a coined walk that labels the direction of the next move, making the shift reversible."}, {"title": "Coined quantum walk", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#coined-quantum-walk", "text": "A discrete quantum walk on states |c, x⟩ that alternates a coin unitary on c with a shift of x conditioned on c."}, {"title": "Commutator", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#commutator", "text": "[A, B] = AB − BA. It vanishes when A and B commute and controls the error of product formulas."}, {"title": "Compute–copy–uncompute", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#compute-copy-uncompute", "text": "Bennett’s pattern for clean reversible evaluation: compute the answer with garbage, copy the answer into a target, then run the computation backwards to clear the garbage."}, {"title": "Condition number", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#condition-number", "text": "κ = σ_max/σ_min, the ratio of the largest to the smallest singular value of a matrix."}, {"title": "Continued fraction", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#continued-fraction", "text": "A representation x = a₀ + 1/(a₁ + 1/(a₂ + …)) computed by Euclid’s algorithm. Its truncations are the convergents."}, {"title": "Continuous-time quantum walk", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#continuous-time-quantum-walk", "text": "Evolution e^{−iHt} with H the (weighted) adjacency matrix of a graph."}, {"title": "Controlled access", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#controlled-access", "text": "The ability to apply a unitary conditioned on a control qubit. The global phase of the unitary then becomes physically significant."}, {"title": "Controlled phase gate", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#controlled-phase-gate", "text": "The two-qubit gate that multiplies |11⟩ by e^{iθ} and leaves other basis states unchanged. It is symmetric in its two qubits."}, {"title": "Controlled powers", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#controlled-powers", "text": "The operations controlled-U^{2^j} used by phase estimation. By repetition they cost 2^m − 1 uses of controlled-U for m control qubits."}, {"title": "Controlled version", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#controlled-version", "text": "The operation |0⟩⟨0| ⊗ I + |1⟩⟨1| ⊗ U, which applies U only when a control qubit is 1. It depends on the global phase chosen for U."}, {"title": "Convergent", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#convergent", "text": "A fraction obtained by truncating a continued fraction. Any fraction p/q with |x − p/q| < 1/(2q²) is a convergent of x."}, {"title": "Cost function", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#cost-function", "text": "The quantity a variational algorithm minimizes, usually an expectation value estimated by measurement."}, {"title": "Data loading", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#data-loading", "text": "The process of turning classical data into a quantum state or a coherently queryable oracle. Its cost must be included in an end-to-end comparison."}, {"title": "Decision problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#decision-problem", "text": "A problem whose required output for each input is a single bit: yes or no."}, {"title": "Deferred measurement principle", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#deferred-measurement-principle", "text": "A measurement followed by classically controlled operations gives the same output statistics as coherent control followed by measurement at the end."}, {"title": "Dequantization", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#dequantization", "text": "Constructing classical algorithms that match a proposed quantum speedup when given comparable sampling access to the input."}, {"title": "Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#deutsch-jozsa-algorithm", "text": "A one-query quantum algorithm that decides whether an n-bit Boolean function promised to be constant or balanced is constant, by measuring the zero amplitude after Hadamards, a phase query, and Hadamards."}, {"title": "Deutsch’s problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#deutsch-s-problem", "text": "Given coherent access to a hidden function f from one bit to one bit, decide whether f(0) equals f(1). One quantum query suffices; two classical queries are necessary for an exact answer."}, {"title": "Diffusive spreading", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#diffusive-spreading", "text": "Growth of a walk’s typical distance like √t, characteristic of classical random walks."}, {"title": "Dirty oracle", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#dirty-oracle", "text": "An oracle implementation that leaves input-dependent garbage in its workspace. It fails interference experiments that a clean oracle passes."}, {"title": "Eigenbasis expansion", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#eigenbasis-expansion", "text": "Writing a state as a combination Σ c_s|u_s⟩ of eigenstates of an operator. Phase estimation then returns eigenphase φ_s with probability |c_s|²."}, {"title": "Eigenstate", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#eigenstate", "text": "A state that an operation changes only by a factor: V|u⟩ = λ|u⟩. For a unitary V the eigenvalue λ has magnitude one."}, {"title": "Eigenvalue", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#eigenvalue", "text": "The factor λ in V|u⟩ = λ|u⟩. For a unitary operation it is a phase e^{iφ}."}, {"title": "Eigenvalue transformation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#eigenvalue-transformation", "text": "A circuit that block-encodes f(H/α) = Σ f(λ_j/α)|v_j⟩⟨v_j| given a block encoding of H/α."}, {"title": "Element distinctness", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#element-distinctness", "text": "Deciding whether a list of N items contains two equal entries; quantum walks solve it with O(N^{2/3}) queries, which is optimal."}, {"title": "Estimation problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#estimation-problem", "text": "A problem whose required output is a number within a stated tolerance of a target quantity, with a stated failure probability."}, {"title": "Exact amplitude amplification", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#exact-amplitude-amplification", "text": "A modification of amplification, possible when p is known exactly, that reaches success probability exactly 1."}, {"title": "Expectation value", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#expectation-value", "text": "The average ⟨ψ|O|ψ⟩ of an observable’s measured values over many repetitions."}, {"title": "Exponential search", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#exponential-search", "text": "Amplification with a randomly chosen iteration count whose range grows geometrically, finding a marked item with O(1/√p) expected calls when p is unknown."}, {"title": "Expressiveness", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#expressiveness", "text": "The set of states a parameterized circuit can reach. Greater expressiveness costs more gates and parameters."}, {"title": "Factoring reduction", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#factoring-reduction", "text": "If r, the order of a mod L, is even and a^{r/2} ≢ −1, then gcd(a^{r/2} ± 1, L) are nontrivial factors of L."}, {"title": "Fair comparison", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#fair-comparison", "text": "A comparison of methods at the same problem instance, error in the same norm, gate set, and access model, counting all overheads such as ancillas and rotation synthesis."}, {"title": "Feedforward", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#feedforward", "text": "Using a mid-circuit measurement outcome to choose a later gate or measurement basis."}, {"title": "Fixed-point amplification", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#fixed-point-amplification", "text": "Amplification using modified phases so that success probability never decreases, whatever the unknown p."}, {"title": "Fourier mode", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#fourier-mode", "text": "A Fourier basis state on a translation-invariant graph such as a cycle; each is an eigenvector of the adjacency matrix."}, {"title": "Fourier state", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#fourier-state", "text": "A state such as M^{−1/2} Σ_y e^{−2πiy/M}|y⟩, which is an eigenstate of adding a constant modulo M."}, {"title": "Frequency", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#frequency", "text": "An outcome of the Fourier transform. A pattern of period r in a register of size N produces frequencies at multiples of N/r."}, {"title": "Garbage", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#garbage", "text": "Input-dependent intermediate values left in the workspace by a reversible computation. In a quantum algorithm garbage acts as a which-path record."}, {"title": "Gate teleportation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#gate-teleportation", "text": "Applying a gate by teleporting a state through an entangled resource and measuring in a chosen basis, followed by an outcome-dependent correction."}, {"title": "Global phase", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#global-phase", "text": "A common factor e^{iθ} multiplying an entire state or operation. It has no observable effect unless the operation is controlled."}, {"title": "Glued trees", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#glued-trees", "text": "Two binary trees joined at their leaves by a random cycle, on which a continuous quantum walk traverses exponentially faster than any classical algorithm."}, {"title": "Good subspace", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#good-subspace", "text": "The span of basis states marked as successful by a predicate. Amplification increases the weight of a prepared state in this subspace."}, {"title": "Gradient descent", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#gradient-descent", "text": "An optimization method that repeatedly moves parameters opposite to the gradient of the cost function."}, {"title": "Ground state", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#ground-state", "text": "The eigenstate of a Hamiltonian with the lowest eigenvalue (energy)."}, {"title": "Grover iterate", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#grover-iterate", "text": "The operator Q = R_ψ S_G, the product of a reflection about the marked subspace’s complement and a reflection about the prepared state."}, {"title": "Hadamard test", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#hadamard-test", "text": "The circuit H, controlled-U, H, measure on a control qubit. Outcome 0 has probability (1 + Re⟨ψ|U|ψ⟩)/2."}, {"title": "Hadamard transform", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#hadamard-transform", "text": "The operation H^{⊗n}, which maps |x⟩ to 2^{−n/2} Σ_z (−1)^{x·z}|z⟩. It is its own inverse."}, {"title": "Hamiltonian", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#hamiltonian", "text": "A Hermitian operator H that generates evolution e^{−iHt}; its eigenvalues are energies."}, {"title": "Hamiltonian simulation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#hamiltonian-simulation", "text": "Constructing a circuit V with ‖V − e^{−iHt}‖ ≤ ε for a given Hamiltonian H, time t, and error ε."}, {"title": "Hardware noise", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#hardware-noise", "text": "Errors from decoherence and imperfect gates, which bias measured expectation values."}, {"title": "Heisenberg limit", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#heisenberg-limit", "text": "Estimation error of order 1/N with N total uses of the evolution, achievable with coherent powers."}, {"title": "Heuristic", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#heuristic", "text": "A procedure that may work well in practice without a proof of its performance."}, {"title": "Hidden string", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#hidden-string", "text": "An unknown bit string that determines an oracle’s behavior and that an algorithm must recover."}, {"title": "Hidden subgroup problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#hidden-subgroup-problem", "text": "Given a function on a group that is constant exactly on the cosets of an unknown subgroup, find the subgroup. Efficiently solvable by Fourier sampling for abelian groups."}, {"title": "Hitting time", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#hitting-time", "text": "The expected number of steps for a walk to first reach a marked set."}, {"title": "Input family", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#input-family", "text": "The set of all problem instances an algorithm must handle, usually indexed by a size parameter. Scaling claims are statements about the whole family."}, {"title": "Inverse access", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#inverse-access", "text": "The ability to apply the inverse of a given unitary. For a sealed box this is an additional assumption; for a known gate sequence it follows by reversing the gates."}, {"title": "Inverse quantum Fourier transform", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#inverse-quantum-fourier-transform", "text": "F_N†, which maps the phase ramp N^{−1/2} Σ_k e^{2πijk/N}|k⟩ back to |j⟩."}, {"title": "Iterate eigenvalues", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#iterate-eigenvalues", "text": "The eigenvalues e^{±2iθ} of the Grover iterate on the good–bad plane, which encode the success probability p = sin²θ."}, {"title": "Iterative phase estimation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#iterative-phase-estimation", "text": "Phase estimation with one control qubit, reading phase bits from last to first and using earlier outcomes to correct later stages."}, {"title": "Jacobi–Anger expansion", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#jacobi-anger-expansion", "text": "The Chebyshev expansion of cos(τx) and sin(τx) with Bessel-function coefficients, which decay rapidly beyond degree τ."}, {"title": "Johnson graph", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#johnson-graph", "text": "The graph whose vertices are r-subsets of an N-element set, adjacent when they differ in one element."}, {"title": "Linear algebra over GF(2)", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#linear-algebra-over-gf-2", "text": "Solving linear equations in which variables are bits and addition is XOR. Gaussian elimination works as usual."}, {"title": "Linear combination of unitaries", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#linear-combination-of-unitaries", "text": "A block encoding of Σ a_j U_j built from PREPARE, SELECT, and PREPARE†, with subnormalization Σ |a_j|."}, {"title": "Magic state", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#magic-state", "text": "A resource state such as T|+⟩ that, consumed by Clifford operations and measurement, implements a non-Clifford gate."}, {"title": "Magic-state distillation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#magic-state-distillation", "text": "A Clifford-only protocol that converts many noisy magic states into fewer, higher-fidelity ones."}, {"title": "Majority vote", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#majority-vote", "text": "Combining independent runs of a decision algorithm by outputting the most common answer. It amplifies any fixed advantage over 1/2 exponentially in the number of runs."}, {"title": "Markov chain", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#markov-chain", "text": "A random process whose next state depends only on the current state, described by a transition matrix."}, {"title": "Measurement basis change", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#measurement-basis-change", "text": "Single-qubit rotations applied before measurement so that a Pauli X or Y is measured as Z."}, {"title": "Measurement-based quantum computation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#measurement-based-quantum-computation", "text": "A model in which an entangled resource state is prepared in advance and the computation proceeds by adaptive single-qubit measurements."}, {"title": "Mid-circuit measurement", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#mid-circuit-measurement", "text": "A measurement performed before the end of a computation, whose outcome can control later operations."}, {"title": "Minimum finding", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#minimum-finding", "text": "The Dürr–Høyer algorithm, which finds the minimum of N values with O(√N) queries by repeated threshold searches."}, {"title": "Minimum gap", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#minimum-gap", "text": "The smallest separation between the ground and first excited energies along an adiabatic path; runtime scales roughly as its inverse square."}, {"title": "Mixer", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#mixer", "text": "The non-commuting layer e^{−iβΣX_j} in QAOA that turns cost-dependent phases into changes of probability."}, {"title": "Model error", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#model-error", "text": "The difference between a physical system and the Hamiltonian used to describe it, independent of the simulation algorithm."}, {"title": "Modular addition", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#modular-addition", "text": "Addition that wraps around at a fixed modulus, such as (a + b) mod 2ⁿ. The wrap-around keeps the map reversible on a fixed number of bits."}, {"title": "Modular exponentiation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#modular-exponentiation", "text": "The operation |k⟩|y⟩ ↦ |k⟩|a^k y mod L⟩, implemented by controlled multiplications by precomputed constants a^{2^j} mod L."}, {"title": "Monte Carlo estimation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#monte-carlo-estimation", "text": "Estimating an expected value by averaging random samples; precision ε costs order 1/ε² samples classically."}, {"title": "No fast-forwarding", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#no-fast-forwarding", "text": "The theorem that general Hamiltonians cannot be simulated for time t with a number of operations sublinear in t."}, {"title": "Non-unitary matrix", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#non-unitary-matrix", "text": "A matrix that changes the length of some vectors. It cannot be applied deterministically by a quantum circuit, only as a block of a larger unitary."}, {"title": "Oblivious amplitude amplification", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#oblivious-amplitude-amplification", "text": "Amplification of a block encoding of a scaled unitary using reflections on the ancillas only, so that it works for unknown input states."}, {"title": "Observable estimation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#observable-estimation", "text": "Estimating ⟨ψ|O|ψ⟩ from repeated measurements; operator-norm evolution error ε changes it by at most 2‖O‖ε."}, {"title": "One-bit teleportation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#one-bit-teleportation", "text": "The two-qubit circuit (controlled-Z, then measurement at angle α) that moves a state to an auxiliary qubit while applying HP(−α) up to a Pauli X."}, {"title": "Operator norm", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#operator-norm", "text": "The largest factor by which an operator stretches a normalized vector: ‖A‖ = max ‖Aψ‖ over unit ψ. It bounds the worst-case effect of an error operator."}, {"title": "Optimal simulation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#optimal-simulation", "text": "Hamiltonian simulation using O(αt + log(1/ε)) block-encoding calls, matching lower bounds."}, {"title": "Oracle", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#oracle", "text": "An operation that gives access to a hidden function or input, specified by its action on basis states, such as U_f|x⟩|y⟩ = |x⟩|y ⊕ f(x)⟩. Its internal construction is not part of the query count."}, {"title": "Order", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#order", "text": "The smallest r > 0 with a^r ≡ 1 (mod L), defined when gcd(a, L) = 1."}, {"title": "Order finding", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#order-finding", "text": "Finding the order r of a modulo L. Phase estimation of modular multiplication on the state |1⟩ samples s/r for random s."}, {"title": "Orthogonal states", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#orthogonal-states", "text": "States with zero inner product. They can be distinguished with certainty by measuring in a basis that contains them."}, {"title": "Overshoot", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#overshoot", "text": "The decrease in success probability when amplification continues past the optimal number of iterations."}, {"title": "Parameter-shift rule", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#parameter-shift-rule", "text": "dE/dθ = [E(θ + π/2) − E(θ − π/2)]/2 for a parameter entering through a single Pauli rotation."}, {"title": "Parameterized circuit", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#parameterized-circuit", "text": "A fixed gate sequence with adjustable rotation angles, producing a family of trial states."}, {"title": "Parity circuit", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#parity-circuit", "text": "A CNOT ladder that computes the parity of several qubits into one, used to apply a phase depending on that parity and then uncomputed."}, {"title": "Parity constraint", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#parity-constraint", "text": "With d signal calls, the implemented polynomial has the parity of d."}, {"title": "Pauli correction", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#pauli-correction", "text": "A Pauli gate applied, depending on a measurement outcome, to remove a known byproduct and make the result deterministic."}, {"title": "Pauli exponential", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#pauli-exponential", "text": "The unitary e^{−iθP} for a Pauli string P, implemented by basis changes, a CNOT parity ladder, and one Z rotation."}, {"title": "Pauli frame", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#pauli-frame", "text": "A classical record of Pauli byproducts that have not been physically corrected, used to reinterpret later measurement outcomes."}, {"title": "Pauli string", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#pauli-string", "text": "A tensor product of single-qubit operators I, X, Y, Z. Pauli strings form a basis for Hermitian matrices on n qubits."}, {"title": "Pebbling", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#pebbling", "text": "A game modelling which intermediate values a reversible computation stores: a stage may be computed or uncomputed only while its predecessor is stored."}, {"title": "Period", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#period", "text": "The smallest shift r under which a pattern or function repeats: f(x + r) = f(x)."}, {"title": "Phase error", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#phase-error", "text": "The difference between an intended and an implemented phase. An error δ in every phase changes any normalized state by at most δ."}, {"title": "Phase estimation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#phase-estimation", "text": "The algorithm that estimates an eigenphase φ of U by applying controlled powers U^{2^j} to an eigenstate and an inverse Fourier transform to the controls."}, {"title": "Phase factors", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#phase-factors", "text": "The rotation angles φ₀, …, φ_d in a quantum signal processing sequence, which determine the implemented polynomial."}, {"title": "Phase gap", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#phase-gap", "text": "The smallest nonzero eigenphase of a quantum walk operator; for Szegedy walks it is at least 2√δ."}, {"title": "Phase gate", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#phase-gate", "text": "The single-qubit gate P(θ) = diag(1, e^{iθ}). Special cases are Z = P(π), S = P(π/2), and T = P(π/4)."}, {"title": "Phase kickback", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#phase-kickback", "text": "The effect by which an operation acting on a target in an eigenstate multiplies the controlling branch by the eigenvalue, moving information from the target into a relative phase."}, {"title": "Phase oracle", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#phase-oracle", "text": "The operation |x⟩ ↦ (−1)^{f(x)}|x⟩, which records a Boolean function in signs. One query to U_f with the target in |−⟩ implements it."}, {"title": "Phase resolution", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#phase-resolution", "text": "The grid spacing 1/2^m of phase estimation with m control qubits. The nearest grid point is obtained with probability at least 4/π²."}, {"title": "Phase-gradient trick", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#phase-gradient-trick", "text": "Producing the phase e^{2πiv/M} by adding v modulo M into a reusable Fourier state, which kicks the phase back onto the controlling register."}, {"title": "Polynomial approximation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#polynomial-approximation", "text": "Finding a low-degree bounded polynomial close to a target function on a region; its degree is the query cost of the transformation."}, {"title": "Postselection", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#postselection", "text": "Keeping a run only when a measurement gives a chosen outcome. Its cost is the inverse success probability, or its square root with amplification."}, {"title": "Preparation circuit", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#preparation-circuit", "text": "A known unitary A with A|0ⁿ⟩ = |ψ⟩. Unlike a supplied copy of |ψ⟩, it can be inverted and controlled."}, {"title": "PREPARE", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#prepare", "text": "The circuit that loads coefficient weights √(a_j/α) into an ancilla register in the LCU construction."}, {"title": "Product formula", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#product-formula", "text": "An approximation of e^{−i(A+B)t} by products of e^{−iAδ} and e^{−iBδ} over short steps δ; also called Trotterization."}, {"title": "Product of block encodings", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#product-of-block-encodings", "text": "Sequential application of two block encodings with separate ancillas, encoding AB/(αβ)."}, {"title": "Product representation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#product-representation", "text": "The factorization of F_N|x⟩ into single-qubit states, one per output bit, each with a phase set by a binary fraction of x."}, {"title": "Promise", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#promise", "text": "A restriction on the allowed inputs that an algorithm may assume without checking. On inputs that violate the promise, the problem places no requirement on the output."}, {"title": "Promise problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#promise-problem", "text": "A problem whose allowed inputs are restricted by a promise; behavior on other inputs is unconstrained."}, {"title": "QAOA", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#qaoa", "text": "The quantum approximate optimization algorithm, alternating cost-phase layers e^{−iγC} with mixer layers e^{−iβB} and optimizing the angles."}, {"title": "QRAM", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#qram", "text": "Quantum random-access memory: a device or circuit that queries classical data at a superposition of addresses. Its size grows with the data stored."}, {"title": "Quadratic speedup", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quadratic-speedup", "text": "A reduction of a cost from order T to order √T, as in search and amplitude estimation."}, {"title": "Quantum Fourier transform", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-fourier-transform", "text": "The unitary F_N|x⟩ = N^{−1/2} Σ_y e^{2πixy/N}|y⟩. For N = 2ⁿ it has a circuit of O(n²) gates."}, {"title": "Quantum linear systems", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-linear-systems", "text": "Preparing a state proportional to A⁻¹|b⟩ from a block encoding of A and a preparation of |b⟩."}, {"title": "Quantum signal processing", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-signal-processing", "text": "Alternating signal calls W(x) with Z-rotations to implement a chosen polynomial P(x) as a matrix entry."}, {"title": "Quantum singular value transformation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-singular-value-transformation", "text": "The framework that applies a polynomial to the singular values of a block-encoded matrix by alternating the encoding, its inverse, and projector-controlled phases."}, {"title": "Qubitization", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#qubitization", "text": "Turning a Hermitian block encoding of H/α into a walk W whose eigenphases are ±arccos(λ_j/α), one two-dimensional plane per eigenvalue."}, {"title": "Query", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#query", "text": "One application of an oracle, whatever state it acts on, including a superposition of inputs."}, {"title": "Query complexity", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#query-complexity", "text": "The number of oracle queries an algorithm uses, ignoring all other operations. Lower bounds in this model hold for every algorithm with the stated access."}, {"title": "Query lower bound", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#query-lower-bound", "text": "A proof that every algorithm in a given access model needs at least a certain number of queries, such as Ω(√N) for unstructured search."}, {"title": "Random walk", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#random-walk", "text": "A process that moves from a vertex to a random neighbour at each step, described by a transition matrix P."}, {"title": "Reflection", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#reflection", "text": "An operation that fixes a chosen state or subspace and negates every orthogonal direction, such as 2|ψ⟩⟨ψ| − I."}, {"title": "Repeat-until-success", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#repeat-until-success", "text": "A circuit that applies the desired operation on one outcome and an undoable operation on another, repeated until success; exact, with a random number of attempts."}, {"title": "Resource accounting", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#resource-accounting", "text": "Listing queries, gates, depth, qubits, precision, repetitions, postselection, and classical processing separately, so that no cost is hidden."}, {"title": "Resource estimate", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#resource-estimate", "text": "A count of qubits, gates, depth, and time for a concrete instance, under stated assumptions about the gate set and error correction."}, {"title": "Reversible arithmetic", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#reversible-arithmetic", "text": "Arithmetic implemented as a permutation of basis states, such as modular addition, so that it can act on superpositions."}, {"title": "Robust phase estimation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#robust-phase-estimation", "text": "An estimation schedule of powers and repetitions designed to bound the error even when individual outcomes are noisy."}, {"title": "Root of unity", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#root-of-unity", "text": "A complex number ω with ω^N = 1. The N-th roots e^{2πik/N} are equally spaced on the unit circle, and their k-th powers sum to zero unless N divides k."}, {"title": "Sampling problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#sampling-problem", "text": "A problem whose required output is a random draw whose distribution is close to a specified target distribution."}, {"title": "Search problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#search-problem", "text": "A problem whose required output is a witness satisfying a condition, or a report that none exists."}, {"title": "SELECT", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#select", "text": "The multiplexed operation Σ_j |j⟩⟨j| ⊗ U_j that applies the j-th unitary on the branch where the ancilla is |j⟩."}, {"title": "Semiclassical Fourier transform", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#semiclassical-fourier-transform", "text": "The inverse QFT with each qubit measured as soon as it is final and the remaining controlled rotations replaced by classically controlled single-qubit rotations."}, {"title": "Shift operator", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#shift-operator", "text": "The permutation |c, x⟩ ↦ |c, x ± 1⟩ (or along edge c) in a coined walk."}, {"title": "Shor’s algorithm", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#shor-s-algorithm", "text": "The polynomial-time quantum algorithm for factoring (and discrete logarithms) that reduces factoring to order finding and solves order finding by phase estimation."}, {"title": "Shot allocation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#shot-allocation", "text": "Dividing measurements among Hamiltonian terms, optimally in proportion to |h_j|σ_j, giving variance at most λ²/R."}, {"title": "Shot noise", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#shot-noise", "text": "The statistical uncertainty in an expectation value estimated from a finite number of measurements, falling as 1/√R."}, {"title": "Signal operator", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#signal-operator", "text": "The one-qubit unitary W(x) = e^{iθX} with x = cos θ, whose top-left entry is the signal x."}, {"title": "Simon’s problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#simon-s-problem", "text": "Given a two-to-one function with f(x) = f(y) exactly when y = x ⊕ s, find s. Quantum algorithms need O(n) queries; bounded-error classical algorithms need Ω(2^{n/2})."}, {"title": "Singular value", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#singular-value", "text": "A stretch factor σ_j in A|w_j⟩ = σ_j|u_j⟩, with orthonormal input and output directions. The largest equals ‖A‖."}, {"title": "Sparse access", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#sparse-access", "text": "Oracles that return the positions and values of the nonzero entries in each row of a sparse matrix."}, {"title": "Spectral decomposition", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#spectral-decomposition", "text": "Writing a Hermitian H as Σ λ_j |v_j⟩⟨v_j| with real eigenvalues and orthonormal eigenvectors, so that functions of H act eigenvalue by eigenvalue."}, {"title": "Spectral filter", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#spectral-filter", "text": "A bounded polynomial approximating a step function on the spectrum, used to project onto eigenvalues in a chosen range."}, {"title": "Spectral gap", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#spectral-gap", "text": "δ = 1 − λ₂ for a Markov chain, which controls classical mixing and hitting times of order 1/δ."}, {"title": "Standard quantum limit", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#standard-quantum-limit", "text": "Estimation error of order 1/√N from N independent repetitions, the same scaling as classical sampling."}, {"title": "State injection", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#state-injection", "text": "Applying a non-Clifford gate by consuming a magic state with Clifford operations, measurement, and a conditional correction."}, {"title": "Stochastic optimization", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#stochastic-optimization", "text": "Optimization using noisy estimates of the objective or gradient."}, {"title": "Subnormalization", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#subnormalization", "text": "The factor α in a block encoding of A/α. It is at least ‖A‖, and success probabilities scale as 1/α²."}, {"title": "Success criterion", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#success-criterion", "text": "The condition a run must satisfy to count as correct, such as exact correctness on every input or correctness with probability at least 2/3."}, {"title": "Success probability", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#success-probability", "text": "The probability p = sin²θ that measuring the prepared state gives a marked outcome."}, {"title": "Suzuki formulas", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#suzuki-formulas", "text": "Recursively constructed product formulas of any even order 2k, with error O(t^{2k+1}/r^{2k})."}, {"title": "Symmetric Trotter formula", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#symmetric-trotter-formula", "text": "The step e^{−iδA/2}e^{−iδB}e^{−iδA/2}, whose error over r steps is O(t³/r²)."}, {"title": "Szegedy walk", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#szegedy-walk", "text": "The quantum walk W = R_B R_A built from two reflections determined by a Markov chain’s transition probabilities."}, {"title": "T gate", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#t-gate", "text": "The single-qubit gate diag(1, e^{iπ/4}). In many fault-tolerant schemes it is far more expensive than Clifford gates, so T count is a key cost measure."}, {"title": "Threshold search", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#threshold-search", "text": "Amplified search for an item whose value is below a given threshold, used as a step in minimum finding."}, {"title": "Time–space tradeoff", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#time-space-tradeoff", "text": "Reducing the number of stored intermediate values by recomputing them when needed, at the cost of more gates."}, {"title": "Toffoli count", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#toffoli-count", "text": "The number of Toffoli gates in a circuit, a common cost measure for reversible arithmetic in fault-tolerant settings."}, {"title": "Toffoli gate", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#toffoli-gate", "text": "The reversible gate |a⟩|b⟩|t⟩ ↦ |a⟩|b⟩|t ⊕ ab⟩, which computes AND into a target while keeping its inputs."}, {"title": "Trainability", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#trainability", "text": "Whether an ansatz’s optimum can be found with reasonable resources, distinct from whether it contains a good state."}, {"title": "Trotter error", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#trotter-error", "text": "The difference between a product formula and the exact evolution. For first order it is at most t²‖[A,B]‖/(2r)."}, {"title": "Trotter steps", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#trotter-steps", "text": "The number r of repetitions of a product-formula step; more steps reduce the Trotter error."}, {"title": "Uncomputation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#uncomputation", "text": "Running a computation in reverse to restore intermediate registers to their initial state after the result has been used or copied."}, {"title": "Variational principle", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#variational-principle", "text": "For any state, ⟨ψ|H|ψ⟩ ≥ E₀, with equality only for ground states."}, {"title": "Variational quantum eigensolver", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#variational-quantum-eigensolver", "text": "A hybrid algorithm that minimizes the measured energy of a parameterized trial state with a classical optimizer."}, {"title": "Walk search", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#walk-search", "text": "Finding a marked vertex by amplitude amplification in which reflection about the stationary state uses phase estimation of a walk, at cost S + (1/√ε)(U/√δ + C)."}, {"title": "Which-path record", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#which-path-record", "text": "Any part of the state whose value differs between computational branches. Branches with orthogonal records cannot interfere, whether or not the record is measured."}, {"title": "Workspace", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#workspace", "text": "The ancilla qubits a subroutine uses temporarily. A clean subroutine returns them to their initial state."}];
